const TEACH={"T4":{"clue":"Late-peaking, dagger-shaped CW Doppler envelope in a tachycardic, septic patient.","mech":"An underfilled, hyperdynamic LV obliterates its cavity in late systole, so the outflow gradient builds late and the velocity peaks late.","confirm":"Walk the PW sample from the apex to the LVOT to localise the step-up; look for SAM and a small end-systolic cavity.","trap":"Do not call severe MR from peak velocity alone: MR is rounded, earlier-peaking and starts with isovolumic contraction."},"L2_8":{"clue":"Tissue Doppler targets myocardium, which moves slowly but reflects strongly.","mech":"Myocardium is dense and moves at roughly 5-20 cm/s, so its echoes are strong; blood cells are weak scatterers moving fast. TDI filters out high-velocity, low-amplitude blood signal.","confirm":"Place a small PW gate on the septal or lateral mitral annulus (or lateral tricuspid annulus), lower the scale and gain, and read e', a' and s'.","trap":"Blood flow Doppler is the high-velocity, low-amplitude signal; picking low amplitude confuses the two. Remember: tissue is slow and loud, blood is fast and quiet."},"L2_9":{"clue":"Shunt flow across a PFO is low velocity, so the colour scale must be lowered.","mech":"Colour Doppler only displays velocities within the set Nyquist range; at a valve-regurgitation scale of 50-70 cm/s, slow interatrial flow falls below display and appears absent.","confirm":"Lower the Nyquist limit to about 20 cm/s, narrow the colour box to keep frame rate, and confirm with an agitated saline bubble study.","trap":"Keeping the standard 50-70 cm/s scale is correct for regurgitant jets but hides low-velocity shunts; baseline shift alone does not reveal slow flow."},"L2_10":{"clue":"Qp is the pulmonary (RV outflow) stroke volume and Qs the systemic (LVOT) stroke volume; the ratio is pulmonary over systemic.","mech":"Stroke volume at any orifice equals cross-sectional area times VTI (continuity principle). Comparing RVOT/PA flow with LVOT flow quantifies net shunt.","confirm":"Qp:Qs = (0.785 x D_PA squared x VTI_PA) / (0.785 x D_LVOT squared x VTI_LVOT); a ratio above 1.5 suggests a significant left-to-right shunt.","trap":"Option B inverts the ratio; VTI ratios alone ignore the different diameters, and heart rate cancels so adds nothing."},"L2_11":{"clue":"The 2018 ASE statement retained only known hypersensitivity as a contraindication to ultrasound-enhancing agents.","mech":"Reactions are rare anaphylactoid (complement-mediated) events; earlier black-box cautions for ACS, pulmonary hypertension and critical illness were removed after safety data showed no excess harm.","confirm":"Screen for prior reaction to the agent or its components (e.g. PEG), use low MI imaging, and have resuscitation available.","trap":"ACS, ventilation and pulmonary hypertension were old FDA warnings now withdrawn; ICU patients are among those who benefit most from contrast."},"L3_1":{"clue":"The artifact places an echo where no structure lies on the central beam axis.","mech":"Side lobes are off-axis beams of acoustic energy; strong reflectors they hit are displayed as if on the main axis, producing spurious echoes alongside real structures.","confirm":"Image from another window or angle; a true structure persists and has anatomical continuity, whereas a side-lobe echo shifts or vanishes. Harmonics reduce side lobes.","trap":"Reverberation violates the travel-time (single reflection) assumption, not the on-axis assumption; range ambiguity violates the one-pulse-at-a-time assumption."},"L3_2":{"clue":"Equally spaced, depth-fading copies that move exactly with the structure in front.","mech":"Sound bounces repeatedly between two strong reflectors (or reflector and transducer); each round trip adds time, so copies appear at multiples of the reflector depth with decreasing intensity.","confirm":"Check that spacing equals the original reflector depth, change angle or window, and look for independent motion; real thrombus moves on its own and is seen in multiple planes.","trap":"Thrombus is the tempting dangerous diagnosis, but a real mass has independent motion and does not repeat at regular intervals."},"L3_3":{"clue":"A jet in an implausible location with identical timing and Doppler profile to a real paravalvular jet, near a strong reflector (mechanical valve).","mech":"A strong specular reflector redirects the beam; the machine assumes straight-line travel and places a duplicate image of the true jet on the far side of the reflector.","confirm":"Image in an off-axis or orthogonal plane; the mirror image disappears or moves while the true jet remains anchored to its origin.","trap":"VSD, Gerbode and ruptured sinus are real diagnoses but would have their own distinct timing, origin and velocity rather than an exact copy."},"L3_4":{"clue":"The question asks which single setting suppresses side lobes and near-field clutter.","mech":"Harmonic signals are generated within tissue and are strongest along the central high-intensity beam; weak side lobes and near-field reverberations generate little harmonic energy, so they are filtered out.","confirm":"Turn on tissue harmonic imaging, then optimise focus, TGC, depth and frequency; use contrast for persistent apical clutter.","trap":"Higher gain or MI increases artifact; widening the sector lowers frame rate and line density without removing clutter."},"L3_5":{"clue":"Brighter tissue lies behind a fluid-filled structure such as the descending aorta.","mech":"Fluid attenuates sound less than surrounding tissue, so more energy reaches deeper structures, but TGC still compensates as if tissue were present, making distal echoes bright.","confirm":"Recognise the bright band directly behind the fluid; reduce TGC in that zone to normalise brightness.","trap":"Shadowing is the opposite: dark dropout behind strongly attenuating or reflecting structures like calcium or prosthetic material."},"L3_6":{"clue":"Hazy apical echo in the near field that does not move with the wall.","mech":"Near-field clutter arises from reverberation and side lobes close to the transducer; a real thrombus is anchored to akinetic myocardium and moves with it.","confirm":"Move focus to the apex, use harmonics, adjust TGC and depth, then give an ultrasound-enhancing agent: a true thrombus appears as a filling defect.","trap":"Anticoagulating or ordering TEE first skips cheap optimisation; TEE images the apex poorly, and starting anticoagulation on artifact exposes the patient to bleeding."},"L3_7":{"clue":"Structures posterior and near the oesophagus: LAA, mitral valve, interatrial septum, thoracic aorta.","mech":"TEE sits directly behind the left atrium, giving short distance, high frequency and no lung or rib interference for posterior structures.","confirm":"For LAA thrombus, endocarditis, PFO or aortic dissection, choose TEE; for apex, RV and anterior structures, TTE is often better.","trap":"Apex, RV free wall, anterior pericardium and right-sided valves are anterior structures in the TEE far field and are usually better seen on TTE."},"L3_8":{"clue":"Unexpected high-velocity diastolic signal on a CW tracing aimed at TR, in a patient with mitral stenosis.","mech":"CW Doppler has no range resolution; it records every velocity along the whole beam, so an adjacent MS jet can be superimposed on the TR recording.","confirm":"Check timing against the ECG (MS is diastolic, TR systolic) and interrogate from another window; use PW or colour to localise the source.","trap":"Aliasing does not occur with CW, and TR or pulmonary hypertension produce systolic, not diastolic, signals."},"L3_9":{"clue":"Need to boost deep signals selectively without altering the near field.","mech":"Sound attenuates with depth, so late-returning echoes are weaker; TGC applies depth-specific amplification to equalise brightness.","confirm":"Adjust TGC sliders at the relevant depth until the myocardium appears uniformly bright from near to far field.","trap":"Overall gain amplifies all depths equally; dynamic range changes contrast, and sector width changes field of view and frame rate."},"L3_10":{"clue":"Sudden bright irregular streaks during surgery or with an LVAD.","mech":"Electrocautery and electrically active devices emit radiofrequency noise picked up by the transducer and system, displayed as streaks or bands unrelated to anatomy.","confirm":"Note the timing with cautery use or the device; the pattern disappears when cautery stops and does not respect anatomical boundaries.","trap":"Side lobes and mirror images are acoustic artifacts tied to real reflectors; they do not appear suddenly with cautery."},"L4_1":{"clue":"Angling within the same imaging plane toward or away from the indicator, without moving the probe.","mech":"Rocking keeps the plane fixed and shifts the beam sideways within it, useful for centring a structure; tilting sweeps the plane across the body.","confirm":"Practise from PLAX: rocking toward the indicator moves the image to the side of the screen; tilting moves you through base-to-apex slices.","trap":"Tilting (fanning) is the commonest confusion: it changes angle out of plane. Rotation turns around the probe axis; sliding moves the footprint."},"L4_2":{"clue":"Going from PLAX to PSAX at the same window.","mech":"The short axis is orthogonal to the long axis, so a 90 degree clockwise rotation moves the indicator from right shoulder to left shoulder, cutting the LV in cross-section.","confirm":"Rotate clockwise about 90 degrees, then tilt from the aortic valve level down through mitral, papillary and apical levels; round LV confirms on-axis.","trap":"Counter-clockwise rotation produces an off-axis or inverted image; sliding or tilting alone does not change the long axis to short axis."},"L4_3":{"clue":"LVOT VTI must be measured in laminar flow just proximal to the valve.","mech":"Stroke volume equals LVOT area times VTI at the same site; sampling in the acceleration zone or at the leaflets records higher velocities and overestimates SV.","confirm":"Apical 5- or 3-chamber, 3-5 mm gate on the LV side of the valve; look for a narrow smooth envelope with a closing click, and trace the modal velocity.","trap":"Sampling at the leaflet tips or aorta captures flow acceleration; the mid-LV cavity samples low, non-representative velocities."},"L4_4":{"clue":"Imaging the aortic arch and its branches on TTE.","mech":"The suprasternal notch window looks down through the mediastinum along the arch, avoiding lung and sternum.","confirm":"Place the probe in the notch with the neck extended and indicator toward the left jaw; identify the arch and three branch take-offs, and sample descending aorta flow.","trap":"Subcostal and apical views do not reach the arch; right parasternal views show the ascending aorta but not branch origins."},"L4_5":{"clue":"Long-axis display of RVOT, pulmonic valve and main PA.","mech":"From PLAX, tilting anteriorly and superiorly toward the left shoulder sweeps the plane onto the RVOT, which crosses anterior to the aortic root.","confirm":"Obtain the RV outflow view from PLAX or use basal PSAX; confirm the pulmonic valve and PA bifurcation and place PW for RVOT acceleration time.","trap":"Apical 3- and 2-chamber views image the left heart; the subcostal IVC view shows venous inflow, not the outflow tract."},"L4_6":{"clue":"Apical 3-chamber equals an apical PLAX, including the LVOT and aortic valve.","mech":"The plane passes through the LV long axis and the aortic valve, so it shows the anteroseptal and inferolateral walls like PLAX.","confirm":"From A4C rotate counter-clockwise about 120 degrees until the LVOT and aortic valve open out; check walls match PLAX segments.","trap":"A2C shows anterior and inferior walls without the aorta; A4C shows inferoseptum and anterolateral wall plus the right heart."},"L4_7":{"clue":"Pulmonary vein Doppler in A4C requires the vein most aligned with the beam.","mech":"The right upper pulmonary vein enters the LA nearly parallel to the apical ultrasound beam, minimising angle error and allowing a clean S, D and Ar recording.","confirm":"Use colour to find the RUPV inflow near the interatrial septum, place a 2-3 mm gate 1-2 cm into the vein and record S, D and Ar.","trap":"Left-sided veins enter at a steeper angle in A4C; the ostium contains LA flow contamination; the IVC is systemic venous flow."},"L4_8":{"clue":"Reflector moving toward the transducer.","mech":"Motion toward the source compresses wavefronts, shortening wavelength and raising received frequency (positive Doppler shift), displayed above the baseline.","confirm":"Doppler shift = 2 x f0 x v x cos(theta) / c; flow toward the transducer is positive and red on standard colour maps.","trap":"Higher frequency with longer wavelength is physically impossible at constant speed of sound; lower frequency and longer wavelength describe motion away."},"L5_1":{"clue":"Mechanically ventilated ICU patients frequently have poor transthoracic windows.","mech":"Hyperinflation, positive pressure, obesity, oedema, dressings, drains and supine positioning interpose air or tissue between probe and heart, degrading TTE in roughly half.","confirm":"When TTE is non-diagnostic for the clinical question, proceed to TEE, which adds new diagnoses in a substantial proportion of ICU patients.","trap":"Assuming most ventilated patients image well (10-25%) underestimates the problem; 90% overstates it, as many studies remain diagnostic."},"L5_2":{"clue":"TR velocity and LV apex are the stated items; TR needs beam alignment and the apex is far field on TEE.","mech":"TEE views the heart from behind, placing the apex far from the probe and often foreshortened; TR jets are commonly poorly aligned with mid-oesophageal beams.","confirm":"Use TTE apical, parasternal RV inflow and subcostal views to align CW with TR and to assess the apex with contrast if needed.","trap":"TEE is superior for dissection, LAA thrombus and localised post-cardiotomy tamponade, which are the distractors."},"L5_3":{"clue":"Upper GI perforation is the rare, serious TEE complication; overall complications are more frequent.","mech":"Perforation results from mechanical trauma, mainly at the hypopharynx and upper oesophagus during insertion, and is rarer than minor injury or bleeding.","confirm":"Before TEE, screen for oesophageal pathology, varices and dysphagia; insert gently with jaw thrust and never force against resistance.","trap":"1 in 500 is the overall complication rate, not perforation; 1 in 50 overstates risk and 1 in 500,000 dismisses it."},"L5_4":{"clue":"Anteflexion and retroflexion of the probe tip.","mech":"The large wheel controls the anterior-posterior flexion cables; the small wheel controls left-right lateral flexion.","confirm":"Before insertion, check wheel function and locks on your probe; use slight anteflexion around the tongue base and keep wheels unlocked during advancement.","trap":"Rotating the probe turns the plane left or right, and advancing or withdrawing changes level; neither flexes the tip."},"L5_5":{"clue":"In mid-oesophageal views the chamber at the top of the sector is the one adjacent to the oesophagus.","mech":"The left atrium lies directly anterior to the oesophagus, so it is in the near field at the apex of the TEE sector.","confirm":"On ME 4C, confirm LA at the top, then LV, with RA and RV toward the right of the screen; this orientation anchors all ME views.","trap":"The RV and RA are anterior structures, far from the probe; transthoracic orientation habits mislead here."},"L5_6":{"clue":"ME 4-chamber is the TEE analogue of A4C.","mech":"The plane cuts the heart through both atria and ventricles in the same orientation as A4C, displaying the inferoseptum and anterolateral wall.","confirm":"At about 0-20 degrees, retroflex slightly to avoid foreshortening and identify the inferoseptum and anterolateral wall for regional analysis.","trap":"ME LAX matches PLAX/A3C (anteroseptal and inferolateral), and ME 2C matches A2C (anterior and inferior); mixing these mismatches coronary territories."},"L5_7":{"clue":"SVC, IVC, fossa ovalis, Eustachian valve and RA appendage in one image.","mech":"At about 90-110 degrees turned to the right, the plane runs along the superior-inferior axis of the right atrium, aligning both cavae with the interatrial septum.","confirm":"Turn the probe rightward from ME 2C at 90-110 degrees; use this view for bubble studies, ECMO cannula position and SVC collapsibility.","trap":"ME AV short axis shows the septum and RA but not both cavae; transgastric and UE arch views are unrelated."},"L5_8":{"clue":"Transgastric mid-papillary SAX displays all three coronary territories at once.","mech":"The cross-section through the papillary muscles includes segments supplied by LAD, RCA and circumflex, and the LV cavity size reflects filling.","confirm":"Use it to track LV end-diastolic area, global contraction and regional wall motion during haemodynamic interventions.","trap":"Aortic gradients need deep transgastric views; PFO needs bicaval; descending aorta needs probe rotation posteriorly."},"L5_9":{"clue":"TEE probe stays in place without the operator touching the chest.","mech":"Continuous imaging during compressions allows rhythm and motion assessment within brief pauses, and visualising the compressed region guides hand position.","confirm":"Pre-position at ME 4C or ME LAX before the pulse check; record during the check and review while compressions continue.","trap":"TTE requires access to the chest and often prolongs pauses; TEE does not prolong pauses or prevent compression assessment."},"L6_1":{"clue":"ASE recommends a volumetric 2D method for EF.","mech":"Biplane method of disks sums elliptical disks from two orthogonal planes, so it accounts for regional variation and requires fewer geometric assumptions than linear methods.","confirm":"Trace endocardium at end-diastole and end-systole in A4C and A2C, avoid foreshortening, and compute EF = (EDV - ESV)/EDV.","trap":"Teichholz and fractional shortening assume symmetric geometry and fail with regional wall motion abnormalities; EPSS is a surrogate, not a measurement."},"L6_2":{"clue":"LVEDD 55 mm and LVESD 41 mm in PLAX.","mech":"Fractional shortening expresses the percentage reduction of the LV minor-axis diameter during systole, a linear index of radial function.","confirm":"FS = (55 - 41)/55 = 14/55 = 25%; measure at the mitral leaflet tips perpendicular to the long axis.","trap":"14% is the absolute difference in mm mistaken for a percentage; 34% and 41% come from dividing by the wrong diameter."},"L6_3":{"clue":"EF 38% lies in the 30-40% band.","mech":"ASE 2015 categories: normal 52-72% men and 54-74% women, mildly abnormal 41-51/53%, moderately abnormal 30-40%, severely abnormal below 30%.","confirm":"Confirm by biplane disks; if image quality is poor, use contrast to improve endocardial definition before classifying.","trap":"Heart-failure EF categories (HFmrEF 41-49%) differ from echo severity grading; do not label 38% as only mildly reduced."},"L6_4":{"clue":"Increased distance between the anterior mitral leaflet E-point and the septum.","mech":"A dilated, poorly contracting LV with low transmitral flow opens the mitral leaflet less and moves the septum away, widening the gap.","confirm":"M-mode through anterior leaflet tip in PLAX; EPSS above 7 mm suggests EF below 30%. Exclude MS, AR jets and septal wall motion abnormalities.","trap":"Normal EPSS is under about 6 mm; thresholds of 2-4 mm would label normal hearts abnormal, and 15 mm is far too insensitive."},"L6_5":{"clue":"MAPSE threshold in a woman for normal EF.","mech":"Longitudinal fibres shorten the LV toward the apex; mitral annular excursion reflects longitudinal systolic function and correlates with global EF.","confirm":"M-mode through the lateral mitral annulus in A4C: at least 11 mm (women) or 13 mm (men) suggests EF above 55%; below 6 mm suggests severe dysfunction.","trap":"Using the male threshold for a woman, or a TAPSE-like value, misclassifies; MAPSE is angle-dependent and foreshortening underestimates it."},"L6_6":{"clue":"Fractional area change of the LV in PSAX.","mech":"FAC compares end-diastolic and end-systolic cavity areas, approximating radial contraction in one plane.","confirm":"FAC = (EDA - ESA)/EDA x 100 at mid-papillary level; normal about 35-65%.","trap":"Values of 20-30% indicate dysfunction, and 70-90% suggests hyperdynamic or underfilled ventricles rather than normal."},"L6_7":{"clue":"Apex not fully included in apical views.","mech":"A foreshortened plane cuts the LV obliquely short of the true apex, shortening the long axis and reducing traced volumes.","confirm":"Look for an elongated, tapered apex; move the probe laterally and inferiorly to find the true apex, and compare long-axis length between A4C and A2C.","trap":"Endocardial dropout causes overestimation, the opposite; foreshortening also often makes the apex look hypercontractile and overestimates EF."},"L6_8":{"clue":"Wall thickness measurement location and timing.","mech":"At end-diastole the LV is at maximal volume and walls are thinnest; standardising timing and level makes thickness reproducible and comparable with normal ranges.","confirm":"Measure in PLAX perpendicular to the LV long axis at the mitral leaflet tips, at the onset of QRS or largest LV dimension, excluding RV trabeculae.","trap":"End-systolic measurements are thicker and not comparable with norms; A4C does not show the inferolateral wall."},"L6_9":{"clue":"LVOT diameter is squared in the CSA formula.","mech":"CSA = pi x (D/2) squared; a 10% error in diameter produces about a 21% error in area and therefore SV.","confirm":"Zoom on the LVOT in mid-systole, measure inner edge to inner edge at the annulus, and keep the same diameter for serial SV tracking.","trap":"VTI tracing and a 10 degree angle (cosine error about 1.5%) matter less; heart rate does not affect SV calculation."},"L6_10":{"clue":"An ICU patient whose EF improved while on norepinephrine and hypovolaemic.","mech":"EF depends on preload, afterload and contractility; catecholamines and low preload change EF without reflecting intrinsic myocardial recovery.","confirm":"Integrate EF with SV/LVOT VTI, filling pressures, LV size and the response to fluid or vasoactive changes to decide whether the heart limits perfusion.","trap":"Chasing a more precise EF or calling it normal misses the critical-care question: is the heart contributing to the current haemodynamic problem?"},"L7_1":{"clue":"Diastole defined physiologically, not electrically.","mech":"Diastole begins when the LV stops ejecting (aortic valve closure) and ends when ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure (mitral valve closure), including IVRT, rapid filling, diastasis and atrial contraction.","confirm":"Mark aortic closure and mitral closure on the Doppler or M-mode traces to identify IVRT and filling periods.","trap":"ECG intervals approximate timing but are not mechanical; MV closure to AV opening is isovolumic contraction, which is systole."},"L7_2":{"clue":"Reduced EF, regional wall motion abnormality or pathological LVH.","mech":"Each of these implies myocardial disease, which virtually always impairs relaxation, so the question becomes filling pressure rather than whether dysfunction exists.","confirm":"Move directly to the ASE 2016 filling-pressure algorithm: E/A and E, average E/e', TR velocity and LAVI.","trap":"A small LA and normal EF without LVH point toward the normal-EF algorithm; IVC size estimates RAP, not LV relaxation."},"L7_3":{"clue":"LA enlargement is sensitive but has non-diastolic causes.","mech":"LA volume reflects chronic exposure to raised pressure or volume, which also occurs in AF, high-output states, athletes and mitral valve disease.","confirm":"Interpret LAVI alongside E/e', TR velocity and mitral inflow; check rhythm, valve disease and output status before attributing it to diastolic dysfunction.","trap":"Hypovolaemia shrinks the LA; pericardial effusion and acute MI are not chronic LA dilators."},"L7_4":{"clue":"E/A 1.2 with E 85 cm/s in an abnormal LV; E/e' 16, TR 3.0 m/s, LAVI 40 mL/m2.","mech":"With intermediate E/A, three criteria arbitrate: E/e' above 14, TR above 2.8 m/s and LAVI above 34. Three of three positive indicates elevated LAP.","confirm":"Count criteria: 16 above 14 (positive), 3.0 above 2.8 (positive), 40 above 34 (positive): 3/3 elevated LAP, grade II.","trap":"Grade III needs E/A at least 2; indeterminate applies only when the criteria split 1:1 with one unavailable."},"L7_5":{"clue":"E/A 0.7 with E 45 cm/s.","mech":"Impaired relaxation shifts filling to late diastole (low E, dominant A); a low E with E/A at most 0.8 indicates LAP is not elevated.","confirm":"Apply the rule: E/A at most 0.8 and E at most 50 cm/s means grade I with normal LAP; no further criteria needed.","trap":"If E/A at most 0.8 but E is above 50 cm/s, criteria must be applied; reading E/A alone without E leads to errors."},"L7_6":{"clue":"E/A 2.3 with EF 25%.","mech":"High LAP drives a vigorous early E, while a stiff LV causes rapid equalisation (short DT) and a small A; this restrictive pattern indicates elevated filling pressures.","confirm":"Confirm with short DT (below about 160 ms), high E/e', TR velocity and enlarged LA; check response to preload reduction.","trap":"In young healthy people E/A above 2 can be normal, but with reduced EF it is restrictive; grade II requires E/A below 2."},"L7_7":{"clue":"E/e' below 8 versus above 15.","mech":"E rises with LAP, whereas e' reflects relaxation and is relatively load-independent; dividing E by e' corrects for relaxation and estimates filling pressure.","confirm":"Measure E and e' (average septal and lateral); below 8 suggests normal PCWP, above 14-15 suggests elevated.","trap":"Reversing the cut-offs is a common slip; values between 8 and 14 are indeterminate and need other criteria."},"L7_8":{"clue":"E 55 cm/s, e' 8 cm/s, TR 2.6 m/s in a dyspnoeic BiPAP patient.","mech":"E/e' 55/8 = 6.9 suggests low LAP; TR velocity below 2.8 m/s shows no pulmonary pressure elevation. Infiltrates are therefore unlikely cardiogenic.","confirm":"Calculate E/e' = 6.9 (normal), check TR below 2.8 m/s, and look for lung ultrasound signs of pneumonia; avoid diuresis on these numbers.","trap":"EF 47% does not mean high filling pressures; BiPAP does not prevent Doppler assessment."},"L7_9":{"clue":"Very low CO and SV with E/e' 5 and low E (grade I pattern).","mech":"Low E/e' and normal LAVI indicate low filling pressure; output is limited by contractility and preload, so removing volume would further reduce SV.","confirm":"Calculate CO (SV x HR) and confirm low LAP; consider careful small fluid challenges with SV monitoring plus inotropic or mechanical support.","trap":"Reflex diuresis in severe HFrEF is the trap; low output with low filling pressure is not congestion."},"L7_10":{"clue":"Conditions that confound mitral inflow or annular velocities.","mech":"AF removes the A wave and varies E; MAC and MV disease alter e' and inflow; regional dysfunction makes septal and lateral e' unrepresentative; constriction raises medial e'.","confirm":"Use alternative indices (TR velocity, LAVI, E/e' averaged over several beats, pulmonary vein flow) when these conditions are present.","trap":"Normal EF and young age are not limitations; the algorithm specifically includes normal-EF patients."},"L8_1":{"clue":"RV larger than two-thirds of the LV but still smaller than the LV in A4C.","mech":"The normal RV is crescentic and smaller than the LV; progressive enlargement is graded by comparing RV and LV size in the same image.","confirm":"Use an RV-focused A4C and measure basal diameter (above 41 mm abnormal) and RV/LV ratio; avoid foreshortened views.","trap":"Moderate enlargement is RV equal to LV and severe is RV larger than LV; overcalling happens when the A4C is rotated to exaggerate RV size."},"L8_2":{"clue":"RV basal diameter cut-off in an RV-focused A4C.","mech":"ASE/EACVI 2015 upper limits: basal 41 mm, mid 35 mm, longitudinal 83 mm; values above these indicate dilation.","confirm":"Use an RV-focused A4C with the apex centred; measure basal diameter in the basal third at end-diastole.","trap":"28 mm and 20 mm are below normal ranges; 60 mm longitudinal is within normal limits."},"L8_3":{"clue":"RV free-wall thickness 7 mm.","mech":"Free-wall thickness above 5 mm reflects hypertrophy from chronic pressure overload or infiltration; the RV cannot thicken within days.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in the subcostal view below the tricuspid annulus, excluding epicardial fat and trabeculae; above 10 mm is severe.","trap":"Acute cor pulmonale produces a dilated thin-walled RV; epicardial fat or effusion can be mistaken for wall thickness."},"L8_4":{"clue":"Dilated thin-walled RV with RVSP below 60 mm Hg.","mech":"A normal RV cannot acutely generate systolic pressures above about 60 mm Hg; chronic overload produces hypertrophy that allows higher pressures.","confirm":"Measure RV wall thickness (5 mm or less), RVSP by TR jet, and assess RA size; high pressure plus RVH points to a chronic process.","trap":"RVSP 85 mm Hg indicates chronicity, not acute failure; RA dilation alone is nonspecific."},"L8_5":{"clue":"TAPSE threshold for RV dysfunction.","mech":"TAPSE measures longitudinal excursion of the lateral tricuspid annulus, the dominant contraction direction of the RV.","confirm":"M-mode through the lateral tricuspid annulus in A4C with the cursor aligned to motion; below 17 mm indicates dysfunction. Cross-check with S' below 9.5 cm/s and FAC below 35%.","trap":"TAPSE is angle- and load-dependent and is reduced after cardiac surgery despite normal RV EF; 20-25 mm are normal values."},"L8_6":{"clue":"IVC 1.8 cm with less than 50% collapse.","mech":"IVC size and collapse reflect RA pressure; a small IVC with poor collapse is a mixed picture, so RAP is intermediate.","confirm":"Apply the ASE scheme: at most 2.1 cm and above 50% collapse = 3 mm Hg; above 2.1 cm and below 50% collapse = 15; otherwise 8 mm Hg.","trap":"Using diameter alone (small = 3) ignores the poor collapse; the ventilated-patient caveats do not apply here because breathing is spontaneous."},"L8_7":{"clue":"Small IVC below 12 mm in a ventilated patient.","mech":"Positive pressure raises intrathoracic pressure and distends the IVC; even so, a small IVC indicates low RA pressure, making it highly specific.","confirm":"Measure IVC 1-2 cm from the RA junction at end-expiration; below 12 mm strongly supports RAP below 10 mm Hg.","trap":"A large IVC under ventilation does not indicate high RAP; collapse above 50% applies to spontaneous breathing, and distensibility predicts fluid response, not RAP."},"L8_8":{"clue":"Tricuspid E/e' at least 4.7 in sepsis.","mech":"Analogous to mitral E/e', the ratio corrects tricuspid inflow for RV relaxation, estimating right atrial pressure.","confirm":"Measure tricuspid E with PW at leaflet tips and lateral tricuspid annulus e'; a ratio at least 4.7 suggests RAP at least 10 mm Hg.","trap":"It estimates pressure, not fluid responsiveness; it does not reflect left-sided PCWP."},"L8_9":{"clue":"TR Vmax 4.04 m/s and RAP 15 mm Hg.","mech":"Simplified Bernoulli converts peak TR velocity to the systolic RV-RA gradient; adding RAP gives RVSP.","confirm":"RVSP = 4 x 4.04 squared + 15 = 4 x 16.3 + 15 = 65 + 15 = 80 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting to add RAP gives 65; failing to square velocity gives about 31; doubling instead of squaring leads astray."},"L8_10":{"clue":"Prolonged bypass, poor myocardial protection and air to the RCA.","mech":"The RCA arises anteriorly so air bubbles enter it in the supine patient; long ischaemic times and inadequate cardioplegia stun the RV.","confirm":"Assess RV size, TAPSE/S', septal motion and TR on post-bypass TEE; correlate with CVP and haemodynamics.","trap":"Off-pump surgery and short-bypass procedures reduce, not increase, RV injury risk; sternal infection is a late, unrelated complication."},"L8_11":{"clue":"RV dysfunction after ROSC.","mech":"RV dysfunction after arrest reflects pulmonary vascular injury, ischaemia or underlying PE and limits LV filling, predicting poor outcome independently of LV function.","confirm":"Assess RV size, TAPSE, septal motion and TR velocity after ROSC; look for cause (PE, infarct, ventilator afterload).","trap":"A hyperdynamic LV and small IVC suggest hypovolaemia, not prognosis; normal RVSP is reassuring."},"L9_1":{"clue":"Distinguish preload, preload responsiveness, need for fluid and fluid tolerance.","mech":"Static preload (LVEDA, CVP) does not predict where the patient sits on the Frank-Starling curve; responsiveness does not mean fluid is needed or safe.","confirm":"Before giving fluid, confirm hypoperfusion, test responsiveness (PLR with VTI) and assess tolerance (venous congestion, B-lines, RV function).","trap":"Fluid responsiveness is a normal physiological state; treating it as an indication for fluid causes overload."},"L9_2":{"clue":"Passive ventilation, Vt 10 mL/kg, regular rhythm, closed chest.","mech":"SVV relies on cyclic changes in intrathoracic pressure from controlled tidal breaths altering preload; any extra variability or low Vt invalidates it.","confirm":"Check prerequisites: passive patient, Vt 8 mL/kg or more, sinus rhythm, normal compliance, HR/RR ratio above 3.6, closed chest; then measure LVOT Vmax variation.","trap":"Low-Vt ARDS, spontaneous breathing, BiPAP and arrhythmia patients produce false negatives or false positives."},"L9_3":{"clue":"Low tidal volume or low compliance.","mech":"Small Vt or stiff lungs transmit little pressure to the pleura, so preload barely changes and SVV stays low even when the patient is fluid responsive.","confirm":"Use a tidal volume challenge (6 to 8 mL/kg) or PLR instead when Vt is low or compliance reduced.","trap":"Spontaneous efforts, arrhythmia and raised abdominal pressure create false positives, not false negatives."},"L9_4":{"clue":"IVC distensibility in passive ventilation using Barbier's index.","mech":"In passive positive-pressure ventilation the IVC distends with inspiration; a fluid-responsive patient on the steep Starling curve shows greater distension.","confirm":"Measure IVCmax and IVCmin with M-mode; distensibility = (IVCmax - IVCmin)/IVCmin x 100, above 18% predicts response.","trap":"Feissel's index uses the mean (above 12%); 50% applies to collapsibility in spontaneous breathing."},"L9_5":{"clue":"Positive-pressure inspiration effect on extrathoracic and intrathoracic veins.","mech":"Raised intrathoracic pressure increases RA pressure, impeding venous return: the extrathoracic IVC distends, while the intrathoracic SVC is compressed.","confirm":"Measure IVC distensibility (TTE) or SVC collapsibility (TEE) to assess fluid responsiveness in ventilated patients.","trap":"Spontaneous inspiration produces the opposite pattern; confusing the breathing mode reverses interpretation."},"L9_6":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC despite fluid responsiveness.","mech":"Conditions raising RA pressure or RV afterload (tamponade, RV infarction, severe TR) distend the IVC and abolish collapse regardless of volume status.","confirm":"Assess RV function and pericardium before interpreting the IVC; use dynamic SV tests such as PLR with VTI.","trap":"Extreme inspiratory effort and lateral displacement cause false positives (collapse without responsiveness), not false negatives."},"L9_7":{"clue":"PLR autotransfusion volume.","mech":"Raising the legs and lowering the trunk shifts venous blood from the lower limbs and splanchnic bed toward the heart, about 300 mL, reversibly.","confirm":"Start semi-recumbent, lower trunk and raise legs 45 degrees; measure LVOT VTI or CO within 1 minute; at least 10-15% rise = responsive.","trap":"1-2 L overestimates; PLR is a reversible challenge equivalent to a modest bolus, not a large fluid load."},"L9_8":{"clue":"Bilateral below-knee amputation, spontaneously breathing, on nasal cannula.","mech":"PLR depends on lower-limb venous volume, which is reduced after amputation; SVV and SVC tests need passive ventilation, leaving IVC collapsibility.","confirm":"Measure IVC collapsibility with M-mode during quiet breathing; thresholds of about 40-48% suggest responsiveness, with limited accuracy.","trap":"PLR is attractive but unreliable here; LVOT variation and SVC distension require mechanical ventilation."},"L9_9":{"clue":"Rapid 50 mL bolus over 10 s with VTI change.","mech":"A tiny rapid bolus raises preload slightly; on the steep Starling curve SV changes measurably, predicting response to a larger bolus.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI before and immediately after 50 mL crystalloid over 10 s; a rise of at least about 10% predicts responsiveness.","trap":"Larger boluses are standard challenges, not mini-challenges; PPV and IVC thresholds are different methods."},"L9_10":{"clue":"PLR with TTE requires consistent probe position and baseline return.","mech":"Small percentage changes in VTI are easily swamped by changes in probe angle or window; returning to baseline confirms the effect.","confirm":"Keep the same apical window and angle, measure VTI baseline, during PLR and after return; a reversible rise of at least 10% confirms response.","trap":"Re-measuring LVOT diameter adds error; IVC size is a static measure; starting supine flat removes the splanchnic contribution."},"L10_1":{"clue":"Four ACP risk factors in ARDS from Mekontso Dessap.","mech":"Pneumonia, high driving pressure, severe hypoxaemia and hypercapnia increase pulmonary vascular resistance and RV afterload.","confirm":"Score one point each: pneumonia, driving pressure at least 18 cm H2O, P/F below 150, PaCO2 at least 48 mm Hg; screen with echo in high scorers.","trap":"Age, lactate, prone and ECMO are tempting severity markers but are not part of the score."},"L10_2":{"clue":"ACP definition: RV dilation plus septal dyskinesia.","mech":"Acute pressure overload dilates the RV and pushes the septum toward the LV at end-systole, producing paradoxical septal motion.","confirm":"Measure RVEDA/LVEDA ratio in A4C or TEE (0.6-1.0 moderate, above 1.0 severe) and assess septal motion in PSAX.","trap":"TAPSE, RVSP or wall thickness alone are not the definition; high RVSP or thick wall suggest chronicity."},"L10_3":{"clue":"Patchy B-lines with spared areas, irregular pleura and subpleural consolidations.","mech":"ARDS is inflammatory and heterogeneous, damaging pleura and causing patchy consolidation; cardiogenic oedema is hydrostatic and homogeneous.","confirm":"Scan multiple zones bilaterally: look for spared areas, pleural irregularity, subpleural consolidations, and reduced sliding; integrate with echo filling pressures.","trap":"Diffuse homogeneous B-lines with smooth pleura favour cardiogenic oedema; A-lines and lung point indicate other diagnoses."},"L10_4":{"clue":"Vasopressor property useful when RV afterload is high.","mech":"Low-dose vasopressin acts on V1 receptors to raise SVR and may cause pulmonary vasodilation via nitric oxide, maintaining coronary perfusion without increasing PVR.","confirm":"Add vasopressin 0.03 U/min to norepinephrine, monitor RV size, septal motion and SV.","trap":"Phenylephrine increases PVR; epinephrine and dopamine cause tachyarrhythmias; they do not reduce RV afterload."},"L10_5":{"clue":"RV-protective ventilation targets.","mech":"High plateau and driving pressures compress pulmonary capillaries and raise PVR; hypercapnia and hypoxaemia cause pulmonary vasoconstriction.","confirm":"Target plateau below 30 and driving pressure below 18 cm H2O, PaCO2 below about 48 mm Hg, adequate oxygenation, and prone positioning; reassess RV on echo.","trap":"High PEEP regardless of overdistension worsens RV afterload; higher Vt and permissive hypercapnia above 60 harm the RV."},"L10_6":{"clue":"Low PCWP, modest CVP, low CO with ACP on high PEEP.","mech":"The RV needs adequate but not excessive preload; a small bolus tests whether SV rises without worsening RV dilation and septal shift.","confirm":"Give 100 mL and measure LVOT VTI and RV size; continue only if SV rises and RV dilation does not worsen. Then reduce driving pressure and consider prone.","trap":"Raising PEEP worsens RV afterload; milrinone and PDE5 inhibitors can worsen systemic hypotension."},"L10_7":{"clue":"Maintain sinus rhythm in ARDS with ACP.","mech":"The failing RV relies on atrial contraction to fill; AF reduces filling and output.","confirm":"Treat AF promptly with cardioversion or rhythm control; monitor RV function and SV after conversion.","trap":"Rate control alone or pacing at 50 bpm does not restore atrial kick; slow rates reduce output in a fixed-SV RV."},"L10_8":{"clue":"Tissue-like lung with air bronchograms.","mech":"Consolidated alveoli filled with fluid or cells transmit sound like solid tissue; air in patent bronchi creates bright branching lines.","confirm":"Observe bronchograms: dynamic movement with breathing suggests pneumonia, static suggests atelectasis; evaluate extent with colour Doppler.","trap":"Effusion is anechoic; pneumothorax shows absent sliding and A-lines; normal lung shows A-lines."},"L10_9":{"clue":"Hypoxaemia worsening with higher PEEP in ARDS with ACP.","mech":"Increased RA pressure from high PEEP and ACP opens a PFO, creating right-to-left shunt and worsening hypoxaemia.","confirm":"Perform an agitated saline bubble study, preferably TEE bicaval view, with release of a Valsalva or PEEP manoeuvre; bubbles in the LA within 3 beats confirm PFO.","trap":"Valve disease and LV thrombus do not explain PEEP-sensitive hypoxaemia; pursuing them delays recognition of the shunt."},"L10_10":{"clue":"Mortality by Berlin ARDS severity.","mech":"Severity strata reflect worsening hypoxaemia and lung injury; mortality rises across mild, moderate and severe groups.","confirm":"Classify by P/F ratio with PEEP at least 5: mild 200-300, moderate 100-200, severe below 100; mortality about 27%, 32%, 45%.","trap":"Very high mortality (40-70%) overstates mild disease; low values (5-25%) understate."},"L11_1":{"clue":"Absence of lung sliding, lung pulse and B-lines at a point.","mech":"Each of these signs requires visceral pleura contact with parietal pleura; air separating them abolishes all three while A-lines persist.","confirm":"Scan anterior chest; if all three are absent, look for the lung point to confirm pneumothorax. Use M-mode for the barcode sign.","trap":"A-lines persist in pneumothorax; ribs and pleural line remain visible; seashore sign indicates normal sliding."},"L11_2":{"clue":"Supine trauma patient: pneumothorax air collects anteriorly where a supine film sees it poorly.","mech":"In the supine chest, free air rises to the anterior, medial and basal pleural space, so no apical edge appears on an AP film. The probe on the anterior chest finds that air directly.","confirm":"Scan the least-dependent anterior zones for absent sliding, absent B-lines and lung pulse, then find a lung point. Pooled LUS sensitivity is about 91% versus about 47% for CXR.","trap":"Option D swaps the numbers. CXR is very specific but misses roughly half of supine pneumothoraces, so a normal film does not exclude one."},"L11_3":{"clue":"Absent sliding can come from any process that stops pleural movement, not only from air between the pleural layers.","mech":"Sliding needs both ventilation and two freely moving apposed pleural layers. Adhesions, bullae, mainstem intubation or obstruction, apnoea, ARDS and hyperinflation all reduce or abolish it.","confirm":"Look for lung pulse or B-lines, which prove apposition and exclude pneumothorax at that spot; only a lung point confirms pneumothorax.","trap":"Effusion and pulmonary oedema usually preserve sliding. Calling pneumothorax from absent sliding alone leads to unnecessary drains, especially with bullae or a right mainstem tube."},"L11_4":{"clue":"A hyperechoic vertical line from the pleura that moves with sliding and reaches the bottom of the screen without fading.","mech":"When the lung periphery has lost some air (fluid, cells, fibrosis), air-fluid interfaces trap sound and reverberate, producing a long vertical artifact that erases A-lines.","confirm":"Count B-lines per intercostal space: three or more defines a positive interstitial region. Note distribution: homogeneous bilateral versus patchy or focal.","trap":"Short vertical artifacts that fade within 1-2 cm are comet tails (Z-lines or I-lines) with no pathological meaning; horizontal lines at multiples of pleural depth are A-lines."},"L11_5":{"clue":"B-lines track extravascular lung water and appear early, but many diseases produce them.","mech":"Any process thickening the interstitium or partially filling alveoli (hydrostatic oedema, ARDS, pneumonitis, ILD, haemorrhage) creates B-lines before oxygenation falls.","confirm":"Count B-lines serially to trend lung water; decide cause using echo filling pressures (E/e', LA, IVC), pleural line morphology and distribution.","trap":"B-lines do not estimate PAOP, and they are not specific for cardiogenic oedema; ARDS and fibrosis also give B-lines."},"L11_6":{"clue":"Air bronchograms that move with respiration inside a consolidation.","mech":"Movement of air within the bronchi means the airway is patent and connected to ventilated lung, which is typical of infective consolidation rather than resorptive collapse.","confirm":"Watch bronchograms through several breaths; dynamic movement favours pneumonia. Static bronchograms with volume loss suggest obstructive atelectasis and may prompt bronchoscopy.","trap":"Obstructive atelectasis shows static or absent bronchograms. Infarcts are typically wedge-shaped and air-free, and effusion is fluid with no air."},"L11_7":{"clue":"Small, homogeneous, hypoechoic, wedge-shaped subpleural lesion with sharp margins and no air.","mech":"Distal to an embolus, haemorrhagic infarction fills alveoli with blood, so the lesion is airless and pleural-based in the shape of the occluded segment.","confirm":"Look for multiple peripheral lesions, often in the dorsal lower lobes with small adjacent effusion, then scan the heart and leg veins. Confirm PE with CTPA when feasible.","trap":"Pneumonia lesions are polygonal with blurred margins and air inclusions; metastases are round with sharp margins. Calling every subpleural lesion pneumonia misses PE."},"L11_8":{"clue":"Septations within an effusion.","mech":"Fibrin strands form in inflammatory exudates and organising blood; transudates have no protein or fibrin to create septations.","confirm":"Treat septations as exudate or haemothorax: sample the fluid (pH, LDH, protein, cell count), and plan a drain if infection is suspected.","trap":"Anechoic does not mean transudate: exudates and acute haemothorax can be anechoic. Size and laterality do not discriminate."},"L11_9":{"clue":"Interpleural distance 30 mm at end-expiration in a supine ventilated patient.","mech":"In the supine patient fluid layers posteriorly; the parietal-visceral separation at the base scales roughly linearly with volume.","confirm":"Balik formula: V (mL) = 20 x separation (mm) = 20 x 30 = about 600 mL. Measure at end-expiration, posterior axillary line, maximal separation.","trap":"Using a different formula or measuring at end-inspiration changes the result; 200 mL undercounts and 1,500-3,000 mL misapply the multiplier."},"L11_10":{"clue":"On M-mode, the visceral pleura moves toward the parietal line with each inspiration, producing a sine wave.","mech":"With free fluid the aerated lung expands into the effusion on inspiration, so the interpleural distance falls cyclically.","confirm":"Place the M-mode line across the effusion; a sinusoid confirms free-flowing fluid. Its absence suggests trapped lung or viscous fluid such as empyema.","trap":"The sinusoid sign is a fluid sign, not a consolidation sign; pneumothorax shows the barcode sign."},"L11_11":{"clue":"Transverse and longitudinal neck scan showing thyroid cartilage, cricoid ring and tracheal rings.","mech":"The cricothyroid membrane spans between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages; it is superficial, relatively avascular and lies below the vocal cords.","confirm":"Use the longitudinal string-of-pearls view (tracheal rings), slide cranially to the cricoid, and mark the membrane just above it before any crisis.","trap":"The thyrohyoid membrane is above the larynx and would enter at the wrong level; tracheal rings are deeper and close to the thyroid isthmus."},"L12_1":{"clue":"BLUE combines lung profiles with leg venous compression.","mech":"Lung ultrasound classifies the pattern (A-lines, B-lines, sliding, consolidation, PLAPS); venous ultrasound adds the DVT probability that identifies PE in patients with a dry A-profile.","confirm":"Scan standard BLUE points then compress femoral and popliteal veins; follow the decision tree (A-profile plus DVT = PE).","trap":"Echocardiography, IVC and diaphragm assessments are useful additions in later protocols but not the original BLUE second component."},"L12_2":{"clue":"Bilateral anterior A-lines with sliding, plus a lower-limb DVT.","mech":"An A-profile means no interstitial syndrome, so cardiogenic oedema is unlikely; dyspnoea with a dry lung and a DVT points to embolic obstruction.","confirm":"Confirm DVT by full venous compression; assess RV size and function; treat as PE or confirm with CTPA depending on stability.","trap":"A-profile plus PLAPS without DVT suggests pneumonia; A-profile without DVT or PLAPS suggests COPD or asthma. B-profile suggests cardiogenic oedema."},"L12_3":{"clue":"Pooled accuracy of lung ultrasound versus CXR for acute heart failure.","mech":"Interstitial oedema produces diffuse bilateral B-lines before radiographic changes, so LUS detects congestion earlier and more reliably.","confirm":"Look for bilateral B-line positive zones (at least two per side); pooled LUS sensitivity and specificity are both about 92%, versus CXR about 77% and 87%.","trap":"Option A gives the CXR figures; choosing them assumes the radiograph is the better test."},"L12_4":{"clue":"LUS-ARDS score cut-off above 8.","mech":"A low cut-off detects almost all ARDS patients (high sensitivity), so a score at or below 8 makes ARDS unlikely; a higher cut-off (above 27) gives specificity.","confirm":"Calculate the score from standardised regions; use above 8 to rule out and above 27 to rule in bilateral opacities under the new global definition.","trap":"High specificity belongs to the higher cut-off. The score detects bilateral aeration loss; it does not predict PEEP recruitability or diagnose pneumonia."},"L12_5":{"clue":"Change in LUS score with PEEP compared with CT-measured recruitment.","mech":"LUS sees only the subpleural lung surface; aeration changes deeper in the lung and overdistension (already fully aerated, A-lines) cannot be graded.","confirm":"Use LUS to track gross aeration; quantify recruitment with pressure-volume curves, recruitment-to-inflation ratio, EIT or CT.","trap":"Assuming fewer B-lines with PEEP proves safe recruitment ignores overdistension, which looks identical to normal aeration on ultrasound."},"L12_6":{"clue":"Rise of at least 6 B-lines across four anterior points during the SBT.","mech":"Removing positive pressure increases venous return and LV afterload; a susceptible LV raises LAP, causing rapid interstitial oedema seen as new B-lines.","confirm":"Count B-lines at four anterior points before and at the end of the SBT; an increase of 6 or more supports weaning-induced oedema. Correlate with E/A and E/e' rise.","trap":"New effusion, lung point or loss of sliding reflect other processes; a single high B-line count without comparison does not show the SBT effect."},"L12_7":{"clue":"Lateral E/e' at most 8 on TEE in ventilated patients.","mech":"Low E relative to e' indicates that the LV fills without high driving pressure, so LA pressure, and therefore PAOP, is not elevated.","confirm":"Measure lateral mitral E and e' on TEE; at most 8 predicts PAOP at most 18 mm Hg. Repeat during SBT to detect rising filling pressures.","trap":"E/e' estimates a range, not an exact pressure; reading low E/e' as PAOP at least 25 reverses the relationship."},"L12_8":{"clue":"Reduced EF with restrictive inflow, or high E/e' with normal EF, and no CO rise with PLR.","mech":"Weaning increases preload and LV afterload; a ventricle already at high filling pressure or unable to increase output decompensates into pulmonary oedema.","confirm":"Before SBT measure EF, E/A, E/e' and PLR response; after SBT look for rising E/A and E/e' and new B-lines.","trap":"Hyperdynamic EF, normal TAPSE or small IVC do not predict cardiac weaning failure; hypovolaemia actually protects against weaning oedema."},"L12_9":{"clue":"Quiet-breathing excursion below 1.0 cm (men) or 0.9 cm (women).","mech":"Diaphragmatic contraction moves the dome caudally; reduced excursion reflects weakness, paralysis or ventilator-induced diaphragm dysfunction.","confirm":"Use M-mode via the liver window in the subcostal area; measure excursion during quiet breathing, and consider thickening fraction during spontaneous breathing trials.","trap":"Deep-breathing (about 4.7/3.7 cm) and sniff limits do not apply to quiet breathing; excursion under ventilator support reflects the ventilator, not the diaphragm."},"L12_10":{"clue":"Integrated cardiac, lung and venous POCUS for acute dyspnoea.","mech":"Bedside imaging quickly separates oedema, pneumonia, effusion, pneumothorax and PE, so correct treatment starts earlier.","confirm":"Integrate focused cardiac, lung and DVT scans with clinical data; evidence shows faster diagnosis and appropriate treatment.","trap":"No mortality benefit has been proven, and POCUS does not replace CT in all patients; claiming harder outcomes overstates the evidence."},"L13_1":{"clue":"The descending aorta is the landmark separating pericardial from left pleural fluid on PLAX.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta; pleural fluid accumulates posterior and lateral to it.","confirm":"In PLAX locate the descending thoracic aorta behind the LA; fluid anterior to it is pericardial, fluid posterior to it is pleural. Confirm in subcostal or apical views.","trap":"Assuming all posterior fluid is pericardial overcalls effusions; echogenicity does not distinguish the two."},"L13_2":{"clue":"Echo-free space 15 mm at end-diastole.","mech":"Effusion size is graded by maximal end-diastolic separation: small below 10 mm, moderate 10-20 mm, large above 20 mm.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in multiple views; 15 mm corresponds to moderate (about 100-500 mL).","trap":"Measuring in systole overestimates; size does not define tamponade, which depends on rate of accumulation and pressure."},"L13_3":{"clue":"RV free-wall inversion in early diastole.","mech":"When pericardial pressure exceeds RV diastolic pressure, the thin RV wall collapses; RV pressure is higher than RA, so its collapse indicates higher pericardial pressure.","confirm":"Use M-mode in PLAX or subcostal to time RV collapse to early diastole; RA collapse lasting more than one-third of the cycle increases specificity.","trap":"RA collapse is more sensitive but less specific; Doppler variation and effusion size alone are not specific."},"L13_4":{"clue":"Respiratory variation of mitral E at least 30% and tricuspid E at least 60%.","mech":"Fixed pericardial volume causes ventricular interdependence: inspiration increases RV filling and decreases LV filling, exaggerating normal variation.","confirm":"Record PW mitral and tricuspid inflow with a respirometer; variation = (max - min)/max. Interpret with clinical signs.","trap":"Variation is blunted by mechanical ventilation and AF; COPD and RV failure cause false positives."},"L13_5":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC in tamponade.","mech":"Raised pericardial pressure elevates RA pressure, distending the IVC and reducing collapse in most cases.","confirm":"Assess IVC size and collapse; a normal collapsing IVC makes tamponade unlikely except in low-pressure tamponade.","trap":"A plethoric IVC is nonspecific (RV failure, TR, ventilation); its absence does not exclude low-pressure tamponade."},"L13_6":{"clue":"Pulmonary hypertension or RVH prevents RV collapse.","mech":"High RV pressure or thick walls resist compression; the left heart may collapse first.","confirm":"Look for LA or LV collapse, Doppler variation and IVC in RVH or PH; integrate clinical signs.","trap":"Assuming no RV collapse means no tamponade misses atypical tamponade in RVH, PH or localised effusions."},"L13_7":{"clue":"Hepatic vein flow with systolic forward dominance, loss of diastolic forward flow and expiratory diastolic reversal.","mech":"Total heart volume is fixed, so venous return can only occur when the ventricles eject (systole); in diastole there is no room, abolishing the y descent, worst in expiration when right filling falls.","confirm":"Record PW 1-2 cm into a hepatic vein from subcostal with a respirometer trace; note systolic dominance and expiratory diastolic reversal.","trap":"Inspiratory diastolic reversal suggests restriction; expiratory reversal also occurs in constriction, so hepatic Doppler alone cannot separate constriction from tamponade."},"L13_8":{"clue":"Annulus reversus, respirophasic septal shift, mitral variation above 25%, expiratory hepatic reversal.","mech":"Constriction limits total filling but myocardium relaxes normally; respiratory changes transmit to the ventricles, creating interdependence.","confirm":"Compare medial and lateral e'; look for septal bounce and Doppler variation; confirm with CT/MRI or catheterisation.","trap":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy shows low e', no respiratory variation and inspiratory hepatic reversal."},"L13_9":{"clue":"Haemopericardium from type A dissection or free-wall rupture.","mech":"Pericardial pressure may be temporarily tamponading the bleed; draining it raises systemic pressure and can extend the tear or reopen the rupture, causing rapid fatal re-accumulation.","confirm":"Look for a dissection flap, dilated root, AR or a regional wall defect; mobilise surgery. Only limited controlled drainage as a bridge if arrest is imminent.","trap":"Malignant and post-surgical effusions are routinely drained percutaneously; para-apical access is a technique, not a contraindication."},"L13_10":{"clue":"Apparently dilated RV in PLAX, RV at the centre of the apical image (teardrop heart), leftward shift.","mech":"Without the left pericardium the heart rotates and shifts into the left chest, so standard planes cut the RV more obliquely and it looks enlarged; the heart is hypermobile.","confirm":"Correlate with CXR (leftward shift, interposed lung) and ECG; confirm with CT or MRI showing absent pericardium.","trap":"Calling RV dilation from pressure or volume overload triggers unnecessary workup; check RV wall thickness, septal shape and pressures, which are normal."},"L14_1":{"clue":"Undifferentiated shock: first identify what will kill the patient in minutes.","mech":"Acute LV failure, tamponade and acute cor pulmonale are directly treatable causes of low output; finding them changes immediate management.","confirm":"Use focused views (PLAX, PSAX, A4C, subcostal, IVC) for LV function, effusion with chamber collapse and RV dilation, then classify the shock.","trap":"Diastolic grading, LV mass and Simpson EF are comprehensive measures that delay life-saving decisions."},"L14_2":{"clue":"Both obstructive, but tamponade has effusion with collapsing right chambers, whereas ACP has a dilated RV without effusion.","mech":"Tamponade compresses the heart from outside, reducing chamber size; ACP from raised afterload dilates the RV and shifts the septum.","confirm":"Subcostal and A4C views: look for effusion with RA/RV collapse versus RV/LV ratio above 1 with septal flattening (D-sign).","trap":"IVC is plethoric in both, so it cannot differentiate; treatments are opposite (drainage versus RV afterload reduction or lysis)."},"L14_3":{"clue":"Small IVC despite high CVP.","mech":"Raised intra-abdominal pressure compresses the intra-abdominal IVC externally, so it looks small even when RA pressure is high.","confirm":"Measure bladder pressure; integrate IVC with RA size, hepatic vein Doppler and clinical signs of abdominal compartment syndrome.","trap":"Severe TR, RV infarction and tamponade raise RAP and distend the IVC, the opposite pattern."},"L14_4":{"clue":"Low e' and high E/e' in cardiogenic shock.","mech":"Low e' reflects impaired relaxation; high E/e' reflects raised LA pressure, markers of severe myocardial dysfunction and congestion.","confirm":"Measure septal and lateral e' and E; average E/e' above 14 indicates high filling pressure and adverse prognosis.","trap":"High e' and low E/e' indicate preserved relaxation and low filling pressure, which are reassuring, not adverse."},"L14_5":{"clue":"Acute severe MR from flail leaflet or papillary muscle rupture.","mech":"Sudden massive regurgitation into a non-compliant LA causes acute pulmonary oedema and forward output falls, producing cardiogenic shock.","confirm":"Look for a flail segment or mobile papillary head, wide eccentric colour jet and hyperdynamic LV; confirm with TEE and urgent surgery referral.","trap":"In acute MR the colour jet may be brief and underestimated because LA and LV pressures equalise; mild AR, trace TR and MAC do not cause shock."},"L14_6":{"clue":"Dynamic LVOT obstruction occurs beyond HCM.","mech":"A hyperdynamic underfilled LV with a narrow outflow (basal septal bulge, Takotsubo basal hyperkinesis) drags the mitral leaflet into the LVOT by Venturi and drag forces (SAM).","confirm":"Look for SAM, a late-peaking dagger-shaped CW signal and mid-systolic aortic valve closure; check response to fluid and stopping inotropes.","trap":"Assuming obstruction only in HCM misses it in sepsis, hypovolaemia and post-op patients on inotropes; DCM and severe AR enlarge the cavity and prevent it."},"L14_7":{"clue":"Prone ARDS patient on PEEP 20 with new shock, dilated RV and septal flattening.","mech":"High PEEP and driving pressure compress alveolar vessels and raise PVR, and hypoxaemia and hypercapnia cause vasoconstriction, causing RV afterload failure.","confirm":"Measure RV/LV ratio, septal motion and TR velocity; reduce driving pressure and PEEP if overdistended, correct CO2, consider inhaled vasodilators.","trap":"Hypovolaemia gives a small RV; tamponade requires effusion; LVOT obstruction requires a hyperdynamic small LV."},"L14_8":{"clue":"Extra-cardiac components of shock protocols.","mech":"Shock can arise from pump (heart), tank (volume and leaks) or pipes (aorta and veins); each needs targeted views.","confirm":"RUSH or E-FAST: lungs (pneumothorax, B-lines, effusion), FAST views for free fluid, aorta (AAA, dissection) and femoral/popliteal DVT.","trap":"Restricting to cardiac or renal views misses haemorrhage, pneumothorax, AAA and DVT."},"L14_9":{"clue":"Circumferential apical akinesis beyond one coronary territory with basal hyperkinesis.","mech":"Catecholamine-mediated stunning involves all apical segments in a non-coronary pattern; LAD occlusion normally spares some apical segments supplied by other arteries.","confirm":"Map all apical segments in A4C, A2C and A3C; check for LVOT obstruction; confirm with angiography.","trap":"A wrap-around LAD can mimic Takotsubo; anteroseptal akinesis sparing the apex or isolated inferior/lateral akinesis suggests coronary infarction."},"L14_10":{"clue":"Tamponade is a clinical and haemodynamic diagnosis.","mech":"Echo shows raised pericardial pressure (chamber collapse, Doppler variation), but the decision to drain depends on haemodynamic compromise and clinical context.","confirm":"Integrate hypotension, tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus and elevated JVP with echo signs; drain when there is compromise.","trap":"Echo signs alone (effusion size, RA collapse) are not sufficient; draining stable patients for echo signs alone exposes them to complications."},"L15_1":{"clue":"Electrical activity with coordinated cardiac motion but no palpable pulse.","mech":"Pseudo-PEA has residual contractility and some forward flow, insufficient to generate a palpable pulse; causes are often reversible.","confirm":"Use echo during the pulse check to identify motion; search for tamponade, PE, hypovolaemia and severe LV dysfunction.","trap":"True PEA has no cardiac motion; confusing them misses reversible causes and mislabels prognosis."},"L15_2":{"clue":"Any visible cardiac activity during arrest, linked to survival to discharge.","mech":"Retained myocardial motion means viable myocardium that may respond to treatment; standstill means very little chance of ROSC.","confirm":"Look for motion on a clip recorded during the 10 s pause and review it during compressions; cardiac activity carries an OR of about 5.7 for survival to discharge.","trap":"OR 3.6 is the figure for survival to admission. Standstill predicts poor outcome but is not a stand-alone reason to stop; integrate it with the clinical picture."},"L15_3":{"clue":"The time limit for a combined pulse check and ultrasound look.","mech":"Coronary perfusion pressure falls quickly when compressions stop and takes several compressions to recover, so each long pause reduces the chance of ROSC.","confirm":"Leader counts down aloud, record a clip during a pause of 10 s or less, then interpret it while compressions continue.","trap":"Allowing 20-30 s to get a perfect view is the classic error; studies show POCUS pauses often exceed the limit unless actively controlled."},"L15_4":{"clue":"No usable view at the 10 s limit.","mech":"Compressions take priority over imaging; another window at the next check costs nothing, whereas extending the pause lowers coronary perfusion.","confirm":"Resume compressions immediately; pre-position for a parasternal or apical view, or use TEE for continuous imaging.","trap":"Scanning longer to get a view, or giving empirical fluid or lytics without a diagnosis, trades CPR quality for uncertain gain."},"L15_5":{"clue":"Monitor shows asystole but TEE in the series showed fibrillating myocardium in some.","mech":"Fine VF produces low-amplitude ECG signals that look like asystole, but the myocardium visibly quivers on ultrasound, so the rhythm is shockable.","confirm":"On TEE look for disorganised, rapid, low-amplitude myocardial fibrillation; if seen, defibrillate. In this series 4 of 33 (12%) had fine VF.","trap":"Assuming monitor asystole is true asystole withholds defibrillation; overestimating (50-80%) misreads the series."},"L15_6":{"clue":"TEE shows the zone of maximal compression over the aortic root or LVOT.","mech":"Compressing the outflow tract narrows it during the compression phase, impeding ejection; compressing the LV body squeezes blood forward through an open outflow.","confirm":"On ME LAX, watch which structure is compressed; move hand position toward the LV until the LV cavity is compressed and the outflow opens.","trap":"Assuming any compression is good compression ignores outflow obstruction found in about half of patients in TEE series."},"L15_7":{"clue":"Dilated hypokinetic RV with an underfilled LV in a patient with organised electrical activity.","mech":"Massive PE abruptly raises RV afterload; the RV dilates and fails, LV preload falls via series and septal interdependence, collapsing output.","confirm":"Look for RV/LV ratio above 1, septal flattening, clot in transit and leg DVT; consider thrombolysis in the arrest setting.","trap":"RV dilation also occurs after prolonged CPR from any cause; tamponade shows effusion with collapse, hypovolaemia small ventricles, and pump failure a dilated LV."},"L15_8":{"clue":"New regional wall motion abnormality after a VT/VF arrest.","mech":"A regional rather than global pattern after a shockable arrest suggests an acute coronary occlusion caused the arrest.","confirm":"Correlate with ECG, map the RWMA to a coronary territory and discuss urgent coronary angiography with cardiology.","trap":"Post-arrest global stunning is common and resolves; thrombolysis is not indicated for RWMA alone, and pericardiocentesis needs an effusion."},"L15_9":{"clue":"In-hospital arrest survival to discharge.","mech":"Survival depends on rhythm, time to CPR and defibrillation and comorbidity; in-hospital arrests are witnessed and monitored, giving better outcomes than out-of-hospital ones.","confirm":"Remember about 18% survive to discharge, most with good neurological function (Morrison 2013).","trap":"Survival of 50-75% describes ROSC or shockable witnessed arrests, not overall discharge; 2% is closer to unwitnessed out-of-hospital outcomes."},"L16_1":{"clue":"EF cut-off for preserved function in the lecture algorithm.","mech":"An EF at least 50% means systolic function is preserved, so heart failure is driven by diastolic dysfunction, valves, pericardium or other causes.","confirm":"Measure EF by biplane disks; if at least 50%, assess LV volumes, diastolic function and etiologic features (RWMA, wall thickness, valves, PA pressure).","trap":"40% is the HFrEF upper cut-off; 41-49% is mildly reduced. 35% is a device-therapy threshold, not a classification boundary."},"L16_2":{"clue":"High E/A, short DT, high E/e', large LA and raised PASP together.","mech":"Elevated LA pressure drives a high early E and a stiff LV causes rapid deceleration; chronic high pressure enlarges the LA and raises PASP.","confirm":"Apply E/A above 1.5, E/e' above 15, DT below 160 ms and LA enlargement with TR velocity to grade filling pressures.","trap":"E/A below 0.8 with long DT is impaired relaxation with normal LAP; normal LA and TR below 2.5 m/s argue against high filling pressure."},"L16_3":{"clue":"SV 26 mL, HR 101 and BSA 1.51 m2.","mech":"Cardiac index normalises output to body size; low CI indicates inadequate perfusion.","confirm":"CO = 26 mL x 101 = 2.63 L/min; CI = 2.63/1.51 = about 1.74 L/min/m2 (low). SV = pi x (1.1 cm)2 x VTI.","trap":"Forgetting to divide by BSA gives 2.6; using LVOT diameter instead of radius in the area formula inflates SV."},"L16_4":{"clue":"Mayo criteria for Takotsubo.","mech":"Catecholamine-mediated myocardial stunning causes transient wall motion abnormalities beyond a single coronary territory, without plaque rupture.","confirm":"Look for apical ballooning or variant patterns, rule out obstructive CAD with angiography, and document recovery on follow-up echo.","trap":"Option B mixes a true exclusion (pheochromocytoma) with a false criterion: no EF threshold is required. A fixed single-territory defect points to infarction, not stress cardiomyopathy."},"L16_5":{"clue":"Asymmetric septal thickening (about 19 mm versus 12 mm) with very low e' and high E/e'.","mech":"HCM is a primary myocardial disease with myocyte disarray, producing asymmetric hypertrophy and markedly impaired relaxation; hypertensive LVH is concentric and milder.","confirm":"Measure septal and posterior wall thickness, septal e' and E/e'; look for SAM and LVOT gradient.","trap":"Mild concentric thickening suggests hypertension; normal e' argues against HCM; dilated LV suggests other cardiomyopathies."},"L16_6":{"clue":"Thick LV and RV walls with sparkling, biatrial enlargement, restrictive filling, apical-sparing strain.","mech":"Amyloid fibrils infiltrate the myocardium, increasing thickness without hypertrophy and causing restrictive physiology.","confirm":"Assess strain (apical sparing), diastolic function and look for low-voltage ECG; confirm with technetium scan, serum free light chains or biopsy.","trap":"Thin walls with dilation suggest DCM; isolated septal bulge is common in the elderly; normal diastolic function argues against amyloid."},"L16_7":{"clue":"Thick LV walls, binary endocardial appearance and low alpha-galactosidase A in renal failure.","mech":"Fabry is an X-linked lysosomal storage disease; glycosphingolipid accumulates in myocardium, causing hypertrophy and a two-layered endocardium.","confirm":"Confirm low enzyme activity (or GLA gene mutation), look for inferolateral LGE on CMR; assess renal and neurological involvement.","trap":"Amyloid also thickens walls but is not linked to low alpha-galactosidase; the binary sign is neither sensitive nor specific alone."},"L16_8":{"clue":"Mechanical MI complications seen on bedside echo.","mech":"Necrotic myocardium can rupture (free wall, septum, papillary muscle) or remodel, causing tethering and ischaemic MR.","confirm":"Use colour Doppler for VSD and MR, look for effusion with tamponade, and identify mobile papillary heads.","trap":"Coronary dissection and Dressler syndrome are not primary echo diagnoses; LV thrombus is only one of several complications."},"L16_9":{"clue":"Preferred method for LV volumes and EF in heart failure.","mech":"Volumetric methods (biplane disks or 3D) account for LV shape distortion and regional wall motion, improving accuracy.","confirm":"Use biplane disks or 3D volumes; add GLS for subclinical dysfunction.","trap":"Linear Teichholz and EPSS assume symmetric geometry; visual estimates alone are less reproducible."},"L16_10":{"clue":"Stages of eosinophilic myocarditis.","mech":"Eosinophil infiltration causes necrosis, endocardial damage, thrombus formation and fibrosis, leading to restrictive physiology.","confirm":"Look for apical thrombus over abnormal myocardium, then restrictive filling and posterior leaflet tethering; confirm with eosinophil count and CMR.","trap":"Hypertrophy, calcification or fatty infiltration are unrelated stages."},"B9_3":{"clue":"Regional pattern: basal and mid RV free wall hypokinetic while the RV apex still contracts.","mech":"Acute afterload rise strains the basal and mid free wall most; the apex may be tethered to a hyperdynamic LV, so apical motion is relatively preserved.","confirm":"In an RV-focused apical 4-chamber view, watch free wall excursion segment by segment and pair it with RV size, septal shape and TAPSE.","trap":"Do not confuse it with the shared apex sign, which reflects RV dilation; and McConnell is not specific to PE, since RV infarction can mimic it."},"B9_4":{"clue":"Basal RV diameter of 44 mm in an RV-focused apical 4-chamber view exceeds the 41 mm threshold.","mech":"The RV dilates mainly at its base and mid cavity in response to volume or pressure load, so basal width is the most sensitive linear index.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in an RV-focused view: basal >41 mm, mid >35 mm, longitudinal >83 mm define dilation.","trap":"The other values sit below their cutoffs (mid 35, longitudinal 83, distal RVOT 27 mm); a foreshortened standard A4C also underestimates RV size."},"B9_5":{"clue":"RV free wall 7 mm at end-diastole in the subcostal view, above the 5 mm upper limit.","mech":"Hypertrophy needs weeks to months of sustained pressure load; an acutely obstructed RV dilates but cannot thicken within days.","confirm":"Measure free wall thickness in subcostal view at end-diastole excluding trabeculae and epicardial fat; look for high PASP (TR velocity) suggesting chronic pulmonary hypertension.","trap":"Assuming any RV strain pattern in suspected PE is acute; a thick RV with very high PASP points to chronic or acute-on-chronic disease such as CTEPH."},"B9_6":{"clue":"Septal flattening in both systole and diastole on parasternal short axis, giving a D-shaped LV throughout the cycle.","mech":"With pressure overload RV pressure approaches or exceeds LV pressure even in systole, so the septum is pushed leftward all cycle long.","confirm":"Time the flattening with ECG or M-mode and calculate eccentricity index; estimate PASP from TR velocity plus RA pressure.","trap":"Volume overload flattens the septum mainly in diastole with normal systolic curvature; diastolic-only flattening points away from pure pressure load."},"B9_7":{"clue":"The question asks about ruling out PE with echocardiography.","mech":"Many PEs, especially small or peripheral clots, do not raise RV afterload enough to cause visible RV dilation or dysfunction, so a normal echo is common.","confirm":"Use CT pulmonary angiography or compression ultrasound and pretest probability to exclude PE; reserve echo for unstable patients to detect RV overload.","trap":"Assuming a normal RV excludes PE; echo is a poor rule-out test and is most useful for diagnosis and risk stratification in shock."},"B9_8":{"clue":"Wedge-shaped, homogeneous, hypoechoic pleural-based lesion in the dorsal lower lobe plus a small effusion.","mech":"Arterial occlusion causes hemorrhagic infarction of the peripheral lung, which appears as a solid, airless, pleural-based wedge without air bronchograms.","confirm":"Look for multiple similar lesions, absent air bronchograms and absent internal flow, then perform compression ultrasound of the legs for DVT.","trap":"Calling it pneumonia: consolidation from pneumonia usually shows dynamic air bronchograms and irregular margins, not a homogeneous wedge."},"B9_9":{"clue":"The vein fails to collapse completely under pressure sufficient to deform the adjacent artery.","mech":"A normal vein has low intraluminal pressure and collapses easily; thrombus fills the lumen and prevents apposition of the walls.","confirm":"Compress the common femoral and popliteal veins in transverse view, applying enough pressure to deform the artery, and document full or incomplete wall apposition.","trap":"Waiting to see echogenic thrombus: fresh thrombus can be anechoic, so incompressibility, not visible clot, defines a positive study."},"B9_10":{"clue":"Lateral-to-medial order in the femoral triangle: nerve, artery, vein.","mech":"Anatomy follows the NAVY mnemonic (Nerve, Artery, Vein, Y-fronts toward the midline), with the vein most medial.","confirm":"Identify the pulsatile, non-compressible artery with color Doppler; the vein lies medial and collapses with pressure.","trap":"Mistaking the artery for a thrombosed vein because both are non-compressible; in the popliteal fossa the vein is superficial to the artery."},"B9_11":{"clue":"High-frequency linear probe, compressions about every 1 cm, reverse Trendelenburg.","mech":"A 5-10 MHz probe gives the resolution needed for superficial veins, and head-up tilt distends leg veins so compressibility is easier to judge.","confirm":"Compress serially from the common femoral vein through the femoral and deep femoral bifurcation and in the popliteal fossa past the crease.","trap":"Trendelenburg empties leg veins; single-point compression skips segments; and Doppler augmentation is not the primary criterion."},"B9_12":{"clue":"Color Doppler is used to identify vessels and to separate them from nodes or nerves.","mech":"Compression is the diagnostic criterion; color flow simply confirms that a tubular structure is a vessel rather than a solid structure.","confirm":"Apply color to identify the artery and vein, then rely on compression for diagnosis; image iliac veins or use CT for suspected pelvic DVT.","trap":"Believing the 2-point exam sees pelvic veins or that ACEP mandates augmentation; an occlusive thrombus blunts, not increases, augmented flow."},"B10_1":{"clue":"Circumferential end-diastolic echo-free space of 22 mm.","mech":"Size is graded by the maximal end-diastolic separation of pericardial layers: moderate 10-20 mm, large over 20 mm, very large over 25 mm.","confirm":"Measure in end-diastole in more than one view and pair size with chamber collapse, IVC and Doppler findings for hemodynamic effect.","trap":"Equating size with tamponade; hemodynamic impact depends on rate of accumulation and pericardial compliance, not size alone."},"B10_2":{"clue":"In PLAX, fluid tracks anterior to the descending thoracic aorta, between it and the LA.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta, whereas the left pleural space extends posterior to it.","confirm":"Locate the descending aorta behind the LA in PLAX; fluid between aorta and heart is pericardial, fluid behind it is pleural.","trap":"An anterior-only echo space that moves with the heart and is brighter than myocardium is usually epicardial fat, not effusion."},"B10_3":{"clue":"Small to moderate effusion causing shock minutes after a lead procedure.","mech":"The pericardial pressure-volume curve is steep; without time to stretch, a small volume sharply raises intrapericardial pressure.","confirm":"Look for RA and RV diastolic collapse and a plethoric IVC; urgent pericardiocentesis with hemodynamic response confirms tamponade.","trap":"Dismissing tamponade because the effusion is small; rate of accumulation, not volume, determines the pressure rise."},"B10_4":{"clue":"RA collapse beginning in late diastole or early systole, more specific if it lasts over 30% of the cycle.","mech":"The thin-walled, low-pressure RA collapses when its pressure is lowest during atrial relaxation; prolonged collapse means intrapericardial pressure exceeds RA pressure for longer.","confirm":"Use apical 4-chamber or subcostal views with M-mode or frame-by-frame review to time collapse relative to the ECG.","trap":"RA collapse is typically the first and most sensitive sign; brief collapse alone is nonspecific, and it is not best seen in PLAX."},"B10_5":{"clue":"RV free wall indentation just after the T wave, that is in early diastole.","mech":"In early diastole RV pressure is at its lowest while intrapericardial pressure is high, so the compliant RV free wall buckles inward.","confirm":"Place M-mode through the RV free wall in PLAX or subcostal view and time inward motion against ECG and mitral opening.","trap":"Calling it RA collapse or normal: RA collapse is atrial and late diastolic; RV early diastolic collapse is less sensitive but more specific."},"B10_6":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC with hepatic vein diastolic flow blunted or reversed in expiration.","mech":"Tamponade fixes total cardiac volume, so venous return occurs mainly in systole; in expiration right heart filling falls further, producing diastolic flow reversal.","confirm":"Record hepatic vein PW Doppler with a respirometer trace, noting expiratory diastolic reversal and systolic predominance.","trap":"IVC plethora alone is sensitive but nonspecific; inspiratory reversal is more typical of constriction or pulmonary hypertension, not tamponade."},"B10_7":{"clue":"Mitral E 100 cm/s in expiration and 62 cm/s in inspiration.","mech":"In tamponade, inspiratory rise in right filling shifts the septum left within a fixed pericardial volume, reducing LV inflow.","confirm":"Variation = (Exp minus Insp) / Exp = (100 minus 62)/100 = 38%, above the 30% mitral threshold.","trap":"Applying the 60% tricuspid threshold to mitral inflow, or dividing by the inspiratory value; and inflow variation alone is never diagnostic."},"B10_8":{"clue":"Positive pressure ventilation with no inflow variation or collapse, but a dilated IVC and a small RV cavity.","mech":"PPV reverses and blunts normal respiratory loading, masking classic signs; high RA pressure with a small RV implies external compression of the RV.","confirm":"Pair the dilated IVC and small RV with clinical shock and consider TEE for loculated collection; drainage with hemodynamic improvement confirms.","trap":"Excluding tamponade because Doppler variation and collapse are absent; these signs are unreliable under mechanical ventilation."},"B10_9":{"clue":"RA and RV diastolic collapse with a small, collapsible IVC in a hemorrhaging patient.","mech":"Hypovolemia lowers intracardiac pressures, so a modest intrapericardial pressure is enough to collapse chambers without venous congestion.","confirm":"Correlate collapse with shock and consider cautious volume plus pericardiocentesis; watch for improvement after drainage.","trap":"Using a small IVC to exclude tamponade; IVC plethora is absent in low-pressure tamponade."},"B10_10":{"clue":"Chronic pulmonary hypertension with RV hypertrophy and a large effusion with hypotension.","mech":"A thick, high-pressure RV resists external compression, so the lower-pressure LV or left atrium becomes the chamber that collapses.","confirm":"Look for LV or LA diastolic collapse and absent RV collapse; tricuspid inflow variation may also be missing.","trap":"Assuming pulmonary hypertension prevents tamponade or that the RV must collapse; it only changes which chamber yields."},"B10_11":{"clue":"Post-cardiac surgery shock with high right pressures and no effusion seen on TTE.","mech":"Postoperative bleeding often forms a loculated, posterior clot compressing atria or ventricles regionally, in windows TTE images poorly.","confirm":"Perform urgent TEE to look for localized hematoma compressing the RA, LA or ventricles, and involve surgeons for reexploration.","trap":"Trusting a negative TTE or pulsus paradoxus; regional tamponade often lacks classic signs and equalization of pressures."},"B10_12":{"clue":"Interim bridge: fluid helps only some patients and norepinephrine raises MAP.","mech":"Volume can raise filling pressure above pericardial pressure in hypovolemic patients, but in others it increases pericardial pressure further and worsens filling.","confirm":"Give small boluses only with monitoring of BP and cardiac output, use norepinephrine for MAP, and proceed promptly to drainage.","trap":"Routine fluid for all, or the belief that low RA pressure predicts benefit; definitive treatment is drainage, not a drug bridge."},"B10_13":{"clue":"New effusion on VA ECMO with failed weaning and equivocal collapse.","mech":"ECMO drains the right heart and supports flow, masking collapse and pressure changes; the effusion still restricts native cardiac filling when flow is reduced.","confirm":"Look for loss of pulsatility, regional compression on TEE and response to drainage or clot evacuation.","trap":"Labelling it hypovolemia or primary RV failure because classic tamponade signs are absent; on ECMO they are unreliable."},"B10_14":{"clue":"Acute RV dilation and shock hours after surgical drainage in a patient with previously normal function.","mech":"Pericardial decompression syndrome: sudden loss of external constraint causes abrupt venous return and ventricular mismatch, producing paradoxical failure.","confirm":"Repeat echo for new RV, LV or biventricular dysfunction and exclude reaccumulation or bleeding; support with inotropes and mechanical support if needed.","trap":"It can occur up to 48 hours later, usually with normal prior function, and is not caused by fluid reaccumulation."},"B11_1":{"clue":"Mitral E 110 cm/s with septal e' 7 cm/s.","mech":"E rises with LA pressure while e' reflects relaxation and is relatively load-independent, so the ratio tracks filling pressure.","confirm":"Septal E/e' = 110/7 = about 16, above the septal cutoff of 15; corroborate with LA size and TR velocity if available.","trap":"Using the average cutoff of 14 to call it indeterminate, or believing a TR jet is needed before E/e' can be interpreted."},"B11_2":{"clue":"Normal lower limits: septal e' at least 7 cm/s and lateral e' at least 10 cm/s.","mech":"The lateral annulus moves faster because it is less tethered by the stiffer septum and its fibres move more freely.","confirm":"Place the TDI sample at the septal and lateral annulus in apical 4-chamber, align with motion and measure early diastolic peak.","trap":"Reversing the values: septal e' is normally lower than lateral, not higher."},"B11_3":{"clue":"E/A 0.6 with low septal e' and E/e' 12 and a normal LA.","mech":"Slow relaxation reduces early filling so atrial contraction contributes more, but LA pressure is not yet high enough to restore E.","confirm":"Check the four criteria: E/e' not over 14, normal LA volume and TR velocity under 2.8 m/s support normal LAP.","trap":"Calling it pseudonormal or restrictive; those require E/A above 0.8 with elevated filling pressure markers."},"B11_4":{"clue":"Ambiguous E/A of 1.2; preload reduction to unmask impaired relaxation.","mech":"Valsalva strain lowers venous return and LAP; in pseudonormal filling E falls and the pattern reverts toward impaired relaxation.","confirm":"Record mitral inflow during strain phase; a 50% or greater fall in E/A indicates elevated filling pressure.","trap":"Fluid or PLR increase preload rather than reduce it, and PEEP effects on deceleration time are not a validated discriminator."},"B11_5":{"clue":"E/A 2.4, low e', E/e' 18 and very short deceleration time.","mech":"A stiff LV with high LAP fills rapidly in early diastole and pressures equalize quickly, so E is tall and decelerates fast.","confirm":"Confirm E/A at least 2 with short DT under 160 ms, high E/e' and dilated LA, indicating grade III.","trap":"Calling it pseudonormal; that pattern has E/A between 0.8 and 2 and needs filling pressure markers to identify."},"B11_6":{"clue":"TR velocity 3.3 m/s in a patient with ARDS.","mech":"TR velocity reflects PA systolic pressure, which mirrors LAP only if pulmonary vascular resistance is normal; ARDS raises PVR independently.","confirm":"Use E/e', LA size, pulmonary vein flow and lateral E/e' instead; compare with RV size and function to judge pulmonary vascular disease.","trap":"Assuming TR velocity is load-independent or valid only in AF or subcostal views; its limit is pulmonary vascular disease."},"B11_7":{"clue":"LAVI cutoff of 34 mL/m2, which reflects chronic remodeling.","mech":"LA enlargement develops over time from sustained high pressure, so it is a marker of chronicity rather than acute filling pressure.","confirm":"Measure by biplane method of discs at end-systole, excluding appendage and pulmonary veins, and index to BSA.","trap":"Assuming LAVI tracks acute changes; acute rises may not dilate the LA and ventilation alters atrial size."},"B11_8":{"clue":"AF with variable RR intervals; E/e' remains usable with matched beats.","mech":"E and e' both vary with cycle length; taking them from beats with similar preceding intervals keeps the ratio internally consistent.","confirm":"Measure E and e' from cycles with comparable preceding RR intervals or average about 10 beats.","trap":"There is no A wave in AF, so E/A is invalid, and LAVI is confounded by AF itself."},"B11_9":{"clue":"Suspected constriction with high septal e' of 12 cm/s and low E/e'.","mech":"In constriction the pericardium restricts lateral motion, so longitudinal septal motion is preserved or exaggerated, making e' high despite high LAP (annulus reversus).","confirm":"Compare septal and lateral e' (septal higher than lateral), look for septal bounce and respiratory variation in inflow and hepatic veins.","trap":"Reading low E/e' as normal LAP; E/e' assumptions also fail in MS, prosthetic MV, significant MR and regional WMAs."},"B11_10":{"clue":"PW gate at the mitral leaflet tips within the LV.","mech":"The highest inflow velocities are at the leaflet tips where the orifice is narrowest; more apical sampling records slower, dispersed flow.","confirm":"Use apical 4-chamber, 1-3 mm gate at the tips, angle under 20 degrees, average 3 beats at end-expiration.","trap":"Sampling at the annulus measures annular inflow, and tissue Doppler filters are for e' not E."},"B11_11":{"clue":"High E/e' used to reject fluid; the question distinguishes LAP from responsiveness.","mech":"Responsiveness depends on whether the ventricle operates on the steep part of its Frank-Starling curve, not on absolute filling pressure.","confirm":"Use a dynamic test such as PLR with VTI change or pulse pressure variation; use E/e' for fluid tolerance judgement.","trap":"Assuming high filling pressure means no response; static pressures, like PCWP, do not predict responsiveness."},"B11_12":{"clue":"Heart rate 135/min with fused mitral E and A waves.","mech":"Short diastole superimposes early and atrial filling; the fused peak still largely reflects the LA-LV gradient, so its ratio to e' tracks filling pressure.","confirm":"Take the fused peak as E and divide by e' (septal, lateral or average), interpreting against the usual cutoffs.","trap":"Declaring the study indeterminate or using deceleration time; E/A is lost with fusion but E/e' remains generally usable."},"B12_1":{"clue":"Small, hyperdynamic LV with near cavity obliteration and LVEDA 4.8 cm2/m2.","mech":"Low venous return reduces end-diastolic filling, so the LV cavity is small and contracts to near obliteration.","confirm":"Indexed LVEDA under 5.5 cm2/m2 supports hypovolemia; add IVC indices and a dynamic preload test such as PLR with VTI.","trap":"Confusing the indexed threshold (5.5 cm2/m2) with the absolute value (over 12 cm2 argues against hypovolemia), or labelling it LVOT obstruction without Doppler."},"B12_2":{"clue":"Passively ventilated patient: IVC distensibility index over 18%.","mech":"Under positive pressure the IVC distends with inspiration; large distension implies the vein sits on the compliant part of its curve, suggesting low volume.","confirm":"Distensibility = (Dmax minus Dmin)/Dmin, measured with M-mode 2 cm from the RA junction in a fully passive patient with tidal volume around 8 mL/kg.","trap":"Using collapsibility thresholds (50% or 75%), which apply to spontaneously breathing patients, not to ventilated ones."},"B12_3":{"clue":"FUSA starts by asking whether chronic cardiac disease is present.","mech":"Chronic dilation or hypertrophy alters baseline chamber sizes and filling pressures, confounding focused volume and pump assessment.","confirm":"Look for LV or RV dilation and hypertrophy, LA enlargement and valve disease before the steps of pump, volume, tamponade and lung.","trap":"Assuming the first step is tamponade or VTI as in other shock protocols; FUSA explicitly screens chronic disease first."},"B12_4":{"clue":"Dilated, apex-forming RV with RV/LV over 1 and septal flattening, but a thin 3 mm free wall.","mech":"The RV dilates acutely when afterload rises abruptly; hypertrophy needs weeks, so a thin wall points to an acute insult.","confirm":"Measure free wall thickness in subcostal end-diastole (5 mm or less is thin), check TR velocity and look for DVT or thrombus in transit.","trap":"Calling chronic pulmonary hypertension; that produces a thick RV wall and usually a higher PASP than an acutely failing RV can generate."},"B12_5":{"clue":"The pipes step of RUSH: aorta and deep veins.","mech":"Ruptured or obstructed vessels cause shock by hemorrhage (aortic rupture, dissection) or obstruction (DVT leading to PE).","confirm":"Image the aortic root in PLAX, suprasternal arch, abdominal aorta segments and compress femoral and popliteal veins.","trap":"IVC, lung and FAST belong to the tank step; pericardium and RV:LV belong to the pump step."},"B12_6":{"clue":"Developing tamponade: the earliest chamber to collapse.","mech":"The RA has the lowest pressure and thinnest wall; during atrial relaxation in systole, intrapericardial pressure exceeds it first.","confirm":"Time collapse on apical 4-chamber or subcostal view with ECG or M-mode; then look for RV diastolic collapse as severity increases.","trap":"RV diastolic collapse is more specific but appears later; LV and LA collapse are uncommon except in loculated or right-sided pressure overload states."},"B12_7":{"clue":"PLAX with depth enough to see the descending aorta: pericardial fluid lies anterior to it.","mech":"The posterior pericardial reflection sits between the heart and the descending aorta; the left pleural space extends behind the aorta.","confirm":"Increase depth to display the descending aorta and note whether fluid tapers in front of or extends behind it.","trap":"Assuming any posterior fluid is pericardial; fluid behind the aorta is pleural, and pericardial fluid does not usually extend behind the LA."},"B12_8":{"clue":"Post-cardiotomy shock with an apparently compressed LV and no effusion on TTE.","mech":"Loculated clot behind the heart can compress the left chambers selectively without visible circumferential fluid on limited postoperative windows.","confirm":"Perform TEE to image posterior structures and look for clot compressing atria or ventricles; inform the surgeon.","trap":"Excluding tamponade because TTE shows no fluid or the right heart is not compressed; regional tamponade can spare the right side."},"B12_9":{"clue":"SV rises from 50 to 59 mL after fluid challenge.","mech":"A rise in SV with preload means the ventricle is on the ascending part of the Frank-Starling curve.","confirm":"Percent change = (59 minus 50)/50 = 18%, above the 15% threshold for fluid responsiveness.","trap":"Applying the 20% contractile reserve threshold, which belongs to inotrope testing, not fluid challenges."},"B12_10":{"clue":"Dynamic LVOT obstruction or severe AR invalidates LVOT VTI as an SV surrogate.","mech":"Obstruction distorts flow and velocity profile in the LVOT, and severe AR adds regurgitant volume so forward flow differs from total LVOT flow.","confirm":"Check for SAM, aliasing or late-peaking LVOT signal, and grade AR before relying on VTI; use an alternative site if invalid.","trap":"Moderate MR, PEEP and normal heart rates do not invalidate LVOT VTI; it still measures forward SV."},"B12_11":{"clue":"Small, hyperdynamic LV with increased SV, normal LVEDA and normal IVC.","mech":"Low afterload lets the LV empty more completely, yielding high SV with normal filling, as in vasodilatory states.","confirm":"Confirm high VTI and cardiac output with normal LVEDA and IVC; exclude other causes since vasodilatory shock is diagnosed by exclusion.","trap":"A small LV with low SV suggests underfilling or obstruction; here SV is increased, which points to low afterload rather than hypovolemia."},"B12_12":{"clue":"LV hypertrophy, hypotension on dobutamine and a late-peaking, dagger-shaped LVOT signal.","mech":"Inotropy and hypovolemia shrink the hypertrophied LV cavity so the outflow narrows progressively in systole, producing a late-peaking gradient.","confirm":"Walk PW from apex to LVOT to localise aliasing, look for SAM, and measure peak gradient with CW; stop inotropes and give volume.","trap":"Confusing with MR: MR is symmetric and earlier peaking; AR is diastolic and directed toward the LV."},"B13_1":{"clue":"IVC collapsed throughout the respiratory cycle in a ventilated patient despite RV overload and effusion.","mech":"Under positive pressure a persistently collapsed IVC means low right atrial pressure, so preload is insufficient to tolerate higher intrathoracic pressure.","confirm":"Give a fluid bolus and reassess IVC, VTI and blood pressure before raising PEEP or recruiting.","trap":"Diuresing for B lines or draining the effusion; low preload would make recruitment poorly tolerated and the effusion is not causing tamponade with low pressures."},"B13_2":{"clue":"Posterior zones in the 12-zone protocol.","mech":"Supine ventilated patients develop gravity-dependent posterior consolidation and loss of aeration that anterior zones miss.","confirm":"Scan anterior, lateral and posterior zones superior and inferior on each side to compute an aeration score.","trap":"Believing it is the only CT-validated protocol or the fastest for pneumothorax; no head-to-head comparison of protocols exists."},"B13_3":{"clue":"A 28-zone protocol for interstitial syndrome monitoring.","mech":"Counting B lines over many anterior and lateral sites quantifies extravascular lung water in heart failure.","confirm":"Count B lines in each zone and sum the total for serial comparison.","trap":"Pneumothorax screening in trauma uses an abbreviated anterior protocol, not 28 zones."},"B13_4":{"clue":"BLUE protocol PE accuracy: sensitivity 81% and specificity 99%.","mech":"In the protocol, PE is called when lungs show an A profile plus venous thrombosis, a combination that is very specific but misses PE without DVT.","confirm":"Combine A profile lungs with compression ultrasound; a positive DVT supports PE, while a negative does not exclude it.","trap":"Inverting the numbers; the protocol is far more specific than sensitive for PE because not all PE patients have visible DVT."},"B13_5":{"clue":"Absent lung sliding over an entire hemithorax with A lines but no lung point, after intubation.","mech":"With right mainstem intubation the left lung is not ventilated, so the pleura do not slide although they remain in contact.","confirm":"Look for a lung pulse, which proves pleural apposition, and check tube position by auscultation, CXR or tube depth.","trap":"Absent sliding alone is not pneumothorax; only a lung point is specific, and lung pulse excludes pneumothorax at that site."},"B13_6":{"clue":"Pericardial movement in the paracardiac area mimics a lung point.","mech":"Cardiac motion next to the pleura creates an alternating pattern that resembles the edge of a pneumothorax.","confirm":"Move away from the heart border and confirm the pattern changes with respiration, not with the cardiac cycle.","trap":"Assuming any transition is a lung point; diaphragm motion, adhesions or bulla edges can also mimic it, and complete pneumothorax has no lung point."},"B13_7":{"clue":"Dependent echogenic layer with an anechoic layer above in a trauma effusion.","mech":"With time, red cells sediment dependently while serum separates above, forming a hematocrit sign.","confirm":"Correlate with the timing of injury and hemoglobin trend; perform diagnostic thoracentesis or CT as needed.","trap":"Acute hemothorax is usually homogeneously echogenic; layering suggests an older bleed."},"B13_8":{"clue":"Completely anechoic pleural effusion.","mech":"Echogenicity depends on cells, protein and fibrin; many exudates remain anechoic, so absence of echoes cannot classify the fluid.","confirm":"Perform diagnostic thoracentesis and apply Light criteria to classify the fluid.","trap":"Assuming anechoic means transudate; transudates are usually anechoic but exudates frequently are too."},"B13_9":{"clue":"Multiple coalescent B lines in an intercostal space.","mech":"Coalescent B lines reflect thickened interlobular septa and alveolar flooding leaving little air, so aeration is severely reduced.","confirm":"Score the region as 2 in the aeration score and compare with consolidation (score 3) and well-spaced B lines (score 1).","trap":"Isolated B lines in dependent areas or the elderly may be normal; well-spaced B lines mean moderate, not severe, loss."},"B13_10":{"clue":"Vertical hyperechoic artifacts with subcutaneous emphysema after chest tube insertion.","mech":"Air in subcutaneous tissue generates E lines that start above the pleural line and obscure deeper structures.","confirm":"Check whether the artifacts arise from the pleural line; true B lines start at the pleura and move with lung sliding.","trap":"Calling them B lines gives false interstitial syndrome and falsely excludes pneumothorax."},"B13_11":{"clue":"High pretest probability (PJP with sudden dyspnea) and LUS showing sliding in a semi-recumbent patient.","mech":"Free air rises to the least dependent area; when not fully supine, the apex rather than the anterior chest is least dependent and may be missed.","confirm":"Scan higher apical regions and obtain CXR or CT if clinical suspicion persists.","trap":"Overtrusting a negative LUS in high-risk patients; sliding excludes pneumothorax only at the area imaged."},"B13_12":{"clue":"Acute respiratory failure and pleuritic pain with normal LUS (A lines and sliding).","mech":"A normal aerated lung with sliding excludes pneumothorax, edema and consolidation, leaving vascular causes such as PE.","confirm":"Perform compression ultrasound of the legs and assess RV size, septal shape and TR velocity.","trap":"LV assessment is the next step for diffuse B lines, not for a normal A-line profile."},"B14_1":{"clue":"End-SBT LUS aeration score of 19.","mech":"Loss of aeration at the end of the SBT reflects derecruitment or edema that will worsen after extubation.","confirm":"Compare with thresholds: over 17 predicts failure, under 13 predicts success; optimize aeration and repeat.","trap":"Assuming passing a 1-hour SBT guarantees success; aeration loss predicts distress despite passing."},"B14_2":{"clue":"Coalescent B lines over more than half of the pleural line in a region.","mech":"Coalescent lines indicate severe interstitial and alveolar involvement with little residual air.","confirm":"Score 0 for A lines or 2 or fewer B lines, 1 for 3 or more spaced B lines, 2 for coalescent lines, 3 for consolidation.","trap":"Scoring it 3, which is reserved for tissue-like consolidation."},"B14_3":{"clue":"Twelve regions each scored 0 to 3.","mech":"Anterior, lateral and posterior areas, each superior and inferior, give 6 regions per hemithorax.","confirm":"Multiply 12 regions by 3 points to give a maximum score of 36.","trap":"Forgetting posterior zones or counting 6 regions total; the global score includes both hemithoraces."},"B14_4":{"clue":"Diaphragm thickness 2.0 mm at end-expiration and 2.3 mm at end-inspiration.","mech":"Thickening of the diaphragm reflects active muscle contraction; low thickening fraction indicates weak inspiratory effort.","confirm":"TF = (2.3 minus 2.0)/2.0 = 15%, below the 20% normal threshold.","trap":"Dividing by inspiratory thickness or interpreting absolute thickness alone; TF depends on the change from baseline."},"B14_5":{"clue":"Right hemidiaphragm excursion 0.8 cm with M-mode.","mech":"Excursion reflects caudal displacement during inspiration; values below 1 cm mean the diaphragm generates little volume.","confirm":"Use low-frequency probe in the subcostal view and measure excursion with M-mode along the movement axis; compare with 1.5-2.5 cm normal.","trap":"Assuming M-mode cannot be used or that 0.8 cm is mild; under 1 cm defines severe dysfunction."},"B14_6":{"clue":"Excursion measured during pressure support reflects both patient and ventilator activity.","mech":"Positive pressure passively displaces the diaphragm, so excursion does not purely represent muscle function.","confirm":"Measure during unassisted breathing (T-piece) or use thickening fraction, which reflects active contraction.","trap":"Thinking excursion correlates poorly with fluoroscopy; it correlates well, but ventilator assistance confounds it."},"B14_7":{"clue":"Lateral E/e' rising from 7 to 14 during an SBT with preserved LVEF.","mech":"Removing positive pressure increases venous return and LV afterload, raising LV filling pressure and causing weaning-induced edema.","confirm":"Repeat E/e', look for new B lines and rising LAP markers; diurese and reassess before the next SBT.","trap":"Labelling it diaphragm or lung failure; the cardiac Doppler change identifies a filling pressure mechanism."},"B14_8":{"clue":"Pseudonormal or restrictive mitral inflow in weaning patients.","mech":"High filling pressure at baseline worsens when intrathoracic pressure falls, precipitating pulmonary edema during weaning.","confirm":"Perform CCE after any weaning or extubation failure, measuring inflow pattern and E/e'.","trap":"Focusing on LVEF; reduced EF is present in fewer than 20% of weaning patients, while diastolic dysfunction is common."},"B14_9":{"clue":"Mitral prosthesis invalidates E/e'.","mech":"The prosthesis alters transmitral flow and annular motion, so E and e' no longer reflect filling pressure and relaxation.","confirm":"Use alternative indices such as pulmonary vein flow, LA size and clinical findings instead.","trap":"Mild tachycardia, mild TR and norepinephrine do not invalidate E/e'; MS, MAC and significant MR do."},"B14_10":{"clue":"Septal hypertrophy with hypotension, new murmur and MR from dynamic LVOT obstruction during SBT.","mech":"Adrenergic surge and tachycardia shrink the LV cavity, causing SAM, obstruction and MR; slowing the rate and increasing afterload reduce it.","confirm":"Use CW Doppler for a late-peaking LVOT gradient and color for posterior MR; give beta-blocker and phenylephrine and reassess gradient.","trap":"Dobutamine, milrinone, nitrates and diuretics reduce volume or afterload or increase contractility and worsen obstruction."},"B14_11":{"clue":"High LUS score with posterior losses but normal e' and E/e' 6.8.","mech":"Normal filling pressure excludes cardiogenic edema; dependent loss with aerated anterior fields indicates derecruitment.","confirm":"Optimize aeration with PEEP, positioning or physiotherapy and repeat LUS score before extubation.","trap":"Calling cardiogenic edema because of impaired relaxation; E/A under 1 with low E/e' means filling pressure is not elevated."},"B14_12":{"clue":"Rising antero-lateral B lines during SBT.","mech":"Nondependent regions lose aeration when pulmonary edema develops; dependent regions may improve from diaphragm contraction.","confirm":"Compare antero-lateral B lines before and during the SBT, alongside E/e' changes.","trap":"Assessing only anterior fields or relying on effusion size; these are not accurate predictors."},"B15_1":{"clue":"Severe LV dysfunction before ECMO for ARDS.","mech":"VV ECMO only oxygenates blood; it does not support circulation, so a failing LV cannot pump the oxygenated blood.","confirm":"Quantify LVEF, VTI and RV function and consider VA ECMO or hybrid configurations if output is insufficient.","trap":"Believing VV ECMO unloads the LV or that LV dysfunction contraindicates ECMO."},"B15_2":{"clue":"TR velocity should be measured before VV ECMO.","mech":"VV ECMO drains venous blood and improves oxygenation, decompressing the RV and lowering pulmonary pressures, altering TR jet.","confirm":"Obtain baseline TR velocity and RVSP before cannulation to compare RV recovery later.","trap":"Assuming TR disappears or cannot be measured with TEE; it changes because of circuit decompression."},"B15_3":{"clue":"TR velocity 3.5 m/s and RA pressure 15 mm Hg without pulmonic stenosis.","mech":"The simplified Bernoulli equation converts TR velocity into the RV-RA pressure gradient.","confirm":"RVSP = 15 + 4 x 3.5 squared = 15 + 49 = 64 mm Hg, which equals PASP without pulmonic stenosis.","trap":"Forgetting to add RAP gives 49 mm Hg; using v rather than v squared gives the wrong number."},"B15_4":{"clue":"Femoral-jugular VV ECMO: drainage tip in the IVC about 10 cm below the RA, return tip just inside the RA.","mech":"Separating drainage from return keeps oxygenated return blood from being immediately re-aspirated, minimising recirculation.","confirm":"Use the TEE bicaval view or TTE subcostal view to locate both tips and check distance and freedom from septum and tricuspid valve.","trap":"Placing tips close together at the cavo-atrial junction maximises recirculation rather than mixing."},"B15_5":{"clue":"Dual-lumen bicaval cannula: drainage ports in SVC and IVC, return jet aimed at the tricuspid valve.","mech":"Directing oxygenated return across the tricuspid valve sends it to the RV and lungs instead of back into the drainage ports.","confirm":"Use TEE bicaval and RV inflow views with color Doppler to confirm the return jet crosses the tricuspid valve.","trap":"Directing return toward the SVC or into the hepatic IVC promotes recirculation or hepatic vein injury."},"B15_6":{"clue":"Imaging guidewires and cannulas in both cavae during cannulation.","mech":"The midesophageal bicaval view shows the SVC, RA and IVC in one plane, aligned with cannula paths.","confirm":"Obtain the ME bicaval view at about 90-110 degrees and follow the wire from SVC through RA into IVC.","trap":"Transgastric and aortic valve views do not display both cavae; with TTE, use subcostal and RV inflow-outflow views."},"B15_7":{"clue":"Persistent hypoxemia on VV ECMO with high flow and good oxygenator, cannula tip in the RV.","mech":"A malpositioned dual-lumen cannula brings the drainage port near the return jet, so oxygenated blood is re-drained rather than delivered to the patient.","confirm":"Inject agitated saline into the return limb and watch bubbles enter the drainage port, and check pre-oxygenator saturation.","trap":"Assuming high native cardiac output; that also causes hypoxemia but the malpositioned tip points to recirculation."},"B15_8":{"clue":"Rhythmic chugging of the drainage line with the IVC collapsing around the cannula.","mech":"Excess negative drainage pressure exceeds venous return, so the IVC walls collapse around the cannula and flow falls.","confirm":"Reduce pump speed, give a fluid bolus while watching the IVC and flow on ultrasound, and reassess cannula position.","trap":"Raising pump speed increases negative pressure and worsens collapse; conversion to VA does not address suck-down."},"B15_9":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, VTI 25 cm, HR 120 with ECMO flow 4 L/min.","mech":"When native output far exceeds ECMO flow, much desaturated venous blood bypasses the oxygenator and dilutes arterial saturation.","confirm":"SV = 3.14 x 1.0 squared x 25 = 78.5 mL; CO = 78.5 x 120 = about 9.4 L/min; consider esmolol to reduce CO.","trap":"Dobutamine increases CO and worsens shunt; sweep gas affects CO2 removal, not oxygenation fraction."},"B15_10":{"clue":"Severe aortic insufficiency before VA ECMO.","mech":"Retrograde aortic flow from VA ECMO increases afterload and worsens AR, causing LV distension and pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Grade AR before cannulation with color, vena contracta and pressure half-time; consider alternative support if severe.","trap":"PE with RV failure and post-MI cardiogenic shock are indications, not contraindications."},"B15_11":{"clue":"On VA ECMO: AV no longer opens, dense LA smoke and a dilating LV.","mech":"Retrograde arterial flow raises LV afterload; if the LV cannot eject, blood stagnates and pressures rise, risking thrombus and edema.","confirm":"Lower ECMO flow under echo until the AV opens; if not possible, vent the LV with IABP, Impella or surgical vent.","trap":"Increasing flow further raises afterload and worsens LV distension; this is not an expected benign finding."},"B15_12":{"clue":"LVEF 30%, LVOT VTI 12 cm and mitral S' 7 cm/s during turndown.","mech":"These values show the LV can generate adequate forward stroke volume when ECMO support is reduced.","confirm":"Verify predictors: LVEF over 25%, VTI over 10 cm, S' over 6 cm/s, no LV dilation and no tamponade.","trap":"Progressive LV dilation, low VTI or a new effusion indicate weaning failure even if EF appears moderate."},"B15_13":{"clue":"RV must be assessed before decannulation from VA ECMO.","mech":"ECMO drains the right heart and offloads the RV, so RV failure may only appear when support is removed.","confirm":"At each turndown step, measure RV size, TAPSE, S', PASP and tricuspid valve function.","trap":"Assuming RV performance does not change or that TR velocity is accurate at full flow; low flow can still mask dysfunction."},"B15_14":{"clue":"Femoral VA ECMO: drainage tip at IVC-RA junction, return in iliac artery or distal aorta.","mech":"Drainage at the junction maximises venous capture; the short femoral return cannula ends in the iliac or distal aorta, delivering retrograde flow.","confirm":"Use TEE bicaval or TTE subcostal views for the drainage tip and visualise the guidewire in the aorta before dilating.","trap":"Expecting the return cannula tip in the thoracic aorta or ascending aorta; femoral return cannulas are short."},"B16_1":{"clue":"SAH with diffuse B lines, preserved sliding, reduced LV function and normal RV.","mech":"Catecholamine surge after SAH injures myocardium, producing LV dysfunction and cardiogenic pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Look for regional wall motion abnormalities beyond a single coronary territory, raised troponin and E/e', and follow recovery on serial echo.","trap":"Lung sliding excludes pneumothorax and a normal RV argues against massive PE despite the new central line."},"B16_2":{"clue":"Temporal window: phased-array 1-3 MHz above the zygoma, marker toward the eyes.","mech":"Low-frequency sound penetrates the thin temporal squama, and orienting along the orbitomeatal line aligns with the basal cisterns.","confirm":"Place the probe anterior and superior to the tragus and adjust until the butterfly-shaped midbrain appears.","trap":"High-frequency linear probes are for ocular imaging, and occipital or mastoid placement does not give the axial basal cistern plane."},"B16_3":{"clue":"Hypoechoic butterfly shape in the basal cistern plane.","mech":"The cerebral peduncles of the midbrain are hypoechoic and surrounded by echogenic cisterns, forming a butterfly.","confirm":"Tilt slightly upward to see the third ventricle as two parallel hyperechoic lines anterior to it.","trap":"The third ventricle is seen as hyperechoic lines, not a butterfly; the pineal is a bright posterior dot."},"B16_4":{"clue":"Third ventricle distance 76 mm from the right and 64 mm from the left.","mech":"With midline shift, the third ventricle moves away from one side and closer to the other; half the difference gives the shift.","confirm":"Midline shift = (76 minus 64)/2 = 6 mm, toward the left (shorter distance side).","trap":"Reporting the raw difference of 12 mm; also, no shift does not exclude raised ICP."},"B16_5":{"clue":"Sharp systolic upstroke with diastolic velocity less than one quarter of peak systolic velocity, no reversal.","mech":"Rising ICP increases distal resistance, reducing diastolic flow and increasing pulsatility.","confirm":"Calculate pulsatility index (PSV minus EDV)/mean velocity and trend it; correlate with ICP and CT.","trap":"Calling it normal; normal diastolic flow is one third to one half of systolic, and reversal indicates ICP above diastolic BP."},"B16_6":{"clue":"Reproducible diastolic reversal in all major intracranial arteries.","mech":"ICP above diastolic BP prevents forward diastolic flow, producing oscillating flow, a sign of irreversible circulatory arrest.","confirm":"Insonate all anterior and posterior circulation vessels, repeat after 30 minutes and integrate with clinical brain death testing.","trap":"Calling it vasospasm or filter artifact; a high wall filter removes low velocities but does not create reversal."},"B16_7":{"clue":"Nonpulsatile forward MCA flow with mean velocity about 60 cm/s on VA ECMO.","mech":"Continuous ECMO flow removes pulsatility, so continuous forward flow is expected and indicates intact cerebral perfusion.","confirm":"Confirm forward flow direction and velocity in both MCAs; clinical examination is confounded by paralysis.","trap":"Interpreting loss of pulsatility as circulatory arrest; on ECMO, absence of forward flow, not pulsatility, defines arrest."},"B16_8":{"clue":"Absent MCA flow without a prior study documenting a window.","mech":"Around 10% of people lack an adequate temporal window, so absent signal may reflect bone, not absent flow.","confirm":"Document windows on an earlier study or find flow in other vessels; insonate all anterior and posterior vessels per protocol.","trap":"Assuming absent flow equals circulatory arrest; it is only valid when flow was previously insonated."},"B16_9":{"clue":"MCA mean velocity 201 cm/s with aliasing on day 7 after SAH.","mech":"Velocity rises when the vessel narrows (vasospasm or stenosis) or when flow increases (hyperemia).","confirm":"Calculate the Lindegaard ratio (MCA/ICA mean velocity) to separate vasospasm from hyperemia and correlate clinically.","trap":"Treating velocity alone as proof of vasospasm; hyperemia also raises velocity without narrowing."},"B16_10":{"clue":"Suspected low-flow state on TCD.","mech":"High scale and wall filters reject low-frequency shifts, so low velocities are filtered out or not displayed.","confirm":"Lower PRF and wall filter, increase gain, and use a 5 mm gate on the proximal MCA.","trap":"Raising the wall filter or enlarging the gate degrades sensitivity, and linear probes cannot penetrate the skull."},"B16_11":{"clue":"Normal MCA waveforms, velocities and pulsatility index in a patient without invasive ICP monitoring.","mech":"Normal waveforms indicate adequate perfusion pressure across the cerebral bed, so significant CPP compromise is unlikely.","confirm":"Repeat TCD serially and correlate with neurological examination and CT when feasible.","trap":"TCD does not assess brainstem function or midline shift; it cannot exclude brain death or structural shift."},"B16_12":{"clue":"TCD shows flow patterns but does not assess brainstem function.","mech":"TCD measures intracranial arterial flow and can show circulatory arrest, but not neurological function.","confirm":"Complete clinical brain death examination and apnea test, then use protocolized insonation of all vessels as an ancillary test.","trap":"Assuming TCD replaces clinical testing or that bilateral MCA insonation alone confirms brain death."},"B17_1":{"clue":"The most sensitive RUQ site for free fluid is the caudal tip of the liver.","mech":"Free fluid tracks along the inferior liver edge toward the right paracolic gutter, often before filling Morison's pouch.","confirm":"Image all three RUQ areas: subdiaphragmatic space, hepatorenal recess and caudal tip of the liver.","trap":"Focusing only on Morison's pouch misses fluid at the liver tip."},"B17_2":{"clue":"Hypotensive trauma patient with an initially negative FAST and no other bleeding source.","mech":"FAST sensitivity is limited; small or early bleeding may be missed, so repeat examination increases detection.","confirm":"Repeat FAST; if two scans are negative but suspicion persists, proceed to DPL or exploratory laparotomy.","trap":"Accepting a single negative FAST, or sending an unstable patient to CT."},"B17_3":{"clue":"Cochrane pooled FAST accuracy: sensitivity 68% and specificity 95%.","mech":"FAST detects free fluid, not organ injury, so injuries without significant hemoperitoneum are missed, lowering sensitivity.","confirm":"Use a positive FAST to proceed but follow a negative FAST with CT or repeat scans.","trap":"Mixing with lung ultrasound pneumothorax data (81/98) or older single-centre studies (78.6/100)."},"B17_4":{"clue":"Fewer than 10% of operators detected less than 400 mL of fluid.","mech":"Free fluid must accumulate before it appears in dependent spaces; average RUQ threshold was about 619 mL.","confirm":"Use Trendelenburg to lower the RUQ threshold and image the pelvis, which detects smaller volumes.","trap":"Assuming small volumes such as 50 mL are reliably detected in the RUQ."},"B17_5":{"clue":"US detects as little as 20 mL of pleural fluid versus about 175 mL on radiograph.","mech":"Ultrasound directly visualises anechoic fluid in dependent recesses, whereas radiographs need enough fluid to blunt costophrenic angles.","confirm":"Image the costophrenic recess above the diaphragm and look for anechoic fluid and the spine sign.","trap":"Supine radiographs need even more fluid, so a normal CXR does not exclude hemothorax."},"B17_6":{"clue":"Thoracic spine visible above the diaphragm (spine sign).","mech":"Aerated lung normally scatters the beam; pleural fluid transmits sound and reveals the spine above the diaphragm.","confirm":"Look for anechoic or echogenic fluid above the diaphragm and quantify; correlate with hemoglobin trend.","trap":"Calling it mirror artifact; normal lung produces a mirror image of the liver, not a visible spine."},"B17_7":{"clue":"Absent sliding but visible B lines from the pleural line.","mech":"B lines arise from the visceral pleura; air between pleural layers prevents their formation.","confirm":"Any B line rules out pneumothorax at that point; confirm by scanning other sites.","trap":"Equating absent sliding with pneumothorax; B lines or lung pulse exclude it locally."},"B17_8":{"clue":"Absent sliding over the whole left hemithorax after field intubation with absent breath sounds.","mech":"Right mainstem intubation prevents ventilation of the left lung, abolishing sliding without pneumothorax.","confirm":"Look for lung pulse, check tube depth and withdraw the tube if necessary, then reassess sliding.","trap":"Placing a chest tube without excluding mainstem intubation; absent sliding is not specific for pneumothorax."},"B17_9":{"clue":"Large left hemothorax with negative subxiphoid view after a stab wound.","mech":"A pericardial laceration can drain blood into the pleural cavity, preventing pericardial accumulation.","confirm":"Pursue further evaluation such as repeat imaging, pericardial window or surgical exploration.","trap":"Trusting a negative pericardial view despite high sensitivity; hemothorax can mask cardiac injury."},"B17_10":{"clue":"Parasternal third to fifth interspaces in a supine patient.","mech":"Air rises to the highest point of the supine chest, around the anterior lower chest near the fifth or sixth rib.","confirm":"Scan parasternal interspaces 3 to 5 and lateral regions; scanning multiple interspaces improves sensitivity.","trap":"Focusing on apical or lateral axillary interspaces misses small anterior pneumothoraces."},"B17_11":{"clue":"LUQ free fluid most commonly between diaphragm and spleen.","mech":"Fluid collects in the subphrenic space before the splenorenal recess because of the phrenicocolic ligament.","confirm":"Start at the posterior axillary line, angle obliquely, and image the subdiaphragmatic space and splenorenal recess.","trap":"Focusing only on the splenorenal recess misses fluid above the spleen."},"B17_12":{"clue":"Trauma arrest with no cardiac motion on subxiphoid ultrasound.","mech":"Absent cardiac motion indicates no meaningful cardiac activity, so resuscitative thoracotomy rarely succeeds.","confirm":"Confirm absence of motion in an adequate subxiphoid or parasternal view and consider stopping resuscitation.","trap":"Assuming draining tamponade changes outcome; mortality was 100% with no motion."},"B18_1":{"clue":"16 segments: 8 normal, 4 hypokinetic and 4 akinetic.","mech":"WMSI averages segmental scores; higher values reflect larger infarct size and greater dysfunction.","confirm":"WMSI = (8 x 1 + 4 x 2 + 4 x 3)/16 = 28/16 = 1.75, above the 1.7 high-risk threshold.","trap":"Dividing by 17 segments or miscounting scores; only visualised segments are counted."},"B18_2":{"clue":"Basal inferoseptal, mid inferior and apical inferior hypokinesis.","mech":"The RCA usually supplies inferior and inferoseptal segments via the posterior descending artery.","confirm":"Map abnormalities on the 17-segment model and look for RV involvement suggesting proximal RCA occlusion.","trap":"The LAD supplies anterior and septal segments; the LCx supplies lateral segments."},"B18_3":{"clue":"Flail leaflet with a mobile mass moving between LV and LA days after small inferior MI.","mech":"The posteromedial papillary muscle has single blood supply from the posterior descending artery, making it vulnerable to infarction and rupture.","confirm":"Use TEE to identify the ruptured head and chordae and quantify MR; arrange urgent surgery.","trap":"The anterolateral papillary muscle has dual LAD and LCx supply and ruptures less often."},"B18_4":{"clue":"High-velocity LV to RV jet in the apical septum with hypercontractile noninfarcted LV.","mech":"Infarcted septal myocardium ruptures, creating a left-to-right shunt and pulmonary overflow.","confirm":"Scan the entire septum from base to apex with color Doppler and measure shunt ratio Qp/Qs.","trap":"Papillary muscle rupture causes MR into the LA, not a ventricular jet; LVOT obstruction produces late-peaking gradients."},"B18_5":{"clue":"Effusion with intrapericardial echogenic material and tamponade after anterior STEMI.","mech":"Subacute free wall rupture leaks blood into the pericardium, forming thrombus and tamponade.","confirm":"Look for hemopericardium, thrombus and a small slit-like defect; arrange urgent surgery.","trap":"Post-MI pericarditis effusions are small and rarely cause tamponade."},"B18_6":{"clue":"Apical infarct with basal hyperkinesis, SAM and a new murmur while dobutamine is started.","mech":"Hyperkinetic basal segments narrow the LVOT and drag the mitral leaflet into it; beta-1 stimulation increases contractility and worsens obstruction.","confirm":"Use CW Doppler for a late-peaking LVOT gradient and color for posterior MR; stop inotropes, give volume and a pure vasoconstrictor, and recheck the gradient.","trap":"Assuming pump failure because of the infarct; inotropes worsen obstruction, and SAM-related MR is not papillary muscle rupture."},"B18_7":{"clue":"Inferior STEMI with hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs, severe hypoxemia and interatrial septum bowing into the LA.","mech":"RV infarction raises RA pressure above LA pressure, opening a PFO and shunting deoxygenated blood right to left.","confirm":"Inject agitated saline and look for bubbles crossing into the left heart within three beats; measure TAPSE and IVC size.","trap":"Attributing hypoxemia to LV failure or PE; clear lungs and RA-LA septal bowing point to shunting."},"B18_8":{"clue":"Post-MI outpouching with a narrow neck and thrombus.","mech":"Incomplete free wall rupture is contained by pericardium and organized hematoma, forming a narrow-necked false aneurysm prone to rupture.","confirm":"Measure neck-to-body diameter ratio (narrow neck, ratio under about 0.5), look for abrupt myocardial discontinuity and flow across the neck with color Doppler.","trap":"True aneurysms also contain thrombus and are dyskinetic, but they have a wide neck and walls of thinned myocardium."},"B18_9":{"clue":"Takotsubo requires exclusion of an acute culprit coronary lesion.","mech":"Catecholamine surge produces regional dysfunction beyond a single coronary territory that echo cannot reliably separate from ACS.","confirm":"Perform coronary angiography and follow recovery of wall motion on serial echo over days to weeks.","trap":"It represents 1-3% of suspected STEMI, apical ballooning is most common, and coexisting CAD does not exclude it."},"B18_10":{"clue":"Ischemic cascade timing: wall motion abnormality before ECG changes and pain.","mech":"Ischemia impairs perfusion, metabolism, then diastolic and systolic function before electrical changes and symptoms appear.","confirm":"Look for new regional wall motion abnormalities in the acute setting even when ECG is nondiagnostic.","trap":"Assuming ECG changes or chest pain occur first; necrosis is the last step."},"B18_11":{"clue":"Dilated RV with preserved RV apical motion and a hyperdynamic LV.","mech":"Acute afterload rise in PE strains the RV free wall but the apex is pulled by the hyperdynamic LV; LV underfilling reflects reduced RV output.","confirm":"Check TR velocity (usually PASP under 60 mm Hg), IVC plethora and DVT on compression ultrasound.","trap":"Inferior hypokinesis with RV dilation suggests RV infarction from RCA occlusion, not PE."},"B18_12":{"clue":"Thinned segment moving outward in systole.","mech":"Scarred, noncontractile myocardium is pushed outward by LV systolic pressure, defining dyskinesis.","confirm":"Score 4 in ASE 2015: 1 normal or hyperkinetic, 2 hypokinetic, 3 akinetic, 4 dyskinetic or aneurysmal.","trap":"Scoring as akinesis (3), which is absent thickening without outward motion; older schemes used 5 for aneurysm."},"B19_1":{"clue":"Color scale of 15 cm/s producing mosaic flow across normally opening leaflets.","mech":"With a low Nyquist limit, normal inflow velocities exceed the limit and alias, mimicking turbulence.","confirm":"Reset Nyquist to 50-70 cm/s and reassess; use CW Doppler and leaflet motion to evaluate stenosis.","trap":"Calling stenosis or regurgitation from aliasing at low scale; normal opening and laminar flow at the correct scale exclude obstruction."},"B19_2":{"clue":"Systolic flow reversal in more than one pulmonary vein.","mech":"Severe MR raises LA pressure during systole above pulmonary venous pressure, reversing flow in multiple veins.","confirm":"Sample PW Doppler 1 cm into at least two pulmonary veins, ideally with TEE, and look for systolic reversal.","trap":"Systolic blunting is moderate and nonspecific; single-vein reversal from an eccentric jet can occur with less than severe MR."},"B19_3":{"clue":"Peak aortic velocity 4.5 m/s.","mech":"The simplified Bernoulli equation converts velocity to pressure gradient when proximal velocity is negligible.","confirm":"Gradient = 4 x 4.5 squared = 4 x 20.25 = 81 mm Hg; align CW beam coaxially from multiple windows.","trap":"Forgetting to square the velocity; off-axis alignment underestimates the gradient."},"B19_4":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, LVOT VTI 20 cm and AV VTI 80 cm.","mech":"The continuity equation assumes flow through the LVOT equals flow through the aortic valve.","confirm":"CSA LVOT = 0.785 x 2.0 squared = 3.14 cm2; AVA = (3.14 x 20)/80 = 0.8 cm2, severe AS.","trap":"Using radius instead of diameter in the formula or sampling LVOT VTI at a different level from diameter measurement."},"B19_5":{"clue":"LVOT diameter in mid-systole, inner edge to inner edge, within 2-5 mm of hinge points.","mech":"The LVOT is largest and most circular in mid-systole near the annulus; diameter errors are squared in area.","confirm":"Measure in zoomed PLAX or ME long-axis view, parallel to the valve plane, in mid-systole.","trap":"Measuring in diastole, at the sinotubular junction or leading edge to leading edge introduces squared errors."},"B19_6":{"clue":"Peak velocity 4.2 m/s in a septic, hyperdynamic patient but DI of 0.30.","mech":"High flow inflates velocity and gradient across a moderately narrowed valve; DI corrects for flow because both VTIs rise.","confirm":"DI = LVOT VTI/AV VTI = 30/100 = 0.30, above 0.25 so not severe; reassess when flow normalises.","trap":"Grading AS by velocity or gradient alone; these are flow-dependent and overestimate severity in high-output states."},"B19_7":{"clue":"Anterior leaflet contacting the septum, posterior MR jet and late-peaking dagger LVOT signal after MV repair.","mech":"An underfilled, hyperdynamic LV with tachycardia drags the anterior leaflet into the LVOT, causing late systolic obstruction and MR.","confirm":"Measure LVOT gradient with CW Doppler and evaluate SAM on TEE; give volume, stop inotropes and add a vasoconstrictor or beta-blocker.","trap":"AS gives a rounded, earlier-peaking profile; a subaortic membrane is fixed; papillary rupture gives a flail leaflet, not SAM."},"B19_8":{"clue":"LVEF 20% with calcified AV, peak velocity 3.2 m/s, mean gradient 24 mm Hg, AVA 0.8 cm2.","mech":"Low flow reduces velocity and gradient despite a small valve area, producing low-flow, low-gradient AS.","confirm":"Calculate DI and consider low-dose dobutamine stress to distinguish true severe from pseudo-severe AS.","trap":"Grading as moderate from gradient; the continuity area is relatively flow-independent and remains valid."},"B19_9":{"clue":"Mitral pressure half-time 275 ms in rheumatic MS.","mech":"A narrower orifice prolongs the time for the LA-LV gradient to fall by half.","confirm":"MVA = 220/PHT = 220/275 = 0.8 cm2, severe (under 1.0 cm2); measure PHT on the mid E-wave slope.","trap":"Using the steep early part of the slope or applying PHT outside rheumatic MS."},"B19_10":{"clue":"Heavy annular calcification extending into the leaflets with a high transmitral gradient.","mech":"Calcific MS narrows the annulus and base rather than commissures, and PHT reflects chamber compliance more than orifice size.","confirm":"Use mean gradient, continuity equation or planimetry with 3D TEE when feasible.","trap":"Applying the 220/PHT formula, validated only in rheumatic MS and confounded by LV and LA compliance."},"B19_11":{"clue":"Rheumatic MS with AF at 130/min and mean gradient 14 mm Hg.","mech":"Tachycardia shortens diastole and increases flow, raising the gradient independent of valve area.","confirm":"Average the mean gradient over 5 beats and recheck after rate control; correlate with planimetry or PHT.","trap":"Assuming gradients are rate-independent or measuring only the longest RR interval."},"B19_12":{"clue":"Thickened, restricted tricuspid leaflets with a dilated IVC and RA in carcinoid disease.","mech":"Low-pressure right-sided inflow means even small gradients indicate significant stenosis.","confirm":"Measure mean gradient over 5 beats; at least 5 mm Hg, inflow VTI over 60 cm and PHT at least 190 ms support severe TS.","trap":"Using left-sided thresholds or the 1.5 cm2 area; severe TS is 1 cm2 or less, and coexisting TR increases the gradient."},"B19_13":{"clue":"RVSP 90 mm Hg with RVOT or pulmonary valve peak velocity 4.3 m/s.","mech":"A pulmonary valve gradient means RV pressure exceeds PA pressure, so RVSP overestimates PASP.","confirm":"Gradient = 4 x 4.3 squared = about 74 mm Hg, severe PS; PASP = RVSP minus PS gradient.","trap":"Attributing high RVSP to pulmonary hypertension without interrogating the RVOT."},"B19_14":{"clue":"Flail leaflet with an eccentric, wall-hugging jet occupying only about 20% of the LA.","mech":"Coanda effect makes eccentric jets cling to the wall and lose apparent area, underestimating severity.","confirm":"Measure vena contracta and PISA EROA, and check pulmonary vein flow reversal.","trap":"Grading by jet area or adjusting gain or Nyquist to fill the atrium; a flail leaflet itself is specific for severe MR."},"B19_15":{"clue":"PISA radius 1.0 cm, aliasing velocity 40 cm/s, MR peak velocity 500 cm/s, MR VTI 150 cm.","mech":"Flow through the hemispheric convergence zone equals flow through the regurgitant orifice.","confirm":"Flow = 2 x 3.14 x 1 squared x 40 = 251 mL/s; EROA = 251/500 = 0.50 cm2; RVol = 0.50 x 150 = 75 mL, severe.","trap":"Forgetting to square the radius or using mean rather than peak MR velocity."},"B19_16":{"clue":"Short AR PHT in septic shock with low SVR.","mech":"Low aortic diastolic pressure and high LV diastolic pressure equalise faster, shortening PHT independent of AR severity.","confirm":"Use vena contracta, jet width ratio and abdominal aortic holodiastolic reversal rather than PHT.","trap":"Assuming short PHT always means severe AR; it is load-dependent."},"B19_17":{"clue":"Holodiastolic reversal in the abdominal aorta.","mech":"Large regurgitant volume reverses diastolic flow even far from the valve, which is specific for severe AR.","confirm":"Sample PW Doppler in the proximal abdominal aorta and look for end-diastolic reversal velocity.","trap":"Brief early-diastolic reversal in the descending aorta is normal and can occur in stiff aortas."},"B19_18":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.2 cm, VTI 30 cm; mitral annulus 3.0 cm, VTI 7 cm.","mech":"LVOT flow includes forward plus regurgitant volume; mitral inflow equals forward flow without significant MR.","confirm":"SV LVOT = 0.785 x 2.2 squared x 30 = 114 mL; SV mitral = 3.14 x 1.5 squared x 7 = 50 mL; RVol = 64 mL, RF = 57%.","trap":"Using radius incorrectly or applying the method with significant MR."},"B19_19":{"clue":"Flail P2 segment of the posterior mitral leaflet.","mech":"With prolapse or flail, the regurgitant jet is directed away from the affected leaflet.","confirm":"Use TEE color Doppler to follow jet direction and quantify with PISA or vena contracta.","trap":"Expecting the jet toward the posterior leaflet; that happens with tethering, not flail."},"B19_20":{"clue":"Severe TR, annulus 42 mm, dilated IVC and diastolic-only septal flattening.","mech":"Severe TR causes RV volume overload, flattening the septum in diastole but not systole.","confirm":"Look for systolic hepatic vein reversal and measure vena contracta and EROA for TR severity.","trap":"Calling pressure overload, which flattens the septum throughout systole and diastole."},"B20_1":{"clue":"Persistent S. aureus bacteremia with prosthetic valve and dialysis catheter despite negative TTE.","mech":"TTE has limited resolution for prosthetic valves and devices because of acoustic shadowing.","confirm":"Perform TEE to look for vegetations, abscess or prosthetic dehiscence.","trap":"Accepting negative TTE as exclusion; sensitivity for prosthetic or device IE is only about 40-70%."},"B20_2":{"clue":"Tricuspid vegetation on TEE plus fever, IV drug use and Janeway lesions.","mech":"Vegetation is a major criterion; fever, predisposition and vascular phenomena are three minor criteria.","confirm":"One major plus three minor criteria equals definite IE by modified Duke criteria.","trap":"Waiting for blood cultures; echo evidence counts as a major criterion even before cultures return."},"B20_3":{"clue":"TTE sensitivity only about 40-70% for prosthetic or device IE.","mech":"Prosthetic materials shadow and reverberate, hiding vegetations and abscesses on TTE.","confirm":"Use TEE when TTE is negative and suspicion is high, especially with prosthetic valves or devices.","trap":"TTE specificity for native IE is about 90%, not 50%; TEE is less sensitive for prosthetic than native valves."},"B20_4":{"clue":"Repeat TEE in 3 to 7 days if suspicion persists.","mech":"Small vegetations may enlarge and become visible over days, and TEE is not 100% sensitive.","confirm":"Repeat TEE after 3-7 days, or immediately if clinical deterioration suggests complications.","trap":"Assuming TEE is perfectly sensitive or waiting until completing antibiotics."},"B20_5":{"clue":"12 mm mobile vegetation on the anterior mitral leaflet.","mech":"Large, mobile left-sided vegetations carry higher embolic risk, especially on the anterior mitral leaflet.","confirm":"Measure vegetation length and mobility on TEE and assess valve function, abscess and emboli by imaging.","trap":"Assuming surgery requires a documented embolus or size above 20 mm; at least 10 mm is already a high-risk feature."},"B20_6":{"clue":"Stalked, frond-like mass on the aortic side of the aortic valve with normal valve function and negative cultures.","mech":"Papillary fibroelastomas are benign tumours on the downstream side of valves, prone to embolism.","confirm":"Use TEE to define stalk and location; consider surgical excision if mobile or embolic.","trap":"Vegetations sit on the upstream side and damage the valve; Lambl's excrescences are thin filiform strands."},"B20_7":{"clue":"Verrucous lesions at the mitral leaflet tips in SLE with antiphospholipid syndrome and negative cultures.","mech":"Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis forms sterile platelet-fibrin deposits on valves in autoimmune disease.","confirm":"Check antiphospholipid antibodies, exclude infection with cultures and use TEE to assess lesion size.","trap":"Marantic endocarditis is associated with malignancy; infective vegetations usually cause valve damage."},"B20_8":{"clue":"Vegetations on the ventricular (upstream) side of aortic cusps in the path of the regurgitant jet.","mech":"Vegetations form in low-pressure zones adjacent to high-velocity jets on the upstream surface of regurgitant valves.","confirm":"Use TEE long-axis and short-axis views to locate vegetations and look for perforation or abscess.","trap":"Papillary fibroelastomas sit on the downstream (aortic) side; vegetations favour the upstream side."},"B20_9":{"clue":"New heart block with heterogeneous thickening of the aortic annulus and intervalvular fibrosa on TEE.","mech":"Infection extends from the valve into the periannular tissue near the conduction system, forming an abscess that disrupts AV conduction.","confirm":"Image the annulus in multiple TEE planes for echolucent or heterogeneous perivalvular areas and arrange early surgery.","trap":"Calling it post-inflammatory thickening; new heart block in aortic IE is an abscess until proven otherwise, and TTE often misses it."},"B20_10":{"clue":"Echolucent perivalvular cavity that expands in systole with color flow into and out of it.","mech":"An abscess that ruptures into the lumen becomes a pulsatile pseudoaneurysm communicating with the LVOT or aorta.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show systolic inflow and diastolic outflow and look for fistula into adjacent chambers.","trap":"A closed abscess has no flow communication; a pericardial collection or coronary sinus does not expand with systolic flow."},"B20_11":{"clue":"Rocking mechanical mitral prosthesis with severe periprosthetic regurgitation and bacteremia.","mech":"Mechanical valve infection targets the sewing ring-tissue junction, causing abscess and suture dehiscence that lets the ring move.","confirm":"Use TEE with color Doppler to locate the paravalvular jet and extent of dehiscence; refer for surgery.","trap":"Leaflet destruction is typical of bioprostheses; pannus is fixed and immobile; new dehiscence is a major Duke criterion."},"B20_12":{"clue":"Pacemaker with bacteremia and negative TTE.","mech":"TTE cannot reliably image leads in the SVC and RA because of shadowing, and lead vegetations resemble thrombus.","confirm":"Perform TEE imaging the SVC, RA and RV lead courses; repeat TEE or use PET-CT if suspicion persists.","trap":"Assuming oscillation distinguishes vegetation from thrombus or labelling the Eustachian valve as a vegetation."},"B21_1":{"clue":"End-diastolic RV:LV area ratio over 0.6 with systemic congestion in ARDS.","mech":"High PVR in ARDS dilates the RV; when dilation causes venous congestion, the RV is failing.","confirm":"Measure end-diastolic RV and LV areas in a TEE or apical 4-chamber view and look for raised CVP, IVC plethora and hepatic congestion.","trap":"Using end-systolic ratios or TAPSE alone; TAPSE marks dysfunction but is not the dilation criterion."},"B21_2":{"clue":"Shock in ARDS: sepsis accounts for about half of cases.","mech":"Pneumonia causes both ARDS and sepsis, so vasodilatory circulatory failure is the most frequent mechanism.","confirm":"Use echo to separate vasodilatory shock from acute cor pulmonale and LV dysfunction.","trap":"Assuming acute cor pulmonale dominates; it is the main mechanism only in unstable patients without sepsis."},"B21_3":{"clue":"PPV 16% with a dilated RV enlarging during inspiration, dilated IVC and no SVC variation.","mech":"Positive pressure raises RV afterload in inspiration, reducing RV output and causing PPV without preload dependence.","confirm":"Check RV size and septal motion, absence of SVC collapse and high CVP; adjust ventilation, control CO2 and consider prone positioning.","trap":"Giving fluid for high PPV; in acute cor pulmonale PPV is a false positive and fluid worsens RV failure."},"B21_4":{"clue":"Delta SVC on TEE is much more specific than sensitive.","mech":"The SVC is intrathoracic, so its collapse reflects transmitted pleural pressure on a compliant, underfilled vein.","confirm":"Measure SVC diameter in the upper esophageal view with M-mode and calculate (Dmax minus Dmin)/Dmax.","trap":"Thinking it needs high tidal volumes or a subcostal window; most patients studied had tidal volumes under 8 mL/kg."},"B21_5":{"clue":"End-systolic paradoxical septal motion with a D-shaped LV.","mech":"RV systolic pressure overload prolongs RV ejection past LV ejection, pushing the septum leftward at end-systole.","confirm":"Use short-axis view with M-mode or frame-by-frame to time septal flattening at end-systole.","trap":"Volume overload causes diastolic septal shift, not end-systolic paradox."},"B21_6":{"clue":"Driving pressure 18 cm H2O or higher is a risk factor for ACP in ARDS.","mech":"High driving pressure compresses alveolar vessels and raises PVR, increasing RV afterload.","confirm":"Check plateau minus PEEP and other risk factors: pneumonia, PaO2/FiO2 under 150 and PaCO2 at least 48.","trap":"The relevant PaO2/FiO2 threshold is under 150, not 250, and PEEP or tidal volume alone are not in the score."},"B21_7":{"clue":"Thermodilution devices are inaccurate on ECMO.","mech":"The circuit removes and returns blood, diverting the indicator and distorting thermodilution curves.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI and diameter with PW Doppler to estimate SV and CO serially.","trap":"Assuming pulse contour is contraindicated or that TTE cannot obtain VTI; the issue is indicator loss."},"B21_8":{"clue":"ARDS with pulmonary hypertension, dilated RV, normal LVEF and moderate vasopressors.","mech":"VV ECMO improves oxygenation and CO2 removal, lowering PVR and unloading the RV.","confirm":"Reassess RV size and PASP after VV initiation; conversion to VA is rarely needed.","trap":"Assuming pulmonary hypertension or any vasopressor need mandates VA ECMO; ECCO2R does not fix hypoxemia."},"B21_9":{"clue":"Severe cardiogenic shock with very low CO and severely reduced LVEF on high-dose support.","mech":"VV ECMO cannot support the circulation; profound LV failure needs arterial flow support.","confirm":"Measure LVEF, VTI and cardiac output and assess vasopressor and inotrope needs before choosing VA.","trap":"RV failure from pulmonary hypertension and hypercapnia are usually relieved by VV ECMO."},"B21_10":{"clue":"Low cardiac index decreases intrapulmonary shunt.","mech":"Lower flow reduces perfusion of non-ventilated lung, reducing shunt fraction and making oxygenation look better.","confirm":"Interpret PaO2/FiO2 alongside CO, SvO2 and PFO status.","trap":"Low venous oxygen and PFO shunting worsen oxygenation and overestimate severity."},"B22_1":{"clue":"Large tissue-like echotexture resembling liver.","mech":"Alveoli filled with exudate transmit sound like solid tissue, producing hepatization in lobar or translobar consolidation.","confirm":"Look for air bronchograms and absence of a shred sign; correlate with clinical infection markers.","trap":"Nontranslobar consolidation shows a shred sign; atelectasis and interstitial syndrome do not look liver-like."},"B22_2":{"clue":"Dynamic air bronchograms in a consolidation.","mech":"Air moving within patent bronchi during breathing shows the airway is open, favouring pneumonia over resorptive atelectasis.","confirm":"Observe air bronchograms moving with respiration and look for a lung pulse.","trap":"Static air bronchograms occur in both pneumonia and atelectasis; effusion is nonspecific."},"B22_3":{"clue":"Bilateral B lines with spared areas and subpleural consolidations.","mech":"ARDS causes heterogeneous inflammatory injury, sparing some areas while damaging others and thickening the pleura.","confirm":"Look for pleural thickening over 2 mm, reduced sliding, lung pulse and normal or near-normal filling pressure.","trap":"Homogeneous laser-like B lines, thin regular pleura and bilateral effusions favour cardiogenic edema."},"B22_4":{"clue":"Diffuse B lines with LVEF 60% and average E/e' 16.","mech":"Elevated E/e' reflects raised LV filling pressure, driving hydrostatic pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Average E/e' over 14 indicates elevated LVFP; check LA volume and TR velocity for corroboration.","trap":"Assuming E/e' is invalid with normal EF; it is specifically needed in preserved EF."},"B22_5":{"clue":"B lines in 4 zones, left effusion, normal LV and IVC 18 mm.","mech":"The score weights findings favouring hydrostatic edema and subtracts for a small IVC that argues against congestion.","confirm":"Score = 3 + 4 + 0 minus 2 = 5, between the ARDS cutoff (3 or less) and CPE cutoff (6 or more).","trap":"Forgetting the IVC subtraction or scoring normal LV as positive."},"B22_6":{"clue":"Score at least 6: sensitivity 0.30, specificity 0.98.","mech":"High specificity means few false positives, so a positive score reliably rules in CPE.","confirm":"Use a score of 6 or higher to rule in CPE and a score of 3 or less to suggest ARDS.","trap":"Using it to rule out CPE; low sensitivity means many CPE patients score below 6."},"B22_7":{"clue":"Kigali modification allows lung ultrasound as the imaging criterion.","mech":"In resource-limited settings, LUS can document bilateral loss of aeration without CXR or CT.","confirm":"Apply LUS findings of bilateral B lines and consolidations with oxygenation criteria per Kigali.","trap":"Berlin requires CXR or CT; Montreux is the neonatal definition without LUS."},"B22_8":{"clue":"Homogeneous bilateral B lines with irregularly thickened pleura without ARDS history.","mech":"Pulmonary fibrosis thickens interlobular septa and pleura, producing B lines and irregular pleura.","confirm":"Review history and CT for interstitial lung disease and compare with prior imaging.","trap":"CPE has a thin regular pleura; ARDS is not the only cause of irregular pleura."},"B22_9":{"clue":"B-line predominance in multiple lung fields outperforms CXR, clinical examination and NT-proBNP.","mech":"B lines reflect extravascular lung water directly, whereas other markers are indirect and nonspecific.","confirm":"Scan multiple lung zones for more than 2 B lines per field bilaterally.","trap":"Assuming BNP or CXR reliably rule out CPE; their accuracy is only around 50-60%."},"B22_10":{"clue":"Interstitial syndrome with large bilateral pleural effusions.","mech":"Raised hydrostatic pressure in left heart failure causes both interstitial edema and transudative effusions.","confirm":"Look for elevated E/e', dilated LA and IVC plethora to confirm hydrostatic mechanism.","trap":"Subpleural consolidations and lung pulse favour ARDS; absent effusions argue against CPE."},"B23_1":{"clue":"Mini-fluid challenge: 50 mL crystalloid over 10 seconds, VTI change about 9%.","mech":"A small, rapid bolus transiently increases preload; a preload-dependent ventricle increases stroke volume measurably.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI before and immediately after the bolus; a rise of about 9% predicts responsiveness.","trap":"Using 250 or 500 mL bolus thresholds; the mini challenge relies on a small volume."},"B23_2":{"clue":"Passive ventilation, IVC 2.2 cm at inspiration and 1.8 cm at expiration.","mech":"Positive pressure distends a compliant IVC in inspiration when the venous system is underfilled.","confirm":"Distensibility = (2.2 minus 1.8)/1.8 x 100 = 22%, above the 18% threshold.","trap":"Dividing by Dmax (gives 18%) or using the mean denominator threshold of 12%."},"B23_3":{"clue":"Spontaneously breathing septic patient: IVC collapsibility over 48%.","mech":"Negative inspiratory pressure collapses an underfilled IVC more, suggesting volume responsiveness.","confirm":"Collapsibility = (Dmax expiration minus Dmin inspiration)/Dmax expiration; values over 48% favour response.","trap":"Applying 12% or 18% distensibility thresholds, which are for passively ventilated patients."},"B23_4":{"clue":"Septic shock with ARDS: delta LVOT velocity 14% and delta IVC 13%.","mech":"With high risk of fluid overload, a strict threshold of 18% maximises specificity before giving fluid.","confirm":"Use the 18% threshold for both indices; consider PLR or mini-fluid challenge before bolus.","trap":"Using lower thresholds of 10-12%, acceptable only when risk of lung water overload is low."},"B23_5":{"clue":"Aortic peak velocity variation best sensitivity, SVC variation best specificity.","mech":"Aortic velocity reflects SV changes directly, while SVC collapse requires significant underfilling.","confirm":"Use aortic velocity variation to screen and SVC collapse to confirm fluid responsiveness.","trap":"Assuming IVC distensibility or PLR performed best in that study."},"B23_6":{"clue":"After 3 L, normal LVEDA but nearly obliterated end-systolic cavity and minimal velocity variation.","mech":"Low SVR allows near-complete emptying despite adequate filling, producing hypotension.","confirm":"Check LVEDA (normal), minimal respiratory variation and normal or high VTI; start vasopressor.","trap":"Assuming persistent hypovolemia; end-systolic obliteration with normal LVEDA indicates vasodilatation."},"B23_7":{"clue":"Peak instantaneous LVOT gradient at least 50 mm Hg on CW Doppler.","mech":"SAM narrows the LVOT in late systole; gradients of 50 mm Hg or more are hemodynamically significant.","confirm":"Align CW Doppler through the LVOT and measure peak late-peaking velocity; 3.5 m/s equals about 50 mm Hg.","trap":"PW velocity of 1.5 m/s or an early rounded envelope do not define obstruction; VTI variation measures preload response."},"B23_8":{"clue":"Dobutamine causing more hypotension and tachycardia with SAM and late-peaking dagger LVOT profile.","mech":"Inotropy and tachycardia shrink the LV cavity and worsen SAM, increasing obstruction and lowering output.","confirm":"Stop dobutamine, give fluid, slow the heart rate and use a pure vasoconstrictor; recheck the LVOT gradient.","trap":"Increasing inotropes or using milrinone worsens obstruction; diuretics reduce preload and worsen it."},"B23_9":{"clue":"SAM with mitral regurgitation directed posteriorly.","mech":"The anterior leaflet is pulled into the LVOT, creating a coaptation gap so the jet goes posteriorly into the LA.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show a posteriorly directed jet and CW for late-peaking LVOT gradient.","trap":"Anterior jets suggest posterior leaflet prolapse; central jets suggest annular dilatation."},"B23_10":{"clue":"Post-intubation hypotension in COPD with absent sliding, B lines and lung pulse, barcode sign, plethoric IVC and small ventricles.","mech":"Tension pneumothorax raises intrathoracic pressure, preventing venous return and compressing the heart.","confirm":"Search for a lung point if time allows, but proceed to immediate needle decompression in tension physiology.","trap":"Giving fluid or vasopressors; a plethoric IVC with small ventricles signals obstruction, not hypovolemia."},"B23_11":{"clue":"First branch point: high or low cardiac output.","mech":"High output with hypercontractility suggests vasodilatory shock, while low output suggests underfilling or obstruction.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI and cardiac output, then reassess after volume; if low output persists, look for LVOTO or pneumothorax.","trap":"Starting with lung sliding or IVC alone; cardiac output determines the next diagnostic path."},"B23_12":{"clue":"Intubated patient with poor TTE windows and suspected endocarditis with possible aortic root abscess.","mech":"TEE gives high-resolution images close to the heart, overcoming poor windows and detecting abscess.","confirm":"Perform TEE with focus on the aortic root, annulus and valve leaflets.","trap":"TTE is enough for initial qualitative LV or IVC assessment; TEE is for detailed or complicated questions."},"B24_1":{"clue":"No palpable pulse but coordinated ventricular contraction on subcostal view.","mech":"Contraction exists but generates too little pressure to produce a palpable pulse, defining pseudo-PEA.","confirm":"Confirm coordinated motion on a brief pulse check and consider arterial line or Doppler flow; treat as low-flow state.","trap":"True PEA shows cardiac standstill; fine VF shows quivering, not coordinated contraction."},"B24_2":{"clue":"Pooled values: sensitivity 95%, specificity 80%, negative LR 0.06.","mech":"Cardiac motion predicts ROSC well; absent motion makes ROSC unlikely, reflected in a low negative LR.","confirm":"Use focused echo during brief pulse checks to identify motion and support decisions with clinical context.","trap":"Reversing sensitivity and specificity or assuming cardiac standstill alone mandates stopping resuscitation."},"XC05_F37":{"clue":"Mickey Mouse portal triad: portal vein as the head, with two similar-sized tubular ears, one the hepatic artery and one the CBD.","mech":"The CBD carries static bile with no detectable flow, while the hepatic artery carries pulsatile arterial flow, so color Doppler separates two structures that look identical in grayscale.","confirm":"Apply color (or power) Doppler with a low velocity scale; the ear that stays black is the CBD, the one that fills and gives an arterial PW signal is the hepatic artery.","trap":"Harmonics, M-mode and depth change image quality or timing, not tissue identity; neither shows flow, so they cannot tell a duct from an artery of similar caliber."},"XC05_F38":{"clue":"Long-axis CBD measurement with the hepatic artery running alongside it.","mech":"Duct caliber reflects the lumen, so walls are excluded; normal caliber rises with age and after cholecystectomy, so a fixed 4 mm cutoff misclassifies many patients.","confirm":"Place calipers inner wall to inner wall at the widest point and use color Doppler to prove the tube has no flow before measuring.","trap":"Outer-to-outer is the aortic convention, not the duct convention; a single 4 mm cutoff ignores age-related and post-cholecystectomy dilation, which can reach 10 mm."},"XC05_F41":{"clue":"A dependent hypoechoic 'collection' that vanishes when the probe is fanned through the gallbladder.","mech":"Side lobe energy returning from strong reflectors beside the curved gallbladder wall is mapped into the anechoic lumen as low-level echoes; real material persists in every plane.","confirm":"Fan and rotate through the gallbladder, reposition the patient, and lower gain or adjust focus; true sludge stays dependent, layers and shifts slowly with position.","trap":"Sludge, polyps and fluid are real structures and stay visible from multiple angles; a finding seen in only one plane is artifact until proven otherwise."},"XC05_F42":{"clue":"Anterior wall 5.3 mm, pericholecystic fluid and shadowing dependent stones in a febrile patient with RUQ pain.","mech":"A stone obstructing the cystic duct causes gallbladder distension, inflammation and wall edema, producing thickening over 3 mm and pericholecystic fluid.","confirm":"Elicit a sonographic Murphy sign, look for an impacted stone in the neck, and measure the anterior wall only to avoid posterior enhancement artifact.","trap":"Cholelithiasis alone lacks wall thickening and fluid; acalculous cholecystitis requires absent stones and sludge, so visible shadowing stones exclude it."},"XC05_F44":{"clue":"Subxiphoid sagittal view of the gastric antrum beside the inferior liver tip, plus the patient's age.","mech":"Antral cross-sectional area correlates linearly with total gastric fluid volume, so a single-plane area combined with age predicts volume.","confirm":"Measure antral CSA in right lateral decubitus, then volume = 27.0 + 14.6 x CSA - 1.28 x age; divide by weight and compare with 1.5 mL/kg.","trap":"Wall thickness and antral length are not in the validated model; distance from the liver is a landmark, not a measurement of content."},"XC05_F45":{"clue":"Bubbles swirling into the gastric antrum during agitated saline injection through a newly placed NG tube.","mech":"Microbubbles are strong reflectors; if they appear in the stomach during injection, the tube outlet must lie within or very near the stomach.","confirm":"Scan the epigastrium in real time while injecting; also look for the tube in the cervical esophagus beside the trachea and confirm with radiograph if required by local policy.","trap":"Seashore is a lung M-mode sign and sparkle refers to food particles; neither relates to tube position, and an esophageal tube gives no gastric fogging."},"XC05_F46":{"clue":"Particulate, sparkling antral content before emergent intubation.","mech":"Volumes above about 1.5 mL/kg exceed the baseline secretions of a fasted stomach and are associated with increased aspiration risk.","confirm":"Calculate volume from antral CSA (27.0 + 14.6 x CSA - 1.28 x age) and divide by weight; any solid or thick content alone already defines a full stomach.","trap":"0.2 mL/kg is within normal fasting secretions and fixed absolute volumes ignore body size; 1.5 mL/kg is the threshold used in perioperative gastric POCUS."},"XC05_F47":{"clue":"Systematic lawn mower sweep of the abdomen in suspected SBO with focal tenderness.","mech":"Small bowel loops are mobile and dilated segments may be anywhere; starting away from the tender point preserves cooperation and builds a complete map.","confirm":"Sweep in overlapping vertical lanes across all four quadrants, finishing at the tender area, measuring loop diameter and observing peristalsis.","trap":"Starting over the tender point causes guarding and limits the exam; scanning only one region misses distant dilated loops."},"XC05_F48":{"clue":"Fluid-filled loop over 25 mm with to-and-fro movement of intraluminal particulate content.","mech":"Mechanical obstruction makes active peristalsis push contents against a blockage, so they swirl back and forth; ileus lacks effective peristalsis.","confirm":"Observe the loop for at least several seconds, check compressibility, look for a transition point, wall thickening and free fluid.","trap":"Dilation alone does not separate SBO from ileus, and loops under 25 mm or without contents argue against obstruction rather than for it."},"XC06_F01":{"clue":"EDV reported from a single PLAX internal diameter (Teichholz) in a patient whose LV may not be uniform.","mech":"Teichholz cubes one linear dimension assuming a symmetric ellipsoid; regional dysfunction, aneurysm or remodeling breaks that assumption, so error is large.","confirm":"Measure volumes with Simpson biplane method of disks in apical 4- and 2-chamber views, or 3D, as recommended by ASE/EACVI.","trap":"End-diastolic timing, sinus rhythm and tip-level measurement are correct technique; the error is geometric, not timing-related."},"XC06_F02":{"clue":"M-mode at the mitral tips measuring a 13 mm gap between the anterior leaflet E point and the septum.","mech":"Low stroke volume and a dilated LV reduce early diastolic inflow and leaflet excursion, so the leaflet fails to approach the septum.","confirm":"EPSS over 7 mm suggests reduced EF; corroborate with visual EF, MAPSE, LVOT VTI and Simpson biplane.","trap":"AR jets, mitral stenosis and septal hypertrophy can falsely increase EPSS; SAM is a systolic finding, whereas EPSS is measured in early diastole."},"XC06_F03":{"clue":"M-mode through the lateral mitral annulus in apical 4-chamber, measuring systolic base-to-apex excursion.","mech":"Longitudinal subendocardial fibers pull the annulus toward the apex in systole; reduced excursion reflects reduced longitudinal and global LV function.","confirm":"Align the cursor parallel to annular motion; MAPSE above 10 mm suggests EF over 50%, below 8 mm suggests EF under 50%.","trap":"TAPSE uses the tricuspid annulus, EPSS uses the mitral tips in PLAX, and fractional shortening uses short-axis dimensions."},"XC06_F04":{"clue":"End-diastolic A4C endocardial trace divided into a stack of disks.","mech":"The method of disks sums elliptical disks whose two diameters come from orthogonal planes; a single plane forces an assumption of circular disks.","confirm":"Trace the apical 2-chamber view at end-diastole with matched LV length, then repeat both at end-systole to obtain biplane volumes and EF.","trap":"PLAX diameter is a linear method and LVOT PW gives stroke volume; neither completes a biplane volumetric assessment."},"XC06_F05":{"clue":"End-systolic A4C trace with a known end-diastolic volume of 130 mL.","mech":"Stroke volume is the difference between end-diastolic and end-systolic volumes; EF is that difference as a fraction of EDV.","confirm":"ESV about 49 mL; SV = 130 - 49 = 81 mL; EF = 81/130 = about 62%.","trap":"49 mL is the ESV, not SV, and 62 is the EF percentage, not millilitres; read what each number represents."},"XC06_F06":{"clue":"Traced PW systolic envelope from the apical view plus R-R interval calipers.","mech":"The VTI is the stroke distance; multiplying by LVOT cross-sectional area converts it to stroke volume, and heart rate converts that to cardiac output.","confirm":"CSA = 0.785 x LVOT diameter squared; SV = CSA x VTI; CO = SV x 60/R-R interval in seconds.","trap":"TR velocity, E/A and IVC estimate pressures or preload, not flow; diameter errors are squared, so measure the LVOT carefully in mid-systole."},"XC07_F03":{"clue":"One 3D RV sliced at slightly different 4-chamber planes gives basal diameters of 59, 57 and 55 mm.","mech":"The crescentic RV wraps around the LV, so the linear dimension depends strongly on where the plane cuts it; minor rotation foreshortens it.","confirm":"Use an RV-focused apical view with the LV apex centered, showing the maximal basal diameter and whole free wall without foreshortening.","trap":"Linear RV size is not reliable from any plane; PLAX gives only RVOT dimensions, and subcostal measurements are not standardized thresholds."},"XC07_F05":{"clue":"D-shaped LV with septal flattening throughout the entire cardiac cycle in worsening right heart failure.","mech":"When RV pressure approaches LV pressure in both systole and diastole, the septum is pushed leftward throughout the cycle, signalling pressure overload.","confirm":"Assess eccentricity index in systole and diastole separately, estimate RVSP from TR velocity plus RAP, and look at RVOT acceleration time.","trap":"Diastole-only flattening indicates volume overload such as ASD; constriction gives respiratory septal bounce, not fixed flattening."},"XC07_F07":{"clue":"RVOT PW envelope with two systolic peaks: mid-systolic notching in undifferentiated pulmonary hypertension.","mech":"In a stiff, high-resistance pulmonary bed, reflected pressure waves return early and decelerate flow mid-systole, producing a notch and second peak.","confirm":"Measure RVOT acceleration time, estimate PASP, and integrate with RV size and function; mid-systolic notching suggests high PVR and precapillary disease.","trap":"Late-systolic notching fits left heart disease with milder PVR rise; pulmonic stenosis gives high velocities across the valve, not a notched RVOT envelope."},"XC08_F01":{"clue":"Mitral E smaller than A (E/A 0.8 or less) with an LVOT VTI of 10 cm in a ventilated patient in shock.","mech":"Low E/A means LA pressure is not driving early filling, so filling pressure is not high; low VTI means low stroke volume, so a preload challenge is safe and may help.","confirm":"Give a bolus or passive leg raise and repeat VTI; continue while VTI rises about 10-15%, stop when it plateaus or E/A and E/e' rise.","trap":"Low VTI alone does not mean LV failure needing inotropes; LVOT obstruction would show high LVOT velocities, not simply a low VTI."},"XC08_F02":{"clue":"PPV above 13% with a markedly dilated RV and CVP 19 mm Hg.","mech":"Positive pressure raises RV afterload in inspiration; a failing RV drops output cyclically, so LV preload varies even without hypovolemia, creating false-positive PPV.","confirm":"Image the RV: dilation, septal flattening and high CVP indicate RV failure; manage with afterload reduction, ventilator adjustment and RV support, not fluid.","trap":"PPV tracks only cyclic LV preload change, not its cause; treating high PPV as hypovolemia here worsens RV dilation and interdependence."},"XC08_F03":{"clue":"Upper esophageal TEE of the SVC that stays open throughout tidal ventilation in a passive ventilated patient.","mech":"The intrathoracic SVC collapses during insufflation when its transmural pressure is low; a full, noncollapsing SVC indicates adequate preload.","confirm":"Collapsibility index = (max - min)/max diameter on M-mode; above about 36% predicts fluid responsiveness, no collapse argues against it.","trap":"Intermediate variation is the gray zone, not absent variation; tension pneumothorax is a lung ultrasound diagnosis and would not be inferred from SVC alone."},"XC08_F04":{"clue":"Serial end-expiratory IVC M-mode after two boluses with diameter now exceeding 25 mm.","mech":"Fluid fills the compliant venous system until the IVC is distended; beyond 25 mm the heart is on the flat part of its Starling curve.","confirm":"Track end-expiratory IVC: under 10-13 mm suggests response, 13-25 mm is a gray zone, over 25 mm suggests no response; correlate with VTI change.","trap":"A big IVC does not exclude abdominal hypertension, which can distort IVC; it does not mean ongoing responsiveness."},"XC08_F05":{"clue":"Small hyperkinetic LV with cavity obliteration and SAM, but no respiratory variation of the SVC.","mech":"Hypovolemia makes the SVC collapse during insufflation; vasoplegia can make the LV look small and hyperdynamic despite a full venous system.","confirm":"Use TEE SVC collapsibility; low variation points to vasoplegia, so favor vasopressor, then reassess LVOT gradient and SAM.","trap":"Do not equate a small, kissing LV with hypovolemia; low afterload produces the same picture, and fluid alone may not fix it."},"XC09_F02":{"clue":"RVOT diameter over 30 mm shown as an ASE threshold for RV dilation.","mech":"The proximal RVOT in PLAX is the anterior segment of the RV; its diameter enlarges with RV volume or pressure overload.","confirm":"Measure proximal RVOT in PLAX at end-diastole; in PSAX use proximal over 35 mm and distal over 27 mm; inflow uses RV-focused A4C diameters.","trap":"PSAX RVOT has different cutoffs, and RV-focused A4C uses basal, mid and length dimensions rather than RVOT diameter."},"XC09_F03":{"clue":"Markedly enlarged RV with a flattened septum making a D-shaped LV in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"Within the shared pericardium, RV pressure and volume overload push the septum leftward and restrict LV filling, reducing stroke volume.","confirm":"Assess timing of flattening across the cycle, RV/LV ratio, TAPSE, TR velocity and IVC; look for DVT and clot in transit if PE is suspected.","trap":"A small compressed LV can look hypovolemic, but the RV is dilated; tamponade shows effusion and chamber collapse, not RV dilation."},"XC09_F04":{"clue":"Basal and mid RV free wall hypokinesis with preserved apical contraction in suspected PE.","mech":"Acute pressure overload stresses the free wall, while the apex may be tethered by a hyperdynamic LV, producing a regional pattern.","confirm":"Integrate with RV/LV ratio, septal shape, 60/60 sign, TR velocity, DVT search and CT pulmonary angiography.","trap":"McConnell was initially reported as highly specific for PE, but later data show it also occurs in RV infarction and other acute RV failure."},"XC09_F05":{"clue":"RVOT acceleration time 57 ms and TR peak gradient 45 mm Hg in massive PE.","mech":"An unconditioned RV facing sudden afterload cannot generate high pressure, so TR gradient stays modest, while the stiff pulmonary bed shortens acceleration time.","confirm":"60/60 sign: AT 60 ms or less and TR gradient 60 mm Hg or less; combine with McConnell sign and DVT findings.","trap":"Chronic PH produces a hypertrophied RV with higher gradients; a short AT is not normal coupling, and pulmonic stenosis shows high velocity at the valve."},"XC09_F06":{"clue":"Mobile right atrial echodensity plus mobile clot in the right pulmonary artery in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"Clot in transit is embolising thrombus en route from deep veins; ongoing embolisation worsens RV afterload and failure.","confirm":"Assess RV size and function, image the PA bifurcation, scan for DVT, and escalate to reperfusion therapy given high-risk features.","trap":"Do not dismiss right heart thrombus as incidental; catheter-related thrombus exists, but mobile clot plus PA thrombus means PE with high risk."},"XC09_F07":{"clue":"Small peripheral pleural-based hypoechoic consolidation next to normal pleura in suspected PE.","mech":"Peripheral pulmonary artery occlusion causes hemorrhagic infarction of the subpleural lung, producing wedge-shaped or rounded hypoechoic lesions.","confirm":"Look for multiple pleural-based triangular or rounded lesions, minimal air bronchograms, small adjacent effusion, and confirm with CT pulmonary angiography.","trap":"Interstitial syndrome gives diffuse B lines, pneumothorax abolishes sliding with a lung point, and effusion is anechoic above the diaphragm, not a focal peripheral lesion."},"XC09_F09":{"clue":"With probe pressure the common femoral vein disappears while the artery stays round.","mech":"Patent veins are low-pressure and collapse completely under external compression; thrombus prevents wall apposition.","confirm":"Compress at the CFV, saphenofemoral junction, femoral vein and popliteal vein, confirming full wall-to-wall apposition at each site.","trap":"Inadequate pressure would leave the vein partially open, and arterial occlusion is not tested by venous compression."},"XC09_F10":{"clue":"Popliteal vein superficial to the artery being tested by compression.","mech":"Venous thrombus resists compression; pressure enough to deform the higher-pressure artery is enough to collapse a patent vein.","confirm":"Compress until the artery deforms; noncompressibility defines DVT; scan about 7 cm beyond the popliteal crease to include the trifurcation.","trap":"Spontaneous echo contrast is slow flow, not thrombus; vein size and arterial color flow do not define DVT."},"XC09_F11":{"clue":"Common femoral vein under compression that does not collapse and contains echogenic intraluminal material.","mech":"Thrombus fills the lumen and prevents wall apposition; organizing clot becomes echogenic.","confirm":"Compress at multiple levels; noncompressibility is diagnostic even when thrombus is anechoic; color Doppler can show filling defects.","trap":"Reverberation does not prevent compression; nodes are oval with an echogenic hilum outside the vessel; arterial plaque lies in the artery."},"XC09_F12":{"clue":"Oval noncompressible structure with an echogenic center and internal hilar color flow at the groin.","mech":"Lymph nodes are solid with a hypoechoic cortex and fatty hilum containing vessels, so they do not compress and show hilar flow.","confirm":"Track the structure proximally and distally: a vein is tubular and continuous, a node ends; color Doppler shows hilar flow rather than luminal flow.","trap":"A noncompressible round structure is not automatically DVT; thrombus sits in a continuous tubular vein, and a pseudoaneurysm shows swirling yin-yang flow."},"XC09_F13":{"clue":"Common femoral vein PW Doppler during manual calf squeeze.","mech":"Calf compression pushes a bolus of blood upstream; an occlusive thrombus between calf and probe blocks this bolus, blunting augmentation.","confirm":"Compare augmentation and respiratory phasicity, then perform compression ultrasound at each segment to confirm.","trap":"Brisk augmentation and respiratory phasicity are normal findings, and pulsatile flow suggests raised right-sided pressure, not DVT."},"XC10_F01":{"clue":"Two echo-free spaces in PLAX: one anterior and one posterior to the descending thoracic aorta.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta, so pericardial fluid tracks between heart and aorta; pleural fluid lies behind it.","confirm":"Use the descending aorta as the landmark in PLAX and confirm pleural fluid with lateral lung views; pericardial fluid also appears in subcostal views.","trap":"Do not call all posterior fluid pericardial; a left pleural effusion extends behind the aorta."},"XC10_F03":{"clue":"Pericardial effusion with RA collapse in late diastole and early systole in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"The RA has the lowest pressure during atrial relaxation, so even modest pericardial pressure inverts its wall; longer inversion means higher pericardial pressure.","confirm":"Time the RA inversion in M-mode or frame-by-frame; inversion lasting more than one-third of the cycle increases specificity; check RV collapse, IVC and inflow variation.","trap":"RA collapse is sensitive but not the most specific sign; RV diastolic collapse is more specific; it is not confined to expiration."},"XC10_F04":{"clue":"Diastolic RV free wall collapse with pericardial effusion in the same patient.","mech":"RV wall is thicker and its diastolic pressure higher than RA, so collapse requires higher pericardial pressure, appearing first in expiration.","confirm":"Confirm early diastolic timing with M-mode, and integrate IVC plethora and mitral/tricuspid inflow respiratory variation.","trap":"RV collapse is less sensitive but more specific than RA collapse; it can be absent with RV hypertrophy or pulmonary hypertension."},"XC10_F05":{"clue":"M-mode at mitral tips showing inward RV free wall motion in a tachycardic patient with effusion.","mech":"Tamponade-related RV collapse is an early diastolic phenomenon; high temporal resolution M-mode can confirm timing that 2D frame rate misses at high heart rates.","confirm":"Use the mitral valve opening on the same M-mode trace as the diastolic timing marker for RV wall inversion.","trap":"M-mode does not quantify effusion volume, and inflow respiratory variation requires PW Doppler."},"XC10_F06":{"clue":"Prominent expiratory diastolic hepatic venous flow reversal with pericardial effusion.","mech":"In tamponade, expiration reduces right heart filling and raises pressure, so diastolic forward flow blunts or reverses in hepatic veins.","confirm":"Record hepatic vein PW with a respirometer; look for expiratory diastolic reversal, plus IVC plethora, chamber collapse and mitral inflow variation.","trap":"Tricuspid stenosis and portal hypertension alter flow without this respiratory timing; normal hepatic flow has systolic and diastolic forward waves."},"XC10_F11":{"clue":"Large effusion in severe pulmonary hypertension with dilated RV, compressed LV and no tricuspid inflow variation.","mech":"High RV pressure exceeds intrapericardial pressure, so the RV resists collapse; pressure is transmitted to the lower-pressure LV.","confirm":"Look for LV or LA collapse, IVC plethora, and clinical deterioration; interpret classic right-sided signs cautiously.","trap":"Absent RV collapse and inflow variation does not exclude tamponade; ventilation and hypovolemia are not the explanation."},"XC10_F12":{"clue":"Tamponade with marked RV collapse but no mitral or tricuspid inflow variation in severe LV dysfunction and LVH.","mech":"Very high LV diastolic pressure means the pericardium mainly limits RV filling; interdependence and respiratory variation are lost.","confirm":"Rely on chamber collapse, IVC plethora and clinical picture rather than respiratory inflow variation.","trap":"Respiratory variation is not always present in tamponade; LV dysfunction does not reduce the sensitivity of right-sided collapse."},"XC14_F01":{"clue":"Panel C transmitral PW: tall E, small A (E/A 2 or more) and short deceleration time before weaning.","mech":"High LA pressure drives rapid early filling into a stiff LV; pressures equalise quickly, so deceleration time shortens and atrial contribution falls.","confirm":"Add septal and lateral e' to calculate E/e', look at LA size and TR velocity, and consider a trial of diuresis before SBT.","trap":"Impaired relaxation has E/A under 1; young compliant hearts can have high E/A but normal DT and e'; pseudonormal has E/A 0.8-2."},"XC14_F02":{"clue":"Anatomic M-mode of right hemidiaphragm: 2.7 cm on quiet breathing, 4.4 cm on deep breath.","mech":"Diaphragmatic contraction displaces the dome caudally; excursion reflects neuromuscular function and effort.","confirm":"Normal quiet excursion is about 1.5-2.5 cm; under 1 cm indicates severe dysfunction; thickening fraction is measured separately on the zone of apposition.","trap":"5 cm is not the normal threshold; thickening fraction is measured on a linear probe; paradoxical motion would move cranially in inspiration."},"XC14_F03":{"clue":"12-region lung ultrasound scores summing to 18 after a 1-hour SBT.","mech":"Higher LUS scores reflect loss of aeration from edema, derecruitment or consolidation; SBT-induced aeration loss predicts post-extubation failure.","confirm":"Sum 12 regions scored 0-3; under 13 suggests success, over 17 predicts failure; look for cardiac causes with echo.","trap":"Misadding the regions gives 12 or 15; 24 is not total loss of aeration (maximum 36)."},"XC14_F04":{"clue":"Dependent posterolateral consolidation and B lines with normal anterior fields; E/A under 1 and lateral e' 0.11 m/s.","mech":"Normal e' and E/e' near 7 indicate normal filling pressure, so aeration loss is gravitational derecruitment, not cardiogenic edema.","confirm":"E/e' = 0.75/0.11 = 6.8; optimise recruitment and PEEP, physiotherapy, and reassess LUS before extubation.","trap":"Raised score alone does not imply cardiogenic edema; that pattern needs high E/e', diffuse anterior B lines and restrictive inflow."},"XC15_F02":{"clue":"Return cannula tip in IVC 4.8 cm short of the IVC-RA junction during VV ECMO cannulation.","mech":"A return tip far from the RA increases recirculation, as returned oxygenated blood is drained back into the drainage cannula.","confirm":"Advance under subcostal or TEE guidance until the tip sits at the IVC-RA junction; check for recirculation and separation of cannula tips.","trap":"Withdrawal makes it worse; the tip is not yet near the tricuspid valve; re-siting is unnecessary when advancement corrects position."},"XC16_F05":{"clue":"MCA TCD showing oscillating to-and-fro flow with diastolic reversal, persistent and reproducible in all insonated arteries.","mech":"When ICP exceeds diastolic arterial pressure, forward flow occurs only in systole and reverses in diastole, with near-zero net flow.","confirm":"Confirm in anterior and posterior circulations on repeated exams; progression to systolic spikes and absent flow indicates cerebral circulatory arrest.","trap":"High-resistance patterns may be reversible, but persistent oscillating flow is not; wall filters do not produce reproducible reversal."},"XC16_F06":{"clue":"Transtemporal B-mode measurement from temporal bone to the third ventricle.","mech":"A mass effect displaces midline structures; comparing bilateral distances from the skull to the third ventricle quantifies the shift.","confirm":"Midline shift = (distance A - distance B)/2; correlate with CT.","trap":"Third ventricle width does not estimate ICP; ONSD and Lindegaard ratio need different measurements; absence of shift does not exclude raised ICP."},"XC16_F07":{"clue":"Continuous nonpulsatile MCA forward flow with mean velocity about 60 cm/s in a VA ECMO patient.","mech":"VA ECMO delivers continuous flow, so arterial pulsatility is lost, but forward flow with normal velocity means preserved cerebral perfusion.","confirm":"Check mean velocity, direction of flow and all arteries; HITS suggest microemboli and need further investigation.","trap":"Loss of pulsatility on VA ECMO is expected and not circulatory arrest; reverberating flow has to-and-fro reversal."},"XC16_F08":{"clue":"Left MCA mean velocity 201 cm/s with aliasing on color and spectral Doppler.","mech":"Velocity rises with narrowed lumen (vasospasm, stenosis) or increased flow (hyperemia); TCD alone cannot distinguish these.","confirm":"Calculate the Lindegaard ratio (MCA/extracranial ICA mean velocity); over 3 suggests vasospasm, under 3 hyperemia; correlate clinically.","trap":"Do not label all high velocities as vasospasm; systolic spikes are low-velocity and occur in arrest; shock decreases velocities."},"XC17_F03":{"clue":"LUQ FAST probe at the left posterior axillary line at xiphoid level.","mech":"The spleen lies posteriorly and superiorly; free fluid preferentially collects in the subphrenic space before the splenorenal recess.","confirm":"Visualise the subdiaphragmatic space, splenorenal recess, splenic tip and inferior pole of the kidney.","trap":"The spleen is not anterior; looking only at the splenorenal recess misses the most common LUQ fluid location."},"XC17_F08":{"clue":"Longitudinal suprapubic view obtained by rotating the probe toward the head.","mech":"The rectovesical or rectouterine pouch is the most dependent pelvic space; longitudinal views show it behind the bladder.","confirm":"Image both transverse and longitudinal planes; look posterior to the bladder and between bowel loops.","trap":"Bladder wall thickness and volume measurement are not FAST goals; iliac DVT is not assessed in E-FAST."},"XC17_F11":{"clue":"B lines arising from the pleural line despite hard-to-see lung sliding.","mech":"B lines arise from interaction of ultrasound with the visceral pleura and interstitium; air separating the pleural layers would block them.","confirm":"Look for B lines, lung pulse or sliding; any of these rules out pneumothorax at that site.","trap":"Absent sliding alone does not confirm pneumothorax; lung point needs visible transition."},"XC18_F04":{"clue":"Echogenic mass filling the LV apex 10 days after a large anterior STEMI.","mech":"Apical akinesis causes stasis, endothelial injury and inflammation, promoting mural thrombus formation.","confirm":"Use a focused apical view, lower depth, high-frequency settings or contrast to confirm a distinct mass in an akinetic segment.","trap":"Near-field artifact lacks a defined border and moves with probe; apical HCM is myocardium; noncompaction shows trabeculations with deep recesses."},"XC19_F01":{"clue":"Normal mitral valve with turbulent mosaic diastolic inflow on color Doppler.","mech":"Color Doppler aliases when velocity exceeds the Nyquist limit; a low scale of about 15 cm/s makes normal inflow alias.","confirm":"Raise the Nyquist limit to 50-70 cm/s for valve assessment and confirm with PW/CW Doppler velocities.","trap":"Mitral stenosis requires anatomic restriction; wall filter and low gain decrease color, not create mosaic aliasing."},"XC19_F02":{"clue":"Zoomed PLAX with dotted line across the LVOT for continuity equation.","mech":"Diameter is squared in the area calculation, so small errors greatly affect stroke volume and AVA.","confirm":"Measure inner edge to inner edge 2-5 mm from leaflet hinge points in mid-systole; place PW sample at the same level.","trap":"Sinotubular junction and leaflet tips are wrong levels, and outer edges overestimate area."},"XC19_F03":{"clue":"Late-peaking dagger-shaped CW signal after mitral valve repair, with anterior leaflet contacting the septum.","mech":"Excess leaflet tissue and narrow aortomitral angle drag the anterior leaflet into the LVOT in systole, creating dynamic obstruction and posterior MR.","confirm":"Identify SAM on 2D and M-mode, gradient on CW; treat with fluid, beta-blockade, stopping inotropes and raising afterload.","trap":"AS is parabolic with fixed narrowing; paravalvular leak and prosthetic MS do not produce a late-peaking LVOT signal."},"XC19_F04":{"clue":"LVOT area 3.14 cm2, LVOT TVI 20 cm and AV VTI 105.4 cm.","mech":"Flow through the LVOT equals flow through the aortic valve, so the area ratio is the inverse of the VTI ratio.","confirm":"AVA = (3.14 x 20)/105.4 = 0.6 cm2; under 1.0 cm2 is severe AS.","trap":"Using diameter (2.0) instead of area gives about 0.4; omitting the LVOT VTI or swapping VTIs produces answers of 3.1 or larger."},"XC19_F05":{"clue":"TEE short-axis en-face AV with thickened restricted cusps and small orifice at maximal opening with mosaic color.","mech":"Calcification limits cusp opening, reducing orifice area; flow accelerates and becomes turbulent beyond the narrowed opening.","confirm":"Planimetry of the orifice at maximal opening; confirm with continuity equation, peak velocity and mean gradient.","trap":"Normal valves open widely with laminar flow; dissection flaps appear in the aorta; quadricuspid valves show four cusps."},"XC19_F08":{"clue":"Hockey-stick anterior leaflet, atrial-side flow convergence and prolonged pressure half-time in a young woman.","mech":"Commissural fusion restricts tip opening while the body balloons; the narrowed orifice delays pressure equalisation, prolonging PHT.","confirm":"Planimetry of the mitral orifice, MVA = 220/PHT, mean gradient on CW, and Wilkins score for valve anatomy.","trap":"Calcific MS affects the annulus with no commissural fusion; myxoma is a mass; SAM is systolic."},"XC19_F09":{"clue":"Red tracing extends beyond dense mitral CW envelope compared with green.","mech":"Faint signals outside the modal velocity represent noise or transit-time broadening; including them raises VTI and mean gradient.","confirm":"Trace the dense outer edge of the modal velocity; average several beats in AF.","trap":"Overtracing increases the gradient, not underestimates; PHT is measured from the E deceleration slope, not the full VTI."},"XC19_F11":{"clue":"Prosthetic tricuspid valve with turbulent inflow, mean gradient 12 mm Hg and dilated IVC.","mech":"Obstruction impedes RA emptying, raising RA pressure and dilating the IVC with reduced collapse.","confirm":"Measure TV mean gradient, PHT and VTI; mean gradient over 6 mm Hg and PHT over 230 ms suggest significant prosthetic stenosis.","trap":"TR gives systolic jets and would not raise the inflow gradient this much; normal prosthesis gradients are lower."},"XC19_F12":{"clue":"PLAX AR jet showing flow convergence, vena contracta and jet width in LVOT.","mech":"Jet width at its origin reflects regurgitant orifice size; normalising to LVOT diameter grades severity.","confirm":"Jet width/LVOT diameter: 65% or more suggests severe AR; also measure vena contracta, PHT and descending aorta reversal.","trap":"Jet length depends on driving pressure and LV compliance; jet area relative to LA is a MR concept."},"XC19_F13":{"clue":"Holodiastolic flow reversal in descending thoracic aorta in eccentric AR.","mech":"Significant regurgitation draws blood back from the aorta throughout diastole into the LV.","confirm":"PW in proximal descending aorta: holodiastolic reversal with end-diastolic velocity over 20 cm/s supports severe AR; abdominal aorta reversal is more specific.","trap":"Brief early diastolic reversal is normal; coarctation produces diastolic forward flow; PDA can mimic but is uncommon."},"XC19_F14":{"clue":"Dense, early-peaking, rapidly descending CW MR and TR signals.","mech":"Large regurgitant volume rapidly raises atrial pressure (large V wave), narrowing the ventricular-atrial gradient during systole.","confirm":"Assess signal density, vena contracta, PISA, pulmonary or hepatic vein reversal.","trap":"Low LV pressure lowers peak velocity but not shape; malalignment underestimates velocity but does not cause early peaking."},"XC19_F15":{"clue":"Large flow convergence but late systolic MR on CW, traced two ways.","mech":"Late systolic MR flows only part of systole, so regurgitant volume may be modest even with large PISA.","confirm":"Trace only the dense signal and multiply EROA by MR VTI to calculate regurgitant volume.","trap":"A big PISA does not mean severe MR unless holosystolic; overtracing overestimates regurgitant volume; PHT is not used for MR."},"XC19_F16":{"clue":"Hepatic vein PW with flow above the baseline during systole.","mech":"Severe TR raises RA pressure during systole, reversing hepatic venous flow toward the liver.","confirm":"Confirm with large vena contracta, PISA, dense triangular CW TR signal and dilated IVC.","trap":"Constriction gives expiratory diastolic reversal; normal hepatic flow is forward in systole and diastole; MR affects pulmonary veins."},"XC19_F18":{"clue":"AR PISA: aliasing velocity 40 cm/s, radius 0.69 cm, peak velocity 455 cm/s, regurgitant TVI 221 cm.","mech":"Flow through the hemisphere equals flow through the orifice; dividing by peak velocity gives EROA; multiplying by VTI gives regurgitant volume.","confirm":"EROA = 2 x pi x 0.69 squared x 40/455 = 0.26 cm2; RVol = 0.26 x 221 = 58 mL.","trap":"Forgetting to square the radius or using diameter produces large errors; baseline shift should be toward the jet to enlarge the hemisphere."},"XC19_F19":{"clue":"Dissection flap from the aortic sinus prolapsing into the valve with torrential AR and wide vena contracta.","mech":"The flap disrupts commissural support and prolapses through the valve, preventing coaptation.","confirm":"TEE long- and short-axis views of the root; check coronary involvement, effusion and extent of dissection.","trap":"Endocarditis produces vegetations; bicuspid valve gives an eccentric jet; ruptured sinus shows continuous flow into the RV."},"XC19_F20":{"clue":"Mitral annular diameter 4.23 cm and PW annular TVI in AR.","mech":"Without MR, mitral inflow equals forward stroke volume; LVOT stroke volume includes regurgitant flow, so the difference is AR volume.","confirm":"Mitral SV = pi x (4.23/2) squared x annular TVI; AR RVol = LVOT SV - mitral SV.","trap":"Continuity MVA uses tips, not the annulus; PHT is measured on the AR jet; E/e' uses e', not TVI."},"XC19_F22":{"clue":"Dense, early-peaking MR CW signal with rapid deceleration when heart rate rises after AMI.","mech":"Severe acute MR into a noncompliant LA produces a large V wave, rapidly equalising LV and LA pressures in late systole.","confirm":"Assess PISA, vena contracta, pulmonary vein systolic reversal and LA size.","trap":"Low-velocity signal reflects equalisation, not mild MR; LVOT obstruction has late peaking; late systolic MR starts mid-systole."},"XC19_F23":{"clue":"Flail P2 segment of the posterior mitral leaflet.","mech":"Flail leaflet overrides the opposite leaflet, directing the jet away from the flail segment.","confirm":"Use 3D or biplane TEE to identify the flail segment, and assess jet direction and PISA.","trap":"Restricted leaflets direct jets toward the affected leaflet; flail leaflets direct jets away."},"XC19_F24":{"clue":"Equal regurgitant volume but eccentric jet looks smaller than a central jet.","mech":"Coanda effect makes wall-hugging jets spread along walls rather than entrain fluid, reducing visible area.","confirm":"Use vena contracta, PISA EROA and regurgitant volume rather than jet area.","trap":"Driving pressure and Nyquist settings affect jet area but are not the explanation here."},"XC19_F25":{"clue":"Carcinoid heart disease with large coaptation gap and laminar TR flow.","mech":"Fixed open leaflets create a large regurgitant orifice; RA and RV pressures equalise quickly, so flow is laminar and low-velocity.","confirm":"Measure vena contracta, look for a dense triangular CW signal, dilated IVC and hepatic systolic reversal.","trap":"Laminar flow does not imply trivial TR; ASD jets cross the septum, not the valve."},"XC20_F01":{"clue":"Vegetation on bioprosthesis plus heterogeneous periannular thickening extending into the intervalvular fibrosa.","mech":"Infection spreads from the sewing ring into periannular tissue, causing abscess formation.","confirm":"TEE multiple planes; look for echolucent cavities, pseudoaneurysm or fistula with color flow; consider CT or PET.","trap":"Normal sewing ring is uniform; Lambl's excrescence and fibroelastoma are not periannular thickening."},"XC20_F03":{"clue":"Bicuspid aortic valve with viridans bacteremia, nondiagnostic TTE, and TEE showing vegetation and thickened annulus.","mech":"TEE provides higher resolution and closer views of the valve and annulus, revealing vegetation and abscess.","confirm":"TEE short-axis and long-axis of AV; transgastric views of the basal septum.","trap":"A thickened valve on TTE does not exclude IE; TTE misses abscesses."},"XC20_F04":{"clue":"TTE shows single mitral vegetation; TEE of same valve shows multiple lesions on both leaflets.","mech":"TEE has better spatial resolution and closer proximity to the mitral valve.","confirm":"TEE multiplane imaging with 3D for vegetation extent and attachment.","trap":"TTE underestimates lesion number and attachment; TEE does not show calcification here."},"XC20_F06":{"clue":"Thin, linear filament about 2 mm long on the aortic valve, without bulk or soft-tissue mass.","mech":"Lambl's excrescences are degenerative filiform strands at the coaptation line formed by wear and tear, so they are thin, short and uniform rather than bulky infected tissue.","confirm":"Assess location (coaptation line), thickness, length and mobility on zoomed TEE, and integrate with Duke criteria, cultures and valve dysfunction.","trap":"Vegetations are irregular, bulky, oscillating soft-tissue masses with regurgitation; fibroelastomas are frond-like, larger and often on the aortic side mid-cusp."},"XC20_F07":{"clue":"Bulky tricuspid vegetations in a drug user with S aureus, plus a jet through the leaflet and echolucent tissue at the valve base on TEE.","mech":"Virulent S aureus destroys leaflet tissue, causing perforation, and invades the annulus, forming a perivalvular abscess.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show flow through the leaflet body rather than coaptation, and multiple TEE planes to define the periannular cavity.","trap":"Pulmonic vegetations, VSD rupture and myxoma are not shown; stopping at the vegetation misses complications that change surgical indication."},"XC20_F08":{"clue":"Saccular outpouching of the anterior mitral leaflet in aortic valve endocarditis.","mech":"An infected AR jet striking the anterior leaflet, or spread from the intervalvular fibrosa, weakens it and forms a mycotic pseudoaneurysm that may perforate.","confirm":"TEE with color shows systolic expansion and any flow across the aneurysm wall; perforation shows a jet through the leaflet body.","trap":"Dynamic LVOT obstruction and leaflet fusion are not consequences; heart block relates to septal abscess, not leaflet aneurysm."},"XC20_F09":{"clue":"Echolucent cavity within a thickened mitral-aortic intervalvular fibrosa in aortic valve endocarditis.","mech":"As an abscess cavity breaks down and communicates with the bloodstream, it becomes an echolucent pseudoaneurysm that may pulsate.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show flow into the cavity and systolic expansion; assess for fistula and rupture risk.","trap":"Transverse sinus, LAA thrombus and coronary sinus are anatomically distinct structures, not intra-abscess cavities."},"XC20_F10":{"clue":"Systolic color flow from the LVOT into an echolucent periannular space that expands in systole.","mech":"An abscess cavity that ruptures into a cardiac chamber creates a communication (fistula), so flow follows the systolic pressure gradient.","confirm":"Map the entry and exit points with color and CW Doppler, and look for communication with other chambers.","trap":"Sterile thickening has no flow; normal paravalvular backflow does not enter a cavity; Lambl's excrescence is a filament."},"XC20_F11":{"clue":"Color jet crossing the body of the P1 scallop rather than the coaptation line in mitral endocarditis.","mech":"Infection destroys leaflet tissue and creates a hole, so regurgitation passes through the leaflet itself.","confirm":"Use multiple TEE planes or 3D color to confirm the jet origin away from the coaptation line.","trap":"Annular dilatation, tethering and flail chordae produce regurgitation at the coaptation line."},"XC20_F12":{"clue":"3D color jet from the atrial side through the anterior leaflet with only trivial coaptation-line regurgitation.","mech":"Vegetation-associated leaflet destruction creates a perforation, separating regurgitant flow from the coaptation line.","confirm":"Use 3D en-face views from atrium and ventricle to locate the hole relative to the coaptation line.","trap":"Paravalvular leak needs a prosthesis; annular dilatation causes central coaptation-line MR; ASD flow is interatrial."},"XC20_F13":{"clue":"Heterogeneous posterior annular thickening into the intervalvular fibrosa with no color flow into the lesion.","mech":"An abscess is a contained infected collection; without communication to a chamber, no flow enters it.","confirm":"Image multiple planes with low Nyquist color to exclude flow; repeat TEE or CT if the lesion evolves.","trap":"Fistula and dehiscence require flow; normal postoperative thickening is homogeneous and does not extend into the fibrosa."},"XC20_F14":{"clue":"Regurgitant jets passing outside the prosthetic sewing ring at sites of abnormal annular motion.","mech":"Infection destroys the tissue holding the sutures, so the ring separates from the annulus and rocks, causing paravalvular regurgitation.","confirm":"Color Doppler and 3D TEE localize jet origin outside the ring; look for rocking motion and abscess.","trap":"Washing jets are small, central and inside the ring; central regurgitation passes through leaflets; thrombosis raises gradients."},"XC20_F15":{"clue":"3D TEE surgeon's view with two separate jets outside the mitral sewing ring in a bacteremic patient with heart failure.","mech":"Focal dehiscence lets blood regurgitate around the ring at the detached sites.","confirm":"Use 3D color to localize each jet by clock position, which guides surgical or percutaneous repair.","trap":"Washing jets are inside the ring; LAA thrombus and leaflet stenosis do not produce jets outside the ring."},"XC20_F16":{"clue":"Soft-tissue masses on device leads in the RA, RV and SVC in a bacteremic patient.","mech":"Leads promote fibrin and thrombus; infected and sterile lead masses have similar echogenicity and mobility.","confirm":"Interpret with blood cultures, pocket findings, Duke criteria and PET-CT; TEE is preferred for lead imaging.","trap":"Size does not distinguish infection; TTE is less sensitive than TEE; lead masses are not merely calcification artifacts."},"XC21_F02":{"clue":"Transgastric short-axis with septal flattening at end-systole, giving a D-shaped LV in ventilated ARDS.","mech":"When RV systolic pressure approaches LV pressure, the septum is pushed toward the LV at end-systole, signalling acute cor pulmonale.","confirm":"Assess RV/LV area ratio in the mid-esophageal four-chamber view and eccentricity index; reduce driving pressure and consider prone positioning.","trap":"Volume overload flattens in diastole; hypovolemia produces a small round LV; constriction shows respiratory septal bounce."},"XC21_F03":{"clue":"Dilated RV with no respiratory variation of the SVC in a hypotensive ARDS patient.","mech":"An RV already overloaded cannot handle more volume; fluid increases RV dilation and septal shift, reducing LV filling.","confirm":"Assess RV/LV ratio, septal motion and SVC collapsibility; treat with norepinephrine, ventilator adjustment and prone positioning.","trap":"PLR and IVC-guided fluid ignore the RV; the fixed SVC indicates no preload reserve, so fluid will not help."},"XC21_F04":{"clue":"Agitated saline fills the right heart and then appears in the LA and LV in a hypoxemic ARDS patient.","mech":"High RV and RA pressures from ARDS reverse the interatrial gradient, opening a PFO and allowing right-to-left shunting.","confirm":"Count bubble appearance within three to five cardiac cycles for intracardiac shunt; TEE can visualize PFO flow.","trap":"Lungs filter bubbles; late appearance suggests intrapulmonary shunt; spontaneous echo contrast occurs without injection."},"XC21_F05":{"clue":"Dilated RV before VV ECMO that becomes smaller after starting it in refractory ARDS.","mech":"Correcting hypoxemia and hypercapnia lowers pulmonary vascular resistance, which reduces RV afterload.","confirm":"Compare RV/LV ratio and septal position before and after, and check cannula tip position at the IVC-RA junction.","trap":"Cannula obstruction would worsen RV dilation; VV ECMO does not cause LV distension, which is a VA ECMO issue."},"XC22_F01":{"clue":"Smooth pleural line between rib shadows with horizontal A lines and lung sliding.","mech":"A lines are reverberations between the probe and the highly reflective pleura-air interface, seen when the lung beneath is aerated.","confirm":"Confirm sliding in real time or a seashore sign on M-mode; scan other areas for B lines or consolidation.","trap":"A lines alone do not exclude pneumothorax; sliding must be present."},"XC22_F02":{"clue":"Lung tissue with liver-like echotexture above a pleural effusion in a febrile hypoxemic patient.","mech":"When alveoli fill with exudate, the lung becomes a solid tissue that transmits ultrasound, producing tissue-like echoes.","confirm":"Look for dynamic air bronchograms, irregular shred sign and fluid bronchograms; correlate with clinical and laboratory data.","trap":"Interstitial edema produces B lines without tissue texture; pneumothorax has no lung image; PE infarcts are small and peripheral."},"XC22_F04":{"clue":"Punctate and branching hyperechoic foci inside consolidation that move with respiration.","mech":"Air moving in patent bronchi within consolidation creates dynamic air bronchograms, proving airway patency, which argues against resorptive atelectasis.","confirm":"Observe in real time across breathing; static bronchograms favor atelectasis.","trap":"Absence on M-mode and alignment along the pleura are not diagnostic; effusion occurs with both pneumonia and atelectasis."},"XC22_F05":{"clue":"Well-defined vertical hyperechoic artifacts arising from the pleural line reaching the bottom of the screen.","mech":"Thickened interlobular septa and fluid create reverberations that appear as vertical comet tails called B lines.","confirm":"Count B lines per intercostal space; three or more in at least two regions bilaterally suggests interstitial syndrome.","trap":"Normal lung has A lines; pneumothorax abolishes B lines; effusions are anechoic above the diaphragm."},"XC22_F09":{"clue":"Coalescent vertical artifacts with thickened irregular pleural line and nonhomogeneous distribution.","mech":"ARDS involves inflammation of the pleura and patchy alveolar injury, while cardiogenic edema is hydrostatic and diffuse.","confirm":"Look for spared areas, subpleural consolidations, reduced sliding; assess LV filling pressure with E/e'.","trap":"Homogeneous B lines with thin pleura favor CPE; effusions and E/e' 16 point to cardiogenic causes."},"XC22_F11":{"clue":"Normal A-line pattern in one intercostal space amid diffuse bilateral B lines elsewhere.","mech":"ARDS is patchy, leaving islands of aerated lung, whereas cardiogenic edema is gravity-dependent and diffuse.","confirm":"Scan all regions systematically to map spared areas, pleural thickening and consolidations.","trap":"A spared area does not confirm pneumothorax if sliding is present; effusion is anechoic; CPE rarely spares areas."},"XC23_F01":{"clue":"End-diastolic LV area traced in parasternal short axis at the papillary muscle level in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"Severe preload loss shrinks the LV cavity, but only extreme hypovolemia reduces LVEDA below 5.5 cm2/m2.","confirm":"Combine LVEDA with dynamic indices such as VTI variation or PLR response.","trap":"A normal LVEDA does not exclude fluid responsiveness; larger cutoffs are not validated for hypovolemia."},"XC23_F02":{"clue":"Minimal beat-to-beat LVOT peak velocity variation in a ventilated patient with normal LV function.","mech":"Preload-dependent patients show cyclic stroke volume variation with ventilation; minimal variation argues against preload dependence, so vasoplegia is more likely.","confirm":"Calculate respiratory variation in peak LVOT velocity; under about 12% suggests no preload dependence; assess for vasodilatory shock.","trap":"Hypovolemia would show marked variation; LVOT obstruction and AS have high velocities."},"XC23_F03":{"clue":"Late-peaking, dagger-shaped LVOT CW profile about 4 m/s in a hypercontractile, hypotensive patient.","mech":"Dynamic obstruction worsens when LV cavity becomes smaller; reducing preload brings the septum and anterior leaflet closer.","confirm":"Identify SAM and MR; treat with volume, stopping inotropes, beta-blockade and vasoconstrictors.","trap":"Volume and vasoconstriction help by enlarging the cavity; inotropes worsen obstruction."},"XC23_F04":{"clue":"M-mode over the pleural line with horizontal lines above and below the pleura and no granular pattern.","mech":"Air between the pleural layers prevents sliding, producing a uniform barcode pattern.","confirm":"Search for a lung point to confirm pneumothorax; absence of B lines and lung pulse support it.","trap":"Seashore sign shows granular pattern; lung pulse requires transmitted cardiac motion; sinusoid sign indicates effusion."},"XC24_F02":{"clue":"Markedly dilated right heart with plethoric IVC during PEA arrest.","mech":"Massive PE abruptly raises RV afterload, causing acute RV dilation and venous congestion while LV filling falls.","confirm":"Look for clot in transit and DVT; consider thrombolysis after weighing risks.","trap":"Hypovolemia produces a small heart with flat IVC; tamponade shows effusion; tension pneumothorax shows absent sliding."},"XC25_F01":{"clue":"Transverse probe with marker to the patient's left and screen in the operator's line of sight for right IJV cannulation.","mech":"The short-axis out-of-plane view shows the IJV and carotid side by side, which is easier for novices to orient.","confirm":"Confirm vessel identity with compressibility and color Doppler, and track the needle tip with probe sliding.","trap":"Long-axis in-plane needs more skill; oblique and marker caudad are not standard novice setups."},"XC25_F02":{"clue":"Probe indicator and screen indicator aligned on the same side as the operator's.","mech":"Matching orientation maps physical needle movement to the same direction on screen, making steering intuitive.","confirm":"Touch the probe edge or move the needle and verify the corresponding screen direction before puncture.","trap":"Alignment does not change needle echogenicity, view axis or depth."},"XC25_F03":{"clue":"Needle entering the skin 1 cm from a transverse probe over an IJV about 1 cm deep.","mech":"A steep angle ensures the needle crosses the ultrasound plane near the vessel, so the tip is seen as it enters.","confirm":"Watch for a target sign in the lumen and confirm the guidewire in short and long axis before dilation.","trap":"Shallow angles are used for in-plane approaches; perpendicular insertion risks posterior wall puncture."},"XC25_F04":{"clue":"Needle visualized along its length entering the IJV in long axis.","mech":"The in-plane technique keeps the entire needle within the beam, showing shaft and tip continuously.","confirm":"Keep the probe aligned with the needle; small rotation loses the needle image.","trap":"Long axis does not always show the carotid, still requires probe manipulation, and guidewire confirmation remains mandatory."},"XC25_F06":{"clue":"Probe rotated to an oblique view with the needle in-plane toward the IJV and the carotid deeper.","mech":"The oblique view combines in-plane needle visualization with the short-axis advantage of seeing adjacent structures.","confirm":"Keep the needle in-plane while maintaining carotid visualization.","trap":"Purely long-axis views hide the carotid; the oblique view does not remove it from the field."},"XC25_F08":{"clue":"Short-axis view of the CFV with the femoral artery and saphenofemoral junction marked.","mech":"Puncture at the CFV below the inguinal ligament and above the saphenofemoral junction avoids arterial puncture and junction tears.","confirm":"Identify the CFV, femoral artery and saphenofemoral junction, then use a steep angle out-of-plane.","trap":"Puncture distal to the junction risks superficial femoral vessels; above the inguinal ligament risks retroperitoneal bleeding."},"XC25_F12":{"clue":"Needle appearing as an echogenic dot within the vessel lumen in cross-section during peripheral IV.","mech":"In the out-of-plane approach the beam crosses the needle, producing a bright dot rather than a line.","confirm":"Fan or slide the probe to confirm the dot is the needle tip.","trap":"In-plane approaches show the needle along its length."},"XC25_F14":{"clue":"Roll under the extended wrist and screen positioned in the operator's line of sight.","mech":"Wrist extension brings the radial artery superficial and straight; ergonomic setup allows simultaneous view of puncture site and screen.","confirm":"Use a high-frequency linear probe in transverse view and verify arterial pulsation.","trap":"The roll is not for an Allen test; the probe is placed over the radial artery, not the ulna."},"XC25_F16":{"clue":"Echogenic dot above the radial artery during steep out-of-plane cannulation.","mech":"Out of plane, any section of the needle produces a dot; the true tip is located only by tracking the dot distally.","confirm":"Fan or slide the probe toward the needle tip, then advance stepwise, keeping the tip in view.","trap":"Gain increases artifacts, color Doppler does not identify the tip, and blind advancement risks posterior wall puncture."},"XC25_F18":{"clue":"Survey scan along the femoral artery before cannulation, showing its course toward the umbilicus.","mech":"The femoral artery runs obliquely, not parallel to the thigh axis; a needle aimed along the true course stays within the vessel and avoids sidewall exit.","confirm":"Trace the artery proximally and distally in short axis, mark its trajectory on the skin, and puncture below the inguinal ligament aligned with that line.","trap":"The saphenofemoral junction is a venous landmark to avoid, and peak velocity or vein compression does not guide arterial needle direction."},"XC26_F04":{"clue":"Bright horizontal line deep to the intercostal muscle and between rib shadows on a linear probe.","mech":"Without pleural fluid, parietal and visceral pleurae are in contact and their tissue-air interface reflects strongly as one hyperechoic line.","confirm":"Identify the bat sign (two rib shadows with pleural line between) and look for sliding; fluid would separate the layers.","trap":"Periosteum lies at rib level with a shadow beneath; A lines repeat deeper at equal intervals; muscle fascia is superficial to the pleura."},"XC26_F05":{"clue":"Effusion with fibrin strands and septations dividing it into loculations in a febrile patient.","mech":"Inflammatory exudate deposits fibrin that organizes into septations; transudates lack the protein and inflammation needed to form them.","confirm":"Sample the fluid for pH, glucose, LDH and culture, and plan chest tube with fibrinolytics or surgical drainage if loculated.","trap":"Anechoic does not mean transudate, but septated always means exudate; a hematocrit sign is dependent layering, not septation."},"XC26_F06":{"clue":"Post-trauma effusion with dependent posterior fluid more echogenic than fluid above.","mech":"Cellular components of blood settle by gravity, producing a layered echogenicity gradient within free-flowing fluid.","confirm":"Change patient position to show layering shift, measure effusion size and correlate with hemoglobin and chest drain output.","trap":"Septations indicate empyema, not layering; transudates are anechoic; chylothorax is usually homogeneous."},"XC26_F07":{"clue":"Maximal parietal-to-visceral pleural separation at the lung base of 2.92 cm.","mech":"In a supine patient, fluid accumulates posteriorly; separation at the base correlates with total volume.","confirm":"Balik: V (mL) = 20 x separation (mm) = 20 x 29.2 = 584 mL.","trap":"Using centimetres (2.92) or wrong multipliers gives 292 or 876 mL; convert to millimetres first."},"XC26_F10":{"clue":"Hyperechoic linear structure within an anechoic effusion during pleural catheter insertion.","mech":"The metal guidewire is a strong specular reflector, seen as a bright line within fluid.","confirm":"Visualize the wire in the effusion before dilating; confirm away from lung and diaphragm.","trap":"A septation would attach to pleura; diaphragm and liver lie deeper; subcutaneous emphysema causes artifacts superficially."},"XC27_F04":{"clue":"Cricoid disappearing in the transverse plane as the probe slides cephalad, marking the cricothyroid membrane.","mech":"The cricothyroid membrane lies between the cricoid and thyroid cartilages; ultrasound visualizes cartilage borders that palpation misses in obese or distorted necks.","confirm":"Mark the membrane in transverse and longitudinal planes before intubation attempts.","trap":"Palpation is not more accurate; ultrasound images cartilage well and is useful in all patients with poorly defined landmarks."},"XC27_F05":{"clue":"Transverse views through the thyroid cartilage showing vocal ligaments and arytenoids moving.","mech":"Tilting the probe cephalad aligns the beam with the glottic plane, improving visualization of vocal ligament motion.","confirm":"Ask the patient to phonate and watch the ligaments adduct toward the midline symmetrically.","trap":"Longitudinal views over the cricoid or the tracheal rings do not show the vocal cords."},"XC27_F08":{"clue":"Inability to visualize the hyoid bone (H) on sublingual ultrasound.","mech":"Failure to see the hyoid suggests altered airway anatomy, such as a large tongue or short neck, associated with difficult laryngoscopy.","confirm":"Combine sonographic airway indices with clinical predictors and prepare a difficult airway plan.","trap":"Hyoid visualization does not predict postextubation stridor, esophageal intubation or vocal cord paralysis."},"XC27_F09":{"clue":"Structure posterolateral to the left thyroid lobe at the level of the first tracheal rings.","mech":"The esophagus is a muscular tube that shows peristalsis with swallowing.","confirm":"Ask the patient to swallow and watch lumen movement, best seen longitudinally.","trap":"Pulsatile flow identifies the carotid; comet tails arise from tracheal air; collapse with inspiration suggests a vein."},"XC27_F11":{"clue":"Paired hyperechoic lines within the tracheal air column after emergency intubation.","mech":"The ETT walls are strong reflectors seen within the trachea; a second air-mucosa interface posterolateral would indicate esophageal intubation.","confirm":"Check for bilateral lung sliding and diaphragmatic movement with ventilation.","trap":"Calcified rings are external; a double tract sign indicates esophageal intubation."},"XC27_F12":{"clue":"Laryngeal air column widens and becomes trapezoidal after cuff deflation.","mech":"Air passing around the tube indicates adequate space at the glottis, so laryngeal edema is unlikely.","confirm":"Measure air column width before and after deflation; a difference above 1.6 mm suggests low stridor risk.","trap":"A wide column does not indicate high stridor risk, esophageal displacement or vocal cord paralysis."},"XC28_F04":{"clue":"Traditional paracentesis sites: midline infraumbilical and bilateral lower quadrants.","mech":"The left lower quadrant has thinner abdominal wall and deeper fluid pocket, with less risk from a distended cecum.","confirm":"Use ultrasound to locate the largest pocket and avoid vessels and bowel.","trap":"Above the umbilicus or over the ASIS is not standard; flank sites risk bowel puncture."},"XC28_F05":{"clue":"Anechoic fluid outlining the liver and tracking toward the hepatorenal space in cirrhosis.","mech":"Free peritoneal fluid collects in dependent spaces such as Morison's pouch.","confirm":"Scan RUQ, LUQ and pelvis to identify the largest pocket.","trap":"Pleural fluid lies above the diaphragm; subcapsular hematoma lies within the capsule; the gallbladder is a discrete organ."},"XC28_F07":{"clue":"Echogenic bowel loops floating within large anechoic pelvic fluid.","mech":"Large-volume ascites allows bowel loops to float freely within fluid.","confirm":"Identify the largest pocket and confirm needle path avoiding bowel.","trap":"Bladder is a single thick-walled structure; ovarian cysts and abscesses are discrete masses."},"XC28_F08":{"clue":"Anechoic thick-walled oval structure surrounded by free fluid in the lower abdomen.","mech":"The urinary bladder is a fluid-filled structure that can be mistaken for ascites.","confirm":"Have the patient void or insert a catheter, then rescan.","trap":"Puncturing the largest anechoic area risks bladder injury; this is not loculation."},"XC28_F10":{"clue":"Color flow within the abdominal wall at a planned paracentesis site.","mech":"The inferior epigastric vessels run deep to the rectus abdominis about 4-6 cm from the midline; laceration causes abdominal wall or intraperitoneal hemorrhage, the most serious paracentesis complication.","confirm":"Move the entry site laterally, rescan with color Doppler before puncture.","trap":"Z-track reduces leaks, not bleeding; larger catheters increase the risk."},"XC28_F13":{"clue":"Needle seen only as a bright dot in transverse orientation during paracentesis.","mech":"Out of plane, only a cross-section of the needle is visible, so the dot may be the shaft rather than the tip.","confirm":"Move the probe in tandem with the needle to keep the tip in view.","trap":"Fixed probe or steep advancement loses the tip; depth increase and color Doppler do not help track the tip."},"XC28_F15":{"clue":"Needle aligned with the long axis of the beam during paracentesis.","mech":"In-plane alignment shows the entire needle course within the thin beam.","confirm":"Keep the probe still and align the needle carefully within the beam.","trap":"In-plane is harder to maintain; it does not show only the tip; sterility is still required."},"XC29_F02":{"clue":"Agitated saline through the pericardiocentesis needle creates an echogenic cloud in the pericardial space.","mech":"Microbubbles are strong reflectors, so their appearance in the effusion confirms needle position.","confirm":"Inject saline, confirm bubbles in the pericardial space, then drain and verify RV re-expansion.","trap":"Bubbles in the RV indicate intracardiac puncture; pleural puncture shows bubbles outside the pericardium."},"XC32_F08":{"clue":"Multiple vertical hyperechoic artifacts merging from the pleural line with probe parallel to ribs.","mech":"Interstitial fluid and septal thickening create confluent B lines.","confirm":"Rotate the probe parallel to ribs to avoid shadowing in narrow intercostal spaces.","trap":"A lines indicate aerated lung; lung point indicates pneumothorax; hepatization is consolidation."},"XC32_F10":{"clue":"High-resolution linear image showing septations not seen on curvilinear scan.","mech":"Higher-frequency linear probes provide better spatial resolution, revealing septations.","confirm":"Use the linear probe for superficial structures and match probe to patient size.","trap":"Simple transudates are anechoic; hepatized lung is consolidated tissue."},"XC33_F01":{"clue":"IVC markedly larger than the aorta in subcostal transverse view in a child.","mech":"Volume overload distends the IVC relative to the aorta.","confirm":"Calculate IVC:Ao ratio and correlate with clinical findings.","trap":"Small or flattened IVC indicates hypovolemia; coarctation is not assessed by IVC size."},"XC33_F02":{"clue":"Dilated RV with septum bowing into the LV in a hypoxemic child.","mech":"High pulmonary pressures overload the RV, pushing the septum leftward.","confirm":"Estimate RVSP using TR velocity and assess RV function.","trap":"Hypovolemia shows a small LV and RV; tamponade shows effusion; DCM dilates the LV."},"XC33_F03":{"clue":"Improved RV and RA dilation after treatment in pediatric ARDS.","mech":"Inhaled nitric oxide lowers pulmonary vascular resistance, and inotropes support RV contractility.","confirm":"Repeat echo to assess RV size and function after therapy.","trap":"Fluid loading worsens RV dilation; PEEP increases RV afterload."},"XC34_F01":{"clue":"PLAX caliper measurement of LVOT diameter plus apical 5-chamber PW VTI.","mech":"LVOT area times VTI equals stroke volume.","confirm":"CSA = pi x (D/2) squared; SV = CSA x VTI.","trap":"AVA requires VTI across the valve; peak gradient uses CW velocity; EF uses volumes."},"XC34_F02":{"clue":"M-mode at the lateral tricuspid annulus measuring systolic excursion.","mech":"TAPSE reflects RV longitudinal systolic function.","confirm":"Measure excursion with cursor aligned to annular motion; TAPSE below 17 mm suggests dysfunction in adults.","trap":"TAPSE does not measure diastolic filling, PASP or RAP."},"XC34_F03":{"clue":"TR peak velocity 4.74 m/s with gradient 90 mm Hg and RAP 5 mm Hg.","mech":"Bernoulli equation estimates RV-RA gradient; adding RAP gives RVSP.","confirm":"RVSP = 5 + 4 x (4.74) squared = about 95 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting to add RAP gives 90; using velocity without squaring gives wrong values."},"XC34_F04":{"clue":"Flattened interventricular septum producing D-shaped LV in a neonate.","mech":"Elevated RV pressure pushes the septum toward the LV.","confirm":"Estimate pulmonary pressures via TR jet and PDA shunt direction.","trap":"LV dysfunction and hypovolemia do not cause septal flattening; L-R ductal shunt causes LV volume overload."},"XC34_F05":{"clue":"Circular LV cross-section with M-mode at the papillary muscle level.","mech":"PSAX shows the LV in cross-section, allowing perpendicular M-mode measurement.","confirm":"Measure LVIDd and LVIDs to calculate fractional shortening.","trap":"Apical and subcostal views do not show circular LV cross-section."},"XC34_F06":{"clue":"2D and color images showing MPA, RPA and an adjacent vessel in a preterm infant.","mech":"High parasternal view displays the ductus alongside the pulmonary artery.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to assess ductal flow direction and velocity.","trap":"Apical and subcostal views do not optimally show the ductus."},"XC34_F07":{"clue":"Diastolic turbulent flow in the main pulmonary artery of a preterm neonate.","mech":"Continuous ductal flow enters the PA during diastole.","confirm":"Use color Doppler in ductal view to confirm PDA.","trap":"Pulmonary stenosis gives systolic turbulence; pulmonary regurgitation is also diastolic but originates at the valve, whereas ductal flow enters near the bifurcation and is typically continuous."},"XC34_F09":{"clue":"E > A mitral inflow in a preterm neonate with large ductal shunt.","mech":"Increased pulmonary venous return raises LA pressure, pseudonormalizing E/A.","confirm":"Assess LA:Ao ratio and descending aortic diastolic flow.","trap":"Restrictive filling and stenosis are not implied; ductal closure would reduce LA filling."},"XC34_F10":{"clue":"Retrograde descending aortic flow throughout diastole in a neonate.","mech":"A large PDA steals blood from the systemic circulation in diastole.","confirm":"Assess ductal size and shunt direction, LA:Ao ratio and mitral inflow.","trap":"Aortic stenosis and coarctation do not produce holodiastolic reversal."},"XC35_F01":{"clue":"Abrupt transition between sparse and compact B lines in a term neonate born by cesarean section.","mech":"Delayed clearance of lung fluid creates differential aeration, producing a double lung point.","confirm":"Scan anterior and lateral fields to map the boundary.","trap":"RDS shows white lung everywhere; pneumothorax lacks B lines; meconium aspiration shows consolidations."},"XC35_F02":{"clue":"Large irregular subpleural consolidation with compact B lines in a term neonate.","mech":"Meconium causes chemical pneumonitis and airway obstruction, producing consolidation.","confirm":"Correlate with history of meconium-stained fluid and scan all fields.","trap":"Double lung point indicates TTN; normal A lines indicate aerated lung."},"XC35_F03":{"clue":"Confluent B lines throughout all scanned areas in a preterm neonate.","mech":"Surfactant deficiency causes diffuse alveolar collapse and interstitial fluid.","confirm":"Scan all fields, look for absence of spared areas, consider surfactant therapy.","trap":"TTN shows double lung point; pneumothorax shows absent sliding."},"XC35_F04":{"clue":"Irregular pleural line with compact B lines in a young infant with viral prodrome.","mech":"Viral inflammation affects the airways and interstitium, producing pleural irregularity and B lines.","confirm":"Score findings by region; severity correlates with clinical severity.","trap":"Lung point indicates pneumothorax; effusion and normal A lines are not typical of bronchiolitis."},"XC35_F06":{"clue":"0.25 cm caliper between two parallel hyperechoic lines at the midaxillary 9th-10th intercostal space at end-expiration.","mech":"At the zone of apposition the hypoechoic diaphragm muscle lies between the bright pleural and peritoneal membranes, so their separation is muscle thickness.","confirm":"Use a linear probe perpendicular to the chest wall, measure inner to outer border at end-expiration, then repeat at end-inspiration for thickening fraction.","trap":"Excursion is measured subcostally with a phased array and M-mode; an effusion would be anechoic fluid above the diaphragm, not a thin layered band."},"XC35_F07":{"clue":"Zone-of-apposition M-mode: 0.34 cm at maximal inspiration, 0.27 cm at end-expiration.","mech":"Active contraction shortens and thickens diaphragm fibers, so fractional thickening reflects contractility.","confirm":"TF = (0.34 - 0.27)/0.27 = 0.26, about 26%; interpret with patient effort and ventilator support level.","trap":"Dividing by the inspiratory value gives about 21%, and using the ratio of thicknesses gives 79%; the denominator is end-expiratory thickness."},"XC35_F08":{"clue":"Phased array M-mode below the right costal margin with a 2.2 cm positive inspiratory deflection.","mech":"Diaphragmatic contraction moves the dome caudally toward the probe, producing an upward M-mode deflection whose amplitude is excursion.","confirm":"Align the M-mode cursor parallel to diaphragmatic motion through the liver window and measure peak-to-baseline during quiet and deep breaths.","trap":"Thickening fraction is measured at the zone of apposition with a linear probe; IVC collapsibility uses the IVC, not the diaphragm dome."},"XC36_F04":{"clue":"Dilated fluid-filled bowel loops with abnormally echogenic walls in a preterm neonate with feeding intolerance.","mech":"Mucosal injury and inflammation in NEC cause ileus with fluid-filled loops, and edema or inflammatory infiltration increases wall echogenicity.","confirm":"Measure wall thickness, look for pneumatosis, portal venous gas, free fluid and assess bowel wall perfusion with color Doppler.","trap":"Normal collapsed bowel has thin walls with peristalsis; intussusception shows a target mass; free gas is echogenic foci outside bowel."},"XC36_F05":{"clue":"Calipered homogeneously thickened neonatal bowel wall in suspected NEC.","mech":"Edema and inflammation thicken the bowel wall early in NEC, before necrosis thins it.","confirm":"Bowel wall over 2.5 mm is concerning; combine with echogenicity, pneumatosis and Doppler perfusion findings.","trap":"1.0 mm is the threshold for pathological thinning, not thickening; higher cutoffs miss early disease."},"XC36_F06":{"clue":"Focal bowel wall thinning with heterogeneous contents and surrounding free fluid in known NEC.","mech":"Transmural necrosis destroys wall layers, so the wall becomes thin, often under 1 mm, preceding perforation.","confirm":"Check color Doppler for absent wall perfusion and look for echogenic free fluid or free gas; involve surgeons early.","trap":"Thinning after thickening is not resolution of edema; normal peristalsis changes wall thickness transiently but not with this context."},"XC36_F07":{"clue":"Hyperechoic granular foci within the bowel wall with posterior reverberation in a preterm neonate with bloody stools.","mech":"Gas-forming bacteria invade the injured mucosa and produce intramural gas, which reflects strongly and reverberates.","confirm":"Distinguish intramural from intraluminal gas by its location within the wall layers; look for portal venous gas.","trap":"Calcifications shadow rather than reverberate; intraluminal feeds and gas sit centrally within the lumen, not in the wall."},"XC36_F08":{"clue":"Linear, branching and punctate echogenic foci within the hepatic parenchyma in a neonate with NEC.","mech":"Intramural gas enters mesenteric veins and travels to the portal branches, appearing as moving echogenic foci in the liver.","confirm":"Watch for moving bright bubbles in the portal vein in real time; Doppler shows spiky artifacts from bubbles.","trap":"Calcifications are static and shadow; sludge sits in the gallbladder; abscesses are focal masses."},"XC36_F10":{"clue":"Echogenic free fluid with debris between abdominal wall and liver in a deteriorating neonate with NEC.","mech":"Perforation releases bowel content and inflammatory exudate, producing complex rather than anechoic free fluid.","confirm":"Search for free gas anterior to the liver and surgical review.","trap":"Simple ascites is anechoic; physiological fluid is minimal; subcapsular hematoma is contained by the capsule."},"XC36_F11":{"clue":"Small echogenic foci in the peritoneal space anterior to bowel near the abdominal wall.","mech":"Perforation releases gas that rises to the nondependent anterior abdomen and sits outside the bowel wall.","confirm":"Scan the anterior abdomen with a linear probe and look for enhanced peritoneal stripe and reverberation.","trap":"Intramural gas lies within the wall, portal gas within the liver; calcified meconium shadows."},"XC36_F12":{"clue":"Y-shaped and circumferential ring color flow in thickened neonatal bowel.","mech":"Inflammatory vasodilatation in early NEC increases mural flow, showing that bowel is still perfused and viable.","confirm":"Compare with adjacent loops and repeat Doppler; absence of flow in a loop suggests necrosis.","trap":"Transmural necrosis shows absent flow; volvulus is diagnosed by mesenteric vessel orientation; normal neonates do not show these patterns."},"XC36_F13":{"clue":"Central bowel loop with absent flow, wall thinning and pneumatosis flanked by hyperemic loops.","mech":"Necrotic bowel has no viable perfusion, so color flow is absent while adjacent inflamed loops are hyperemic.","confirm":"Use low velocity scale color settings to confirm absent flow; correlate with surgical findings.","trap":"Hyperemia in some loops does not imply viability of all; intussusception produces a target mass."},"XC36_F14":{"clue":"Hyperechoic herringbone appearance of small bowel with hyperemic flow in neonate with NEC.","mech":"Thickened, edematous valvulae with increased flow produce a zebra pattern seen in inflamed bowel.","confirm":"Compare with normal neonatal bowel, assess wall thickness and perfusion patterns.","trap":"Intramural gas is granular with reverberation; portal gas is in the liver; normal bowel lacks this pattern."},"XC37_F01":{"clue":"Operator's hand resting on face or towel while scanning the closed eyelid with a linear probe.","mech":"Stabilizing the hand prevents globe compression, which could distort measurements and is harmful in globe injury.","confirm":"Use a generous gel layer and keep probe pressure minimal; check measurements in both planes.","trap":"Compression degrades images and risks injury; probe angle and gel spread are not the purpose."},"XC37_F02":{"clue":"Line 1 at 3 mm behind the globe and line 2 measuring 4.3 mm across the optic nerve and sheath.","mech":"The retrobulbar optic nerve sheath is distensible; raised ICP transmitted via CSF expands it, most at 3 mm behind the globe.","confirm":"Measure ONSD 3 mm behind the globe perpendicular to the nerve in two planes and average both eyes.","trap":"Measuring the nerve alone underestimates; lens and retina are anterior structures; measuring in wrong position introduces error."},"XC37_F03":{"clue":"Pupil diameter shrinking from 0.37 to 0.15 cm on coronal ocular ultrasound in a sedated patient with periorbital swelling.","mech":"Light stimulation constricts the pupil via the pupillary light reflex, visible sonographically through closed or swollen eyelids.","confirm":"Shine light into the contralateral or ipsilateral eye and measure pupil diameter changes in the coronal plane.","trap":"ONSD measures the optic nerve sheath; lens dislocation and anterior chamber depth do not involve pupil size."},"XC37_F05":{"clue":"Taut, bright linear opacity in the posterior globe tethered to the optic disc.","mech":"The retina is anchored at the optic disc and ora serrata, so a detached retina stays fixed to the disc.","confirm":"Check movement with eye motion; retinal detachments move less and remain tethered.","trap":"Vitreous detachment is mobile and not tethered; vitreous hemorrhage shows wavy strands."},"XC37_F06":{"clue":"Layering retinal hemorrhages and a macular retinoschisis cavity in an infant with suspected abuse.","mech":"Repetitive acceleration-deceleration forces shear retinal layers, causing hemorrhages and schisis.","confirm":"Ophthalmology review, fundoscopy and imaging for intracranial injury.","trap":"Papilledema causes optic nerve sheath bulging; normal retina lacks layering hemorrhages."},"XC37_F07":{"clue":"Optic nerve sheath bulging into the posterior pole of the globe in a child with altered mental status.","mech":"Raised ICP expands the sheath and causes papilledema, visible as bulging of the optic disc.","confirm":"Measure ONSD and correlate with clinical signs and imaging.","trap":"Retinal hemorrhages and detachment are different findings; normal disc is flat."},"XC38_F01":{"clue":"Neonatal lower-extremity PICC visible at the IVC-RA junction on long-axis IVC view.","mech":"Ideal central venous catheter tip position for lower-extremity lines is at the IVC-RA junction.","confirm":"Use longitudinal IVC view to confirm tip, and adjust if too deep or too shallow.","trap":"SVC position applies to upper-extremity lines; hepatic or portal veins are malpositioned."},"XC38_F03":{"clue":"UVC tip well within the right atrium on longitudinal IVC view.","mech":"Catheter contact with atrial wall can perforate, causing pericardial effusion, tamponade or arrhythmias.","confirm":"Withdraw to ductus venosus at its junction with the IVC-RA and re-image.","trap":"Hepatic necrosis, ascites and portal thrombosis are complications of low malposition."},"XC38_F05":{"clue":"Celiac trunk and SMA arising from the aorta while confirming a high umbilical arterial catheter.","mech":"Seeing visceral branches confirms aortic position; a high UAC tip should lie above them in the thoracic aorta.","confirm":"Place tip at T6-T9, above the celiac, SMA and renal origins.","trap":"The low position is L3-L4, and the SMA origin or between renals and bifurcation is unsafe."},"XC38_F07":{"clue":"Small pulsatile vessel flanked by two veins in the abdominal wall above ascites.","mech":"The inferior epigastric artery runs deep to the lateral rectus abdominis about 4-6 cm from midline.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to identify and avoid vessels before needle insertion.","trap":"Fluid pocket selection and bladder exclusion are separate goals."},"XC38_F08":{"clue":"Transverse suprasternal view showing trachea, esophagus and carotid during airway management.","mech":"Real-time imaging shows tube passage into the trachea; a second air-mucosa interface signals esophageal intubation.","confirm":"Watch tracheal flutter during insertion and absence of double tract sign.","trap":"This view does not measure tube depth relative to the carina or detect mainstem intubation."},"XC38_F09":{"clue":"Absent or paradoxical cephalad diaphragm movement during positive pressure ventilation after intubation.","mech":"With esophageal intubation, ventilation does not inflate the lungs, so the diaphragm does not move caudally.","confirm":"Look for bilateral lung sliding and use tracheal view and capnography.","trap":"Unilateral excursion suggests mainstem intubation; diaphragm thickness does not confirm placement."},"XC39_F02":{"clue":"Flattened oval antrum with target appearance deep to the liver and superficial to SMA and IVC.","mech":"An empty antrum collapses, showing layered gastric walls as a bull's eye.","confirm":"Scan in supine and right lateral decubitus positions; measure CSA if needed.","trap":"Liquid distends the antrum; solids cause heterogeneous echogenicity and shadowing."},"XC39_F03":{"clue":"Heterogeneous echogenic antral mass with shadowing obscuring the posterior wall.","mech":"Solid food particles reflect and absorb ultrasound, causing heterogeneous echoes and posterior shadowing.","confirm":"Scan in right lateral decubitus position, and treat as full stomach.","trap":"Empty antrum is flat; clear fluid is anechoic; wall thickening lacks shadowing."},"XC39_F04":{"clue":"Distended antrum with hypoechoic center and increased echogenicity behind it.","mech":"Liquid transmits ultrasound with little attenuation, producing posterior enhancement.","confirm":"Measure antral CSA and calculate volume in right lateral decubitus.","trap":"Solids cause shadowing; air causes reverberation; empty antrum is flat."},"XC39_F05":{"clue":"Hypoechoic cord surrounded by CSF tapering caudally into fibrillar strands on infant sagittal spinal scan.","mech":"The cord tapers to the conus medullaris and continues as cauda equina nerve roots.","confirm":"Identify vertebral level of the conus by counting from the lumbosacral junction.","trap":"Dural sac terminates lower; filum cyst is a small anechoic cavity; sacral hiatus is bony."},"XC39_F06":{"clue":"Needle passing between posterior and anterior sacrum into the caudal epidural space.","mech":"The sacral hiatus is covered by the sacrococcygeal ligament; piercing it gives access to the epidural space.","confirm":"Visualize the ligament and needle tip, and observe spread of local anesthetic.","trap":"Ligamentum flavum and supraspinous ligament are lumbar structures; the dura should not be pierced."},"XC39_F07":{"clue":"Sagittal probe below the lateral clavicle with axillary artery deep to pectoralis muscles and cords around it.","mech":"Infraclavicular block targets the cords of the brachial plexus around the axillary artery.","confirm":"Deposit local anesthetic posterior to the axillary artery to spread around all cords.","trap":"Interscalene and supraclavicular blocks target roots or trunks in the neck; axillary block targets terminal nerves."},"XC39_F08":{"clue":"Transverse probe below the inguinal crease with femoral nerve lateral to the artery beneath the fascia iliaca.","mech":"The femoral nerve lies deep to the fascia iliaca; local anesthetic must spread in this compartment.","confirm":"Visualize the nerve, artery and fascia iliaca, and watch the spread around the nerve.","trap":"Superficial to the fascia lata or within the artery sheath misses the nerve."},"XC39_F09":{"clue":"Sciatic nerve deep to gluteus maximus between two bony prominences at the gluteal crease.","mech":"The nerve lies at the midpoint between the greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity.","confirm":"Scan transversely to identify the nerve and its relation to bony landmarks.","trap":"ASIS and PSIS landmarks are for other blocks; popliteal landmarks are distal."},"XC39_F10":{"clue":"Sciatic nerve superficial to popliteal artery on posterior thigh scan.","mech":"Blocking at the confluence of tibial and common peroneal nerves anesthetizes both branches.","confirm":"Slide cephalad to find the confluence and inject there.","trap":"Injecting at the crease or below the fibular head blocks one branch; gluteal level is a different approach."},"XC39_F11":{"clue":"Transverse view lateral to midline with rectus abdominis above the posterior rectus sheath.","mech":"Terminal thoracolumbar nerves run between rectus and posterior sheath; local anesthetic placed there blocks them.","confirm":"Watch local anesthetic spread in the plane between muscle and sheath.","trap":"Superficial placement misses nerves; TAP block targets internal oblique-transversus plane; intraperitoneal injection is unsafe."},"L17_1":{"clue":"Many acute afterload insults reproduce the same RV picture as PE.","mech":"The RV responds stereotypically to an acute rise in afterload or loss of contractility: dilation, septal flattening and dysfunction, whatever the cause.","confirm":"Look for clues to cause: inferior LV RWMA (RV infarct), ventilator pressures (ARDS), RVH (chronic), clot in transit or DVT (PE); confirm PE with CTPA.","trap":"Treating every dilated RV as PE leads to inappropriate lysis; tamponade compresses rather than dilates the RV."},"L17_2":{"clue":"Mid-free-wall RV akinesis with preserved apical motion.","mech":"The apex may be tethered to a hyperdynamic LV, so it moves passively while the mid free wall fails under acute afterload; the mechanism also appears in RV infarction.","confirm":"Look in an RV-focused A4C; integrate with RV/LV ratio, TR velocity, DVT and clinical probability. Sensitivity about 77%, specificity about 94%.","trap":"McConnell sign is not pathognomonic for PE; RV infarction produces it too, and its absence does not exclude PE."},"L17_3":{"clue":"Mid-systolic notching of the RVOT PW envelope.","mech":"Raised PVR and low PA compliance cause early reflected pressure waves that decelerate mid-systolic flow, producing a notch and shortened acceleration time.","confirm":"PW in RVOT just below the pulmonic valve in PSAX; measure acceleration time (below 100 ms abnormal) and look for notching; combine with TR gradient (60/60 sign).","trap":"Pulmonic stenosis gives high velocity at the valve, not a mid-systolic notch; normal flow has a smooth, rounded envelope."},"L17_4":{"clue":"RVOT acceleration time 90 ms.","mech":"As PA pressure rises, PA compliance falls and peak velocity is reached earlier, shortening acceleration time in an inverse relationship.","confirm":"mPAP = 80 - (AT/2) = 80 - 45 = 35 mm Hg. Check heart rate (AT shortens with tachycardia) and sample just proximal to the pulmonic valve.","trap":"Using AT/2 alone (45) forgets the subtraction; 80 mm Hg forgets AT entirely. Note this simplified formula is usually attributed to Mahan rather than Dabestani."},"L17_5":{"clue":"Peak (early-diastolic) PR velocity 2.7 m/s with RAP 11 mm Hg.","mech":"Early-diastolic PR velocity reflects the PA-RV diastolic gradient near peak, approximating mean PA pressure when added to RAP.","confirm":"mPAP = 4 x 2.7 squared + 11 = 4 x 7.29 + 11 = 29 + 11 = about 40 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting RAP gives 29; using end-diastolic PR velocity estimates PA diastolic pressure, not mean."},"L17_6":{"clue":"Wide-open TR with RA-RV equalisation.","mech":"In severe TR the RA ventricularises; the RV-RA gradient is small and non-Bernoulli (laminar, low velocity), and RA pressure is high and hard to estimate, so RVSP is underestimated.","confirm":"Look for dense triangular early-peaking CW TR signal; use other PH clues (RV size, septal shape, PA acceleration time, PR velocity).","trap":"TR velocity is not always above 5 m/s; aliasing does not affect CW; angle alone is not the issue."},"L17_7":{"clue":"DVT on ultrasound in a patient with suspected PE.","mech":"Most PEs arise from lower-limb DVT; finding DVT confirms venous thromboembolism, which requires anticoagulation regardless of whether PE is proven.","confirm":"Perform proximal compression ultrasound; if DVT found and no contraindication, start anticoagulation.","trap":"Requiring CT before treatment delays therapy; a negative DVT scan does not exclude PE."},"L17_8":{"clue":"Unstable patient with mobile thrombus in the RA.","mech":"Clot in transit carries high immediate risk of fatal embolisation; systemic lysis or embolectomy removes the threat quickly.","confirm":"Confirm mobile thrombus on echo; give systemic thrombolysis or arrange surgical/catheter embolectomy depending on resources.","trap":"Heparin alone or IVC filter does not address an embolus already in the heart."},"L17_9":{"clue":"Small peripheral pleural-based hypoechoic lesions in dorsal lower lobes with effusion.","mech":"Emboli cause infarcts in peripheral lung, especially in dependent lobes; infarcts are airless and often wedge-shaped.","confirm":"Scan posterior and lateral bases; look for multiple subpleural lesions and adjacent effusion; confirm PE with CTPA.","trap":"Diffuse B-lines suggest oedema; large central masses are tumours; A-lines only indicate normal aeration."},"L17_10":{"clue":"Negative 60/60 sign in a hypotensive patient with alternative possibilities.","mech":"The 60/60 sign is specific but insensitive (about 25%), so a negative result cannot exclude PE; other causes like mechanical MI complications must be considered.","confirm":"Complete the focused exam: RV, LV wall motion, effusion, IVC, valves (papillary rupture, VSD) and DVT before deciding on lysis or IABP.","trap":"Treating a negative 60/60 sign as exclusion of PE is wrong; lysis without diagnosis risks bleeding in a recent MI patient on heparin."},"L18_1":{"clue":"Aortic wall layers from inside out.","mech":"Intima lines the lumen, media contains elastic fibres, adventitia provides outer connective tissue; dissection splits the media.","confirm":"Remember: intima, media, adventitia; dissection flap is intima plus inner media separating true and false lumens.","trap":"Cardiac layers (endocardium, myocardium, epicardium) are not aortic layers; reversing the order is a simple slip."},"L18_2":{"clue":"Assessing AR mechanism and intraoperative availability in dissection.","mech":"Echo visualises valve morphology and regurgitant jet in real time and can be done in the operating room; CT and MRI cannot.","confirm":"Use TEE to define AR mechanism (annular dilation, cusp prolapse, flap prolapse), effusion and coronary involvement intraoperatively.","trap":"CT is excellent for side branches and follow-up; CXR is insensitive; MRI is not practical in unstable patients."},"L18_3":{"clue":"True lumen expands in systole with forward flow; false lumen larger with slow flow.","mech":"The true lumen receives direct ventricular ejection, expanding in systole; the false lumen fills through tears with slower flow, thrombosis and SEC.","confirm":"Use M-mode and colour Doppler to show systolic expansion and early systolic forward flow in the true lumen.","trap":"False lumen expands in diastole; spontaneous contrast indicates slow flow in the false lumen, not the true lumen."},"L18_4":{"clue":"Ascending aneurysm larger than 6.0 cm: cumulative rupture or dissection risk.","mech":"Wall stress rises with diameter (Laplace law); around 6 cm the aorta reaches a hinge point beyond which rupture risk escalates.","confirm":"Recall cumulative risk about 31% at this size; operate at 5.5 cm in general population and earlier with risk factors.","trap":"5% and 16% understate the risk; 0% ignores the hinge point."},"L18_5":{"clue":"Surgical threshold for ascending aorta in Marfan syndrome.","mech":"Connective tissue weakness means dissection occurs at smaller diameters, so surgery is recommended earlier.","confirm":"Measure ascending aorta diameter; refer for surgery at about 4.5-5.0 cm in Marfan, or earlier with rapid growth or family history.","trap":"Using 5.5 cm (general population) delays surgery and risks dissection; 4.0 cm is too early."},"L18_6":{"clue":"Linear echo moving parallel to the aortic wall at twice its depth.","mech":"Reverberation between the transducer and the strong aortic wall produces a duplicate echo at double the distance, moving in sync.","confirm":"Check whether the echo is exactly twice the depth and moves identically; view from another window; colour flow is not disrupted by artifacts.","trap":"Assuming every intraluminal line is a dissection flap leads to false diagnosis; real flaps have independent motion and divide colour flow."},"L18_7":{"clue":"Complications of type A dissection seen at the bedside.","mech":"Echo directly shows AR from root dilation or flap prolapse, pericardial effusion or tamponade from rupture into the pericardium, and RWMA from coronary ostial involvement.","confirm":"Perform TTE/TEE for AR, effusion and RWMA; use CT for branch vessel involvement.","trap":"Renal, mesenteric and spinal malperfusion require CT; echo has limited role there."},"L18_8":{"clue":"Proximal main PA thrombus visualised on TTE.","mech":"Basal PSAX or RV outflow view displays the main PA and bifurcation, allowing detection of proximal thrombus.","confirm":"Use basal PSAX at aortic valve level or RV outflow view; look for echogenic mass in PA; confirm with CT.","trap":"Suprasternal and apical views rarely show the main PA; distal thrombus needs CT or TEE."},"L19_1":{"clue":"Measurement not used to estimate PA pressure.","mech":"TAPSE measures RV longitudinal systolic function, not pressure.","confirm":"Use TR velocity plus RAP (systolic), RVOT AT (mean), PR velocity (mean/diastolic) and IVC (RAP) for pressures.","trap":"TAPSE often falls with PH but is not a pressure estimate."},"L19_2":{"clue":"TR Vmax 3.78 m/s and RAP 10 mm Hg.","mech":"Simplified Bernoulli converts velocity to the RV-RA gradient; adding RAP gives PASP if no RVOT obstruction.","confirm":"PASP = 4 x 3.78 squared + 10 = 4 x 14.3 + 10 = 57 + 10 = 67 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting RAP gives 57; using wrong RAP leads to error."},"L19_3":{"clue":"End-diastolic PR velocity 1.77 m/s and RAP 15 mm Hg.","mech":"End-diastolic PR velocity reflects PA-RV diastolic gradient; adding RV end-diastolic pressure (RAP) gives PA diastolic pressure.","confirm":"PADP = 4 x 1.77 squared + 15 = 4 x 3.13 + 15 = 12.5 + 15 = 27.6 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting RAP gives 12.6; using peak PR velocity overestimates."},"L19_4":{"clue":"PASP 60 and PADP 24 mm Hg.","mech":"Mean pressure is weighted toward diastole because diastole lasts longer; mPAP approximates one-third systolic plus two-thirds diastolic.","confirm":"mPAP = 60/3 + (2 x 24)/3 = 20 + 16 = 36 mm Hg.","trap":"Taking a simple average (42) or using only systolic weighting (48) overestimates mean pressure."},"L19_5":{"clue":"RVOT acceleration time 100 ms.","mech":"AT shortens with higher PA pressure; regression formula estimates mPAP.","confirm":"mPAP = 90 - 0.62 x 100 = 90 - 62 = 28 mm Hg.","trap":"Using another formula or adding numbers incorrectly leads to errors; AT at least 120 ms needs the other formula."},"L19_6":{"clue":"Average E/e' 12.","mech":"E/e' correlates with LA pressure; regression formula (Nagueh) or E/e' + 4 provides estimates.","confirm":"PCWP = 12 + 4 = 16 mm Hg (or 1.24 x 12 + 1.9 = about 17).","trap":"Assuming E/e' 12 means normal (8) or high (25-35) ignores the formula."},"L19_7":{"clue":"Conditions where E/e' is inaccurate.","mech":"MAC, MS, MR, prosthetic MV, pericardial disease and regional CAD alter mitral inflow or annular velocities independent of LAP.","confirm":"Use alternative indices (TR velocity, LAVI, pulmonary vein flow) when these conditions are present.","trap":"Normal EF, hypertension and sinus rhythm with normal valves are not limitations."},"L19_8":{"clue":"RV diastolic pressure equals RAP if no TS.","mech":"During diastole the tricuspid valve is open; without TS there is no gradient, so RVEDP equals RAP.","confirm":"Exclude TS by inflow Doppler before using RAP as RVEDP in calculations.","trap":"Pulmonic stenosis affects RVSP-PASP equivalence, not RVEDP-RAP; left-sided valves are irrelevant."},"B24_3":{"clue":"Moderate effusion with marked respirophasic inflow variation: more than 30% across the mitral and more than 60% across the tricuspid valve.","mech":"Fixed pericardial volume makes the ventricles interdependent; inspiration augments right filling at the expense of left filling, exaggerating reciprocal inflow swings.","confirm":"Record mitral and tricuspid PW inflow with a respirometer trace; seek RA systolic and RV diastolic collapse and a plethoric IVC.","trap":"Option C reverses the thresholds. The tricuspid cut-off is the higher one because right inflow normally varies more with respiration."},"B24_4":{"clue":"Small posterior collection with RV diastolic collapse plus a large left pleural effusion in a post-cardiotomy PEA arrest.","mech":"A large pleural effusion raises juxtacardiac pressure and adds to pericardial pressure, producing tamponade physiology even when the pericardial fluid itself is small.","confirm":"Combine cardiac and lung views during pulse checks; drain the pleural fluid and watch for resolution of RV diastolic collapse and return of output.","trap":"Do not dismiss tamponade because the effusion is small; effusion size matters less than rate of accumulation, loculation and extrinsic pleural compression."},"B24_5":{"clue":"The question asks the obstruction threshold at which acute RV strain becomes visible on echo.","mech":"The thin-walled RV tolerates afterload poorly; once more than about 30% of the pulmonary bed is occluded, PVR rises enough to dilate the RV acutely.","confirm":"Look for RV:LV ratio near or above 1, septal flattening, McConnell sign and TAPSE reduction; add DVT scan and look for clot in transit.","trap":"Choosing 50% or 70% confuses the threshold for haemodynamic collapse with the earlier threshold at which RV strain first appears."},"B24_6":{"clue":"Dilated RV with septal shift in PEA arrest; the question asks which added scan best raises diagnostic accuracy for PE.","mech":"RV dilation is nonspecific; finding proximal DVT supplies the embolic source, so the combination greatly increases both sensitivity and specificity.","confirm":"Perform two-point compression ultrasound of the femoral and popliteal veins; noncompressibility confirms DVT.","trap":"A plethoric IVC and high TR velocity are expected in many causes of RV failure and do not establish an embolic cause; acute PE rarely generates very high TR velocity."},"B24_7":{"clue":"Trauma PEA with negative FAST, small hyperdynamic LV with cavity obliteration and an IVC under 1 cm that collapses.","mech":"Low preload empties the LV in systole and the low venous pressure collapses the IVC; hemorrhage may be retroperitoneal, pelvic or long-bone, invisible on FAST.","confirm":"Give blood or volume rapidly and reassess LV filling, IVC size and VTI; extend the search to pelvis, thorax and retroperitoneum.","trap":"A negative FAST does not exclude hemorrhage. Tension pneumothorax and cardiogenic shock would distend the IVC, not collapse it."},"B24_8":{"clue":"Arrest after subclavian line attempt; the question tests which lung ultrasound sign is truly reliable for pneumothorax.","mech":"Lung sliding requires visceral and parietal pleura to be in contact; any air between them abolishes sliding at that point, so visible sliding excludes pneumothorax there.","confirm":"Scan the least dependent anterior zones; if sliding is absent, look for B lines and lung pulse (exclude) or a lung point (confirms).","trap":"Absent sliding alone is not diagnostic: mainstem intubation, apnea, pleurodesis and bullae also abolish it. B lines and lung pulse argue against pneumothorax."},"B24_9":{"clue":"Near-flat ECG line with low-amplitude undulations; echo shows diffuse, asynchronous myocardial twitching.","mech":"Fine VF is disorganised electrical activity producing uncoordinated fibrillatory myocardial motion without effective contraction, unlike asystole, which has no motion at all.","confirm":"Confirm in subcostal view during the pulse check and defibrillate; check leads and gain to avoid missing fine VF.","trap":"Valve leaflets can move passively with ventilation or compressions; do not mistake this for intrinsic activity, and do not call coordinated contraction VF."},"B24_10":{"clue":"The question asks the EASY-ALS time limit for imaging during the pulse check.","mech":"Every second without compressions lowers coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure, so imaging must fit inside the 10-second pulse check, with compressions resumed by 8 seconds.","confirm":"Pre-position the probe subcostally during CPR, record a clip, call resume at 8 seconds, then review the loop while compressions continue.","trap":"Choosing 12 or 15 seconds exceeds the 10-second pulse-check limit; imaging prolongs pauses in practice, which is the harm the protocol is designed to prevent."},"B24_11":{"clue":"Limited arrest TEE: which views best show activity, function, volume and effusion.","mech":"ME 4-chamber, ME long-axis and TG short-axis cover both ventricles, valves, LVOT and pericardium without interrupting compressions, since the probe stays in place.","confirm":"Use ME long-axis during compressions to check whether the area of maximal compression is over the LV rather than the LVOT or aortic root.","trap":"The bicaval, arch, commissural and LAA views answer specific structural questions but add little to rapid arrest decision-making."},"B24_12":{"clue":"Massive PE with ROSC but persisting shock; the question asks the advantage of catheter-directed over systemic lysis.","mech":"Delivering lytic directly into the clot achieves thrombolysis with a fraction of the systemic dose, cutting major bleeding risk.","confirm":"Follow RV size, function, TAPSE and haemodynamics on serial echo to document resolution of RV strain after therapy.","trap":"No lytic strategy eliminates intracranial hemorrhage, and anticoagulation and imaging follow-up are still required; absolute claims are the trap."},"B25_1":{"clue":"Systematic review of 35 trials comparing ultrasound guidance with landmark IJV cannulation.","mech":"Real-time visualisation of the vein, artery and needle path reduces blind passes, raising overall and especially first-pass success and lowering arterial puncture.","confirm":"Remember the pairing: overall success up 12%, first-attempt success up 57%, plus fewer complications and shorter cannulation time.","trap":"Option B swaps the two figures. The larger relative gain is in first-attempt success, which is why fewer passes and fewer complications follow."},"B25_2":{"clue":"Short-axis (out-of-plane) IJV approach: the beam only shows a cross-section of the needle.","mech":"Out of plane, any point of the shaft can look like the tip; the bright dot is wherever the shaft crosses the beam, so the beam must stay just ahead of the tip.","confirm":"Walk-down technique: tilt, fan or slide the probe in increments, losing the dot, then advancing the needle until it reappears, aspirating throughout.","trap":"A fixed probe makes the shaft look like the tip, so the real tip goes deeper, risking posterior wall puncture, carotid or pleural injury."},"B25_3":{"clue":"Anterior wall tenting suddenly resolves, the vein reopens and aspiration becomes easy.","mech":"Release of tenting means the tip has pierced the anterior wall; the needle tip within the lumen appears as a central bright dot in the circular vein.","confirm":"Confirm a target sign, pass the wire, then image the wire in short and long axis before dilation.","trap":"A collapsed vein around the needle usually means back-wall compression or a through-and-through puncture, not correct intraluminal position."},"B25_4":{"clue":"Wire placed in a presumed IJV in a hypotensive patient, with no confirmation yet before dilation.","mech":"Arterial blood can look dark and flow non-pulsatile in shock or hypoxemia, so colour and flash are unreliable; only imaging the wire within the vein proves venous placement.","confirm":"Image the wire inside the vein in transverse and longitudinal planes, proximal to the puncture, ideally following it to the cavoatrial region, before dilating.","trap":"A bright red flash is not a reliable discriminator, and waiting for a chest radiograph delays confirmation and cannot exclude arterial wire passage."},"B25_5":{"clue":"Experienced operator using the oblique view for a right IJV from the transverse start.","mech":"The oblique plane aligns the probe with the vein's course toward the sternal notch, combining short-axis carotid identification with in-plane visualisation of the whole needle.","confirm":"Rotate about 45 degrees so the probe's long axis points to the sternal notch (clockwise on the right, counterclockwise on the left) and insert in plane from the lateral end.","trap":"Rotating toward the shoulder or entering from the medial end brings the needle toward the carotid; the oblique approach is not recommended for novices."},"B25_6":{"clue":"Stable obese patient with femoral artery overlapping the vein.","mech":"External rotation of the leg moves the vein out from under the artery, and reverse Trendelenburg increases femoral venous pressure and diameter.","confirm":"Rescan in short axis after positioning to confirm separation of vein and artery before puncture.","trap":"Trendelenburg distends the IJV, not the femoral vein; Valsalva can distend the femoral vein but is impractical in sedated patients."},"B25_7":{"clue":"Patient on therapeutic heparin; the question asks why the subclavian site is a concern.","mech":"The subclavian artery lies behind the clavicle, so a bleed from inadvertent arterial puncture cannot be controlled by direct external compression.","confirm":"Prefer a compressible site such as femoral or IJV under real-time guidance when anticoagulation cannot be paused.","trap":"Pneumothorax risk is not increased by anticoagulation; the specific concern with heparin is bleeding that cannot be controlled by compression."},"B25_8":{"clue":"Short-axis subclavian cannulation and the recommended bevel orientation.","mech":"With the bevel facing caudally, the tip reflects echoes toward the probe more clearly, and the wire is directed caudally toward the central circulation rather than up into the IJV.","confirm":"Use very light probe pressure, since the SCV collapses easily, and confirm wire direction in long axis.","trap":"Bevel-up is correct for IJV and femoral access; carrying that habit to the SCV is the trap."},"B25_9":{"clue":"Uncertain venous vs arterial cannulation after subclavian access in a hypotensive, ventilated patient.","mech":"Hypotension removes pulsatility and hypoxemia darkens arterial blood, so visual hub cues are unreliable; a pressure waveform and blood gas directly identify the circulation.","confirm":"Transduce the catheter or attach manometry and send a blood gas; image the wire in two planes before any dilation.","trap":"Blood colour and pulsatility fail exactly in the shocked, hypoxemic ventilated patient; dilating first risks large-bore arterial injury."},"B25_10":{"clue":"Agitated saline through a right IJV catheter: bubbles appear in the IJV but not in the RA.","mech":"With correct tip placement, microbubbles emerge near the cavoatrial junction and appear in the RA within seconds; retrograde jugular bubbles indicate the tip points upward or lies elsewhere.","confirm":"Inject agitated saline while viewing subcostal or apical 4-chamber; bubbles in the RA within about 2 seconds support correct placement. Check bilateral lung sliding.","trap":"Do not call the finding normal or right-to-left shunt; a shunt would show bubbles in left chambers after RA opacification."},"B25_11":{"clue":"Meta-analysis comparing ultrasound with chest radiography for post-procedural pneumothorax.","mech":"Supine radiography misses anterior pneumothoraces because air rises anteriorly without a visible pleural edge, so ultrasound is much more sensitive with similar specificity.","confirm":"Remember the pairing: ultrasound 88% sensitive and 99% specific; radiography 52% sensitive and 100% specific.","trap":"Option A swaps the values. Radiography's weakness is sensitivity, not specificity."},"B25_12":{"clue":"Radial artery cannulation at 45 to 60 degrees has just produced a bright red flash.","mech":"The flash means the tip has just entered the lumen; flattening the angle aligns the catheter with the vessel so the catheter itself, not only the bevel, enters before threading.","confirm":"Lower to 10 to 20 degrees, advance 1 to 2 mm, then thread the catheter or wire; confirm intraluminal position in long axis.","trap":"Advancing at a steep angle drives through the back wall; withdrawing and threading blindly loses the lumen."},"B25_13":{"clue":"CPR with two failed radial attempts: what makes the femoral artery advantageous.","mech":"A vessel of about 8.2 mm is far easier to target and cannulate during compressions and low-flow states than a radial artery of about 2.3 mm.","confirm":"Image in short axis lateral to the femoral vein and confirm arterial position with waveform.","trap":"The femoral artery lies lateral to the vein and is the main supply to the leg, so thrombosis risks limb ischemia rather than being harmless."},"B25_14":{"clue":"Double-wall puncture of the femoral artery during cannulation.","mech":"With the tip beyond the back wall, slow withdrawal under aspiration brings it back into the lumen, where free flow returns.","confirm":"Withdraw slowly with gentle suction until free flow; pass the wire and confirm it in transverse and longitudinal views.","trap":"Forcing the wire creates a dissection or extravascular track; firm probe pressure compresses the artery and obscures the lumen."},"B26_1":{"clue":"Maximal interpleural separation of 25 mm measured by Balik's method in a ventilated patient.","mech":"Effusion volume scales roughly linearly with the parietal-visceral separation at the base in a supine patient, giving an empirical factor of 20 mL per mm.","confirm":"V = 20 x separation (mm) = 20 x 25 = 500 mL. Measure at end-expiration at the lung base, supine with the torso slightly elevated.","trap":"Choosing 250 or 1250 mL comes from using the wrong multiplier (10 or 50); the Balik factor is 20."},"B26_2":{"clue":"Pleural fluid with multiple fibrin strands and septations in pneumonia.","mech":"Septations form from fibrin deposition driven by inflammation and protein-rich fluid, which transudates do not produce.","confirm":"Sample the fluid for pH, LDH, protein and glucose; septated parapneumonic effusions often need chest tube, fibrinolytics or surgery.","trap":"Anechoic does not mean transudate, but septated means exudate. Septations change the drainage strategy; they do not make catheter drainage impossible."},"B26_3":{"clue":"Smallest pleural effusion volume that ultrasound can reliably detect compared with supine radiography.","mech":"Ultrasound images fluid directly in the dependent costophrenic recess, whereas radiography needs enough fluid to change radiographic density or blunt the angle.","confirm":"Scan the posterior axillary line at the base with a curvilinear or phased array probe; look for an anechoic space above the diaphragm and the spine sign.","trap":"The 200 mL figure is the radiographic threshold, and 500 mL is the amount needed to obscure the hemidiaphragm, not ultrasound limits."},"B26_4":{"clue":"Large malignant effusion after 1.5 L removal; more fluid remains and the patient is comfortable.","mech":"Reexpansion edema is linked to steeply falling intrapleural pressure rather than volume alone, so pressure monitoring defines the safe limit.","confirm":"Use pleural manometry and stop when pressure falls below -20 cm H2O, or at chest discomfort or cough.","trap":"Free drainage until it stops, or a single big aliquot after clamping, ignores pleural pressure; symptoms are not a reliable stop signal."},"B26_5":{"clue":"Colour Doppler shows the intercostal artery running mid-space at the planned site in an elderly patient with pulmonary hypertension.","mech":"Intercostal arteries become tortuous with age and pulmonary hypertension, leaving the protection of the rib groove and crossing the space where the needle passes.","confirm":"Scan adjacent spaces with linear probe and colour Doppler to find a site where no vessel crosses the path.","trap":"The rule that the artery hides under the rib fails in exactly these patients; a smaller needle still lacerates it."},"B26_6":{"clue":"Ventilated patient with INR 1.7 and platelets 70,000 and an effusion delaying weaning.","mech":"Positive pressure does not increase pneumothorax rates with ultrasound guidance, and modest coagulation abnormalities do not predict bleeding after thoracentesis.","confirm":"Proceed with ultrasound guidance, Doppler the intercostal vessels, and weigh individual bleeding risk.","trap":"Routine transfusion to normal values exposes the patient to transfusion risk without proven benefit; ventilation is not a contraindication."},"B26_7":{"clue":"Right pneumothorax with absent sliding anteriorly and a lung point in the midaxillary line.","mech":"The lung point marks the edge of the air; sites anterior to it overlie air, not lung, so the tube enters the pleural air without hitting lung.","confirm":"Insert above the diaphragm, anterior to the lung point, where sliding is absent; reappearance of sliding confirms reexpansion.","trap":"Inserting at or posterior to the lung point risks lung puncture; below the diaphragm risks liver or spleen."},"B26_8":{"clue":"Static marking technique: the question tests which step preserves the validity of the mark.","mech":"Pleural fluid moves with gravity; changing posture shifts the fluid and lung, so the mark no longer overlies the deepest pocket.","confirm":"Mark, measure skin-to-fluid and skin-to-lung or organ depths, then puncture promptly in the same position.","trap":"The phased array, not linear, probe finds the largest pocket; depth measurement is still needed; both ultrasound techniques beat landmark."},"B26_9":{"clue":"Effusion in a smoker with pleural thickening over 1 cm and pleural nodularity.","mech":"Tumour infiltration thickens the pleura and forms nodules; transudates and simple parapneumonic effusions do not cause solid pleural nodules.","confirm":"Sample fluid for cytology and arrange contrast CT or thoracoscopic biopsy.","trap":"Bilateral effusions with B lines suggest heart failure; a hematocrit sign suggests blood, not specifically malignancy."},"B26_10":{"clue":"Persistent pneumothorax after drainage of a chronic malignant effusion, with a lung that does not reexpand, in a stable patient.","mech":"A trapped lung encased by visceral pleural peel cannot fill the space vacated by fluid, so air or negative pressure fills it ex vacuo rather than from a leak.","confirm":"Confirm stability and absence of growth; pleural manometry showing steep pressure falls supports trapped lung. Usually no tube is needed.","trap":"Placing a chest tube for a non-expandable lung does not reexpand it; this is not operator error and not tension."},"B27_1":{"clue":"Focused upper airway ultrasound: probe and depth.","mech":"The airway structures lie within a few centimetres of the skin, where a high-frequency probe gives the best axial resolution.","confirm":"Use a linear 7.5 to 15 MHz probe at about 4 cm depth, supine with neck neutral or extended.","trap":"Low-frequency probes penetrate more but lose the resolution needed for cartilage and membrane detail."},"B27_2":{"clue":"Moving cephalad in transverse plane, the thick oval cricoid disappears.","mech":"The cricoid is the only complete ring and thicker than tracheal rings; the membrane lies immediately cephalad, so its disappearance marks the membrane's caudal edge.","confirm":"Confirm the membrane longitudinally as the hyperechoic line between cricoid and thyroid cartilage, then mark the skin.","trap":"Vocal ligaments lie above the thyroid cartilage, not at the cricothyroid membrane; palpation is unreliable in abnormal necks."},"B27_3":{"clue":"Longitudinal midline neck view of the tracheal cartilages.","mech":"Cartilage rings, cut longitudinally, appear as rounded hypoechoic beads separated by ligaments, with the larger cricoid as a hump cephalad.","confirm":"Slide transversely to confirm each ring as an inverted U; count rings below the cricoid to plan cricothyrotomy or tracheostomy.","trap":"The inverted U is the transverse appearance of a ring; an oval heteroechoic ring describes the cricoid transversely."},"B27_4":{"clue":"Location of the esophagus on transverse neck ultrasound.","mech":"The esophagus usually lies left of the trachea, posterior to the left thyroid lobe, around tracheal rings 1 and 2.","confirm":"Ask the patient to swallow to see peristalsis; confirm longitudinally, which also shows a misplaced tube.","trap":"The esophagus is never anterior to the cricoid; confusing it with the trachea leads to missed esophageal intubation."},"B27_5":{"clue":"Skin-to-epiglottis distance of 31 mm before intubation.","mech":"Greater anterior neck soft tissue depth reflects bulk that hinders displacement of tongue and epiglottis at laryngoscopy, predicting a difficult view.","confirm":"Compare with the 27.5 mm threshold: 31 mm exceeds it; integrate with clinical predictors and prepare a difficult airway plan.","trap":"No single measurement excludes difficulty, and this measure predicts laryngoscopy difficulty, not stridor or tube size."},"B27_6":{"clue":"Hyoid bone cannot be seen on sublingual ultrasound.","mech":"A non-visible hyoid suggests a deeply placed, high or anteriorly obscured airway anatomy, linked to a poor laryngeal view at direct laryngoscopy.","confirm":"Treat as high-risk: prepare videolaryngoscopy and front-of-neck access; the sign has high specificity for Cormack-Lehane grade 3 or higher.","trap":"It is a difficulty predictor, not a sign of esophageal intubation or vocal cord paralysis."},"B27_7":{"clue":"CPR with flat capnography of uncertain reliability after intubation.","mech":"Low pulmonary blood flow during arrest lowers exhaled CO2, so capnography can falsely suggest esophageal placement; direct tracheal imaging does not depend on flow.","confirm":"TRUE exam: linear probe transverse at the suprasternal notch; a single air-mucosa line means tracheal; a second air interface (double tract sign) means esophageal. Add bilateral lung sliding.","trap":"Gastric distension and diaphragm motion are indirect and late; the hyoid longitudinal view does not show tube position."},"B27_8":{"clue":"Air column width difference of 1.2 mm between cuff inflated and deflated.","mech":"With laryngeal edema, little air passes around the deflated cuff, so the air column barely widens; a small difference signals a narrowed glottic lumen.","confirm":"Compare with 1.6 mm: 1.2 mm is below it, predicting stridor; consider steroids and prepare for reintubation.","trap":"A small difference is a warning, not reassurance or a falsely low value; it reflects edema, not cord paralysis."},"B28_1":{"clue":"Cirrhotic with abdominal pain and largest ascites pocket 3 cm deep.","mech":"SBP risk is linked to ascites volume; when the deepest pocket is under 5 cm, the probability of SBP becomes negligible.","confirm":"Measure the deepest pocket in both lower quadrants; with larger pockets plus pain, perform diagnostic paracentesis.","trap":"Option D implies any fluid needs sampling; the evidence links negligible risk to pockets under 5 cm."},"B28_2":{"clue":"The only absolute contraindication to paracentesis.","mech":"An acute surgical abdomen requires surgical assessment; needle drainage may delay definitive care or obscure diagnosis.","confirm":"Look for peritonism, perforation or obstruction before paracentesis.","trap":"INR, pregnancy and cellulitis are relative contraindications managed by ultrasound site selection."},"B28_3":{"clue":"Diagnostic paracentesis with INR 2.4 and platelets 45,000 in decompensated cirrhosis.","mech":"Cirrhotic coagulopathy is rebalanced, and bleeding after paracentesis is rare and unrelated to INR or platelet count.","confirm":"Proceed promptly under ultrasound, avoiding epigastric vessels with colour Doppler; no prophylactic FFP or platelets.","trap":"Correcting numbers delays an early tap, which is associated with lower mortality, without reducing bleeding."},"B28_4":{"clue":"Left lower quadrant large-volume paracentesis and the most ominous vascular complication.","mech":"The inferior epigastric arteries run along the rectus sheath; puncturing them can cause large abdominal wall or intraperitoneal bleeding.","confirm":"Map them with colour or power Doppler using linear or curvilinear probe and choose a track lateral to them.","trap":"Z-tracking prevents leakage, not bleeding; palpation of the ASIS alone does not locate vessels."},"B28_5":{"clue":"Real-time paracentesis with transverse (short-axis) needle approach.","mech":"Out of plane, only the point where the needle crosses the beam is seen, so the probe must move with the tip to track it.","confirm":"Advance the probe along with the needle, keeping the bright dot as the tip; consider in-plane for whole-needle view.","trap":"A fixed probe shows only the shaft crossing point; M-mode and acoustic shadows do not track the tip."},"B28_6":{"clue":"Flow stops midway while ultrasound still shows a large amount of fluid.","mech":"Bowel or omentum drawn against the catheter side holes occludes it; fluid remains but cannot reach the catheter.","confirm":"Image the catheter tip, reposition the patient or catheter, or flush a small volume of saline.","trap":"Blind advancement risks bowel injury, high suction pulls more tissue into the holes, and aborting is unnecessary."},"B29_1":{"clue":"Stable patient with a chronic (over 3 months) circumferential effusion despite therapy.","mech":"Large chronic effusions over 20 mm in diastole carry a meaningful risk of progression to tamponade, justifying drainage, whereas small ones are low-yield.","confirm":"Measure the largest echo-free space at end-diastole in multiple views; over 20 mm qualifies.","trap":"10 mm defines a small-moderate effusion; diagnostic drainage below 20 mm is not justified unless TB, bacterial or neoplastic disease is suspected."},"B29_2":{"clue":"Type A dissection with tamponade and profound hypotension, surgery not immediately available.","mech":"Rapid decompression restores arterial pressure and shear on the false lumen, which can extend dissection or rerupture; controlled small drainage restores perfusion without hypertension.","confirm":"Drain small aliquots while watching arterial pressure, targeting a systolic around 90 mm Hg, as a bridge to surgery.","trap":"Option A treats the contraindication as absolute even in arrest; complete drainage and positive pressure both worsen outcomes."},"B29_3":{"clue":"Mayo ultrasound-guided approach to pericardiocentesis.","mech":"The safest needle path is the shortest route to the largest fluid pocket without intervening liver, lung or vessels, which is often para-apical rather than subcostal.","confirm":"Scan multiple windows, measure depth to fluid, and mark the site and trajectory.","trap":"Routine subxiphoid puncture is a landmark habit; echo-selected sites have higher success and fewer complications."},"B29_4":{"clue":"Parasternal pericardiocentesis in the fifth intercostal space more than 1 cm lateral to the sternum.","mech":"The internal thoracic vessels run about 1 cm lateral to the sternal edge; needle placement there risks laceration and haemorrhage.","confirm":"Map the vessels with a high-frequency linear probe and colour Doppler, then use an in-plane approach away from them.","trap":"The LAD and phrenic nerve are not the specific parasternal concern addressed by this landmark."},"B29_5":{"clue":"Subxiphoid pericardiocentesis once the needle is beneath the costal cartilage.","mech":"A shallow 15 to 30 degree path toward the left shoulder follows the inferior heart border, staying above the peritoneum and away from the RA.","confirm":"Advance under echo or ECG guidance with continuous aspiration, aimed at the left shoulder.","trap":"Steep angles enter the peritoneum or liver; medial aim risks RA puncture."},"B29_6":{"clue":"Bloody aspirate with uncertain tip position.","mech":"Agitated saline creates echogenic microbubbles; seeing them in the pericardial space places the tip outside the chambers.","confirm":"Inject about 5 mL of agitated saline through the needle while imaging; bubbles in a chamber indicate intracardiac placement.","trap":"Advancing to clear aspirate risks myocardial puncture; colour Doppler cannot localise the tip."},"B29_7":{"clue":"Aspirate clots quickly and its haemoglobin equals peripheral blood.","mech":"Pericardial blood is defibrinated by cardiac motion and diluted by effusion, so it does not clot and has lower haemoglobin; fresh clotting blood is intracardiac.","confirm":"Confirm with agitated saline contrast and stop advancing; consider pressure transduction.","trap":"Hemorrhagic effusions can look bloody; the clotting and matching haemoglobin, not colour, identify chamber puncture."},"B29_8":{"clue":"Indwelling pericardial catheter after tamponade drainage.","mech":"Extended drainage lets pericardial layers appose and reduces reaccumulation; removal is safe once output falls below 25 mL in 24 hours.","confirm":"Aspirate every 4 to 6 hours and record output; remove under 25 mL/24 h; keep up to 72 hours if output exceeds 30 mL in the first 24 hours.","trap":"Removing at under 100 mL/day or at a fixed 24 hours shortens drainage and raises recurrence; fluid colour does not indicate when the space has sealed."},"B29_9":{"clue":"Acute pulmonary edema and shock hours after rapid drainage of 1.8 L of pericardial fluid.","mech":"Sudden decompression abruptly increases venous return to a ventricle adapted to low preload and high sympathetic tone, producing paradoxical ventricular failure.","confirm":"Initially drain no more than 1 L, enough to normalise CVP and BP, then complete drainage slowly.","trap":"Recurrent tamponade causes low output without edema; reexpansion edema is a pleural phenomenon."},"B29_10":{"clue":"Moderate pericardial effusion plus very large left pleural effusion with RA and RV collapse.","mech":"Large pleural effusions raise intrapleural and intrapericardial pressure, causing chamber collapse that resolves when the pleural fluid is drained.","confirm":"Drain the pleural fluid first, then reassess collapse and haemodynamics; reconsider pericardiocentesis if collapse persists.","trap":"RA collapse is the most common false-positive tamponade sign, especially in hypovolemia."},"B29_11":{"clue":"Severe pulmonary hypertension with a large effusion, hypotension and pulsus paradoxus but no chamber collapse.","mech":"Raised right-sided pressures keep transmural pressure positive, so the RA and RV resist collapse despite tamponade.","confirm":"Look for respirophasic inflow variation, plethoric IVC and LA or LV collapse; treat tamponade as a clinical diagnosis.","trap":"Absent collapse is a false negative in high right pressures, not reassurance and not constriction."},"B29_12":{"clue":"Tachypneic tamponade patient before drainage; question asks the risk of positive pressure ventilation.","mech":"Positive intrathoracic pressure further reduces venous return to an already restricted heart, lowering cardiac output.","confirm":"Drain first under local anaesthesia; if ventilation is unavoidable, use low pressures and be ready for collapse.","trap":"Hypercapnia raises, not lowers, pericardial pressure; positive pressure lowers venous return."},"B30_1":{"clue":"ACCE-competent intensivist and which scenario needs formal echo and cardiology input.","mech":"Prosthetic valve assessment requires specialised Doppler quantification and comparison with prosthesis-specific norms beyond ACCE scope.","confirm":"Request formal TTE or TEE and cardiology review for prosthetic valves, complex congenital disease, embolic source and stress echo.","trap":"VTI, RV quantification and preload indices are core ACCE skills and do not mandate referral."},"B30_2":{"clue":"A4C clip with rounded apex and short LV.","mech":"Foreshortening cuts the LV obliquely, missing the true apex, which hides apical abnormalities and distorts volumes and EF.","confirm":"Slide laterally and inferiorly to the true apex until the LV is elongated with a tapered apex.","trap":"Seeing four chambers and both valves is not enough; quality criteria require a non-foreshortened apex."},"B30_3":{"clue":"Equivocal RV enlargement on limited CCUS in a hypotensive patient being considered for fibrinolysis.","mech":"CCUS is qualitative and favours specificity; equivocal results do not reach a decision threshold for high-risk therapy.","confirm":"Obtain formal echo, urgent expert review or CT pulmonary angiography before fibrinolysis.","trap":"Training volume or repeat CCUS does not make an equivocal finding definitive; false RV enlargement can cause harmful lysis."},"B30_4":{"clue":"NBE critical care echocardiography certification experience requirement.","mech":"Certification requires 150 complete critical care TTE studies personally performed and interpreted, a subset reviewed by a certified supervisor.","confirm":"Log studies that meet quality criteria with supervisor review.","trap":"50 interpreted/30 performed is the SCCM basic standard; 50 studies annually is a maintenance criterion."},"B32_1":{"clue":"Neonate with CDH repair and anterior liver-like structure beneath the pleura.","mech":"The neonatal thymus is large and coarse, homogeneous and liver-like, mimicking consolidation on radiograph and ultrasound.","confirm":"Look for its anterior mediastinal location, lack of air bronchograms and homogeneous texture.","trap":"Adult reflex diagnosis of consolidation or tumour atelectasis ignores the pediatric differential."},"B32_2":{"clue":"Child with LV dysfunction but normal RV and normal-low IVC:Ao ratio on epinephrine and milrinone.","mech":"Low LV function alone does not mean volume intolerance; a normal RV and low IVC:Ao suggest preload reserve.","confirm":"Give a small cautious bolus with serial RV, IVC:Ao and lung reassessment.","trap":"Adult IVC cut-offs do not apply to children; withholding all fluid ignores signs of preload responsiveness."},"B32_3":{"clue":"Blurred images in a crying, tachycardic infant.","mech":"Frame rate falls with depth and sector width because each line requires a pulse round trip; narrowing both raises temporal resolution.","confirm":"Reduce depth and sector to the region of interest; choose the highest frequency that still penetrates.","trap":"Increasing depth or widening the sector lowers frame rate; lowest frequency degrades resolution."},"B32_4":{"clue":"Budget pediatric POCUS purchase and transducer selection.","mech":"Pediatric phased arrays are higher frequency with small footprints, imaging between ribs and adequately penetrating thin children up to adolescence.","confirm":"Choose pediatric phased array, high-frequency linear and possibly micro-convex probes.","trap":"Neonates need higher, not lower, frequencies; large curvilinear probes rarely fit."},"B32_5":{"clue":"Rib shadowing obscures B lines in a 19-month-old with narrow interspaces.","mech":"Ossified ribs block sound; aligning the probe within the interspace avoids rib shadows.","confirm":"Rotate the probe parallel to the ribs and use a small footprint probe.","trap":"Increasing gain or switching to large curvilinear probes does not remove rib shadows."},"B33_1":{"clue":"Spontaneously breathing child with flat IVC: 6 mm IVC and 10 mm aorta.","mech":"Aortic diameter scales the IVC for body size; a low ratio reflects low venous filling.","confirm":"IVC:Ao = 6/10 = 0.6. Normal is 1 to 1.4; under 0.8 suggests hypovolemia.","trap":"0.6 is not within normal range, and transverse view is sufficient to calculate it."},"B33_2":{"clue":"Ventilated child with IVC distending 25% with each breath.","mech":"Positive pressure raises RA pressure and impedes venous return, distending the IVC; large distension implies preload reserve.","confirm":"Calculate distensibility: (max - min)/min; above 20% suggests responsiveness. Confirm with aortic peak velocity variation.","trap":"The 50% threshold applies to non-intubated collapsibility; inspiratory distension under positive pressure is expected."},"B33_3":{"clue":"Most accurate pediatric fluid responsiveness index.","mech":"Respiratory variation in aortic peak velocity reflects heart-lung interaction on stroke volume, which is more directly related to responsiveness than IVC size.","confirm":"Place PW in LVOT on A5C and calculate (Vmax - Vmin)/mean velocity over a breath; above 20% suggests responsiveness.","trap":"IVC diameter and IVC:Ao ratio are static and confounded by ventilation and abdominal pressure; LV wall thickening reflects contractility, not preload reserve."},"B33_4":{"clue":"Septal flattening into the LV during systole at mid-papillary PSAX in a child with ARDS.","mech":"When RV systolic pressure approaches or exceeds LV pressure, the septum is pushed toward the LV in systole; diastolic flattening instead reflects RV volume overload.","confirm":"Confirm a true short-axis view with horizontal papillary muscles, check timing with M-mode or frame-by-frame, and estimate RVSP from TR velocity.","trap":"Volume overload flattens the septum mainly in diastole; off-axis views can mimic flattening, so check alignment first."},"B33_5":{"clue":"Echo-free fluid posterior to the heart in PLAX in a child in shock.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta, so pericardial fluid tracks in front of it while pleural fluid lies behind it.","confirm":"Locate the descending aorta behind the LA; confirm with subxiphoid view.","trap":"Respiratory variation and mitral annulus crossing do not reliably separate pericardial from pleural fluid."},"B33_6":{"clue":"Hypotensive child with distended fixed IVC, RV larger than LV with reduced function, septal flattening, no effusion.","mech":"Acute obstruction to RV outflow raises RV afterload, dilating and failing the RV and backing up venous pressure.","confirm":"Look for clot, McConnell sign, absent lung sliding and DVT; treat the cause.","trap":"Hypovolemic and early distributive shock have small or collapsible IVCs; isolated LV failure does not enlarge the RV beyond LV."},"B34_1":{"clue":"Ductal diameter of 2.4 mm measured at the narrowest pulmonary end in a preterm infant.","mech":"Ductal diameter at the pulmonary end determines resistance to shunt flow; 1.5 to 3 mm is moderate.","confirm":"Classify 2.4 mm as moderate; then assess overcirculation (LA:Ao, LVO) and steal markers (descending aortic diastolic flow).","trap":"Size alone does not define hemodynamic significance; it must be integrated with other markers."},"B34_2":{"clue":"Echo finding that best correlates with ductal shunt volume on MRI.","mech":"Holodiastolic reversal in the descending aorta shows diastolic steal from systemic to pulmonary circulation via a large shunt.","confirm":"Place PW in the descending aorta distal to the duct or subcostal view; look for holodiastolic reversal.","trap":"LA:Ao can be falsely reassuring if an atrial shunt decompresses the LA; ductal diameter alone does not quantify flow."},"B34_3":{"clue":"Preterm with large PDA and mitral E/A above 1.","mech":"Preterm LV filling relies on atrial contraction, so E/A under 1 is normal; increased pulmonary venous return elevates LA pressure and early filling, pseudonormalising E/A.","confirm":"Integrate with LA:Ao, LVEDD, LVO and descending aortic diastolic flow.","trap":"Do not mistake E/A over 1 as normal maturation; restrictive or right-to-left patterns are not implied."},"B34_4":{"clue":"LVO of 350 mL/kg/min in a hypotensive preterm with large left-to-right PDA.","mech":"Ductal shunt blood re-enters the LV via pulmonary veins, so LVO includes shunt flow and overestimates systemic flow.","confirm":"Calculate LVO = VTI x CSA x HR / weight; values over 300 mL/kg/min suggest significant shunt. Use SVC flow or RVO for systemic flow estimate.","trap":"High LVO does not mean adequate perfusion; ductal steal can coexist with low systemic flow."},"B34_5":{"clue":"SVC flow as a marker of systemic blood flow in preterm neonates and its limitation.","mech":"SVC return reflects upper body, mainly cerebral, flow and avoids shunt confounding, but measuring the compressible SVC diameter is unreliable.","confirm":"Measure SVC VTI and diameter carefully; interpret alongside other markers.","trap":"SVC flow does not equal LV output and does not need TEE; its main limitation is reproducibility."},"B35_1":{"clue":"Term cesarean neonate with compact inferior B lines and abrupt transition to noncompact superior B lines.","mech":"Delayed lung fluid clearance leaves dependent zones wetter, creating a sharp boundary called the double lung point.","confirm":"Scan anterior and lateral zones; confirm the double lung point and resolution over hours.","trap":"RDS shows homogeneous white lung without spared areas; the double lung point is not the pneumothorax lung point."},"B35_2":{"clue":"Preterm on CPAP with confluent B lines everywhere, irregular pleura and lung score 9.","mech":"Surfactant deficiency causes uniform alveolar collapse and fluid, creating white lung; the score reflects aeration loss and predicts surfactant need.","confirm":"Calculate the segmental score (0 to 3 per region); 9 exceeds the threshold of 5 or 6 for infants under 30 weeks.","trap":"TTN has spared zones and a double lung point; normal transition does not produce white lung with abnormal pleura."},"B35_3":{"clue":"Sudden desaturation during HFOV.","mech":"HFOV abolishes normal lung sliding and M-mode signs, but the lung point still shows the edge where visceral pleura contacts parietal pleura.","confirm":"Search for a lung point; B lines and lung pulse rule out pneumothorax locally.","trap":"Under HFOV sliding is replaced by lung shimmy, so absent sliding and M-mode seashore or barcode patterns cannot be interpreted; only the lung point rules in."},"B35_4":{"clue":"Diaphragm end-expiratory thickness 1.6 mm, maximal inspiratory thickness 2.4 mm.","mech":"Thickening fraction expresses active inspiratory thickening relative to resting thickness, reflecting diaphragm contractile effort.","confirm":"TF = (2.4 - 1.6)/1.6 = 0.5, i.e. 50%. Measure in the zone of apposition.","trap":"33% divides by inspiratory thickness; 150% divides 2.4 by 1.6. The denominator must be end-expiratory thickness."},"B35_5":{"clue":"Meconium-stained term infant with patchy asymmetric subpleural consolidations that change over time.","mech":"Meconium causes patchy airway obstruction, chemical pneumonitis and surfactant inactivation, creating heterogeneous findings.","confirm":"Scan both hemithoraces serially; look for changing consolidations and spared areas.","trap":"Uniform white lung is RDS; the double lung point is TTN; isolated A lines are normal."},"B36_1":{"clue":"Suspected NEC with bowel gas paucity; colour Doppler shows absent bowel wall flow.","mech":"Transmural necrosis destroys the mural microcirculation, so colour Doppler shows no wall flow.","confirm":"Use low velocity scale and wall filter; compare with adjacent loops and look for free air or complex fluid.","trap":"Y, ring and zebra hyperaemic patterns indicate inflamed but viable bowel in Bell stage I to II, not necrosis."},"B36_2":{"clue":"Neonatal bowel wall thickness over 2.5 mm, and the meaning of a wall under 1.0 mm.","mech":"Early NEC causes mucosal oedema and wall thickening; as necrosis progresses, the wall thins.","confirm":"Measure wall thickness and colour Doppler flow; a thin hyperechoic wall with absent flow suggests necrosis.","trap":"Thinning is not reassuring resolution; it may be late necrosis, so correlate with perfusion and clinical course."},"B36_3":{"clue":"Meta-analysis question: which NEC ultrasound feature was not associated with surgery or death.","mech":"Portal venous gas reflects absorption of intramural gas into the portal circulation, a diagnostic marker of NEC rather than of transmural necrosis or perforation.","confirm":"Prognostic red flags are pneumoperitoneum, aperistalsis, complex ascites and focal fluid collections; look for them on serial scans.","trap":"Portal venous gas looks dramatic, but it is diagnostic, not prognostic; complex ascites and focal collections carry the bad outcome."},"B36_4":{"clue":"Neonatal NEC scan: which finding is most specific for perforation.","mech":"Leakage of bowel contents provokes localised inflammatory, complex fluid walled off between loops, which is more specific than free simple fluid.","confirm":"Look for focal fluid with septations or debris between loops and free intraperitoneal air.","trap":"A small amount of anechoic fluid is common; intramural gas and portal venous gas define NEC but not perforation."},"B36_5":{"clue":"Hemodynamically stable child after blunt abdominal trauma; randomised trial of 925 children.","mech":"In stable children, pediatric FAST sensitivity for hemoperitoneum is modest and many injuries do not produce free fluid, so FAST adds little beyond clinical assessment and selective CT.","confirm":"Use FAST mainly in unstable children, where a positive result guides operative decisions.","trap":"Assuming adult FAST benefits carry over ignores the trial: no change in care, length of stay, missed injury or charges."},"B37_1":{"clue":"No ophthalmic preset available before ocular scanning.","mech":"The lens and cornea are avascular and poorly cooled, so they are vulnerable to heating and mechanical bioeffects; strict MI and TI limits keep exposure safe.","confirm":"Manually set power under 30%, MI under 0.23 and TI under 1; keep scan time short, especially with Doppler.","trap":"MI 1.9 is the general diagnostic ceiling, not the ocular limit; safety mode does not justify prolonged Doppler."},"B37_2":{"clue":"Infant of 9 months with hydrocephalus undergoing ONSD measurement.","mech":"The optic nerve sheath is continuous with the subarachnoid space and distends with raised ICP; normal values are smaller in infants than in older children and adults.","confirm":"Measure 3 mm behind the globe, perpendicular to the nerve; above 4 mm in infants under 1 year suggests intracranial hypertension.","trap":"5 to 5.7 mm are adult-range cut-offs; applying them to infants misses raised ICP."},"B37_3":{"clue":"Technique question: where to measure ONSD in a child.","mech":"The anterior retrobulbar sheath is where distension with raised CSF pressure is greatest and imaging contrast is best, standardised at 3 mm behind the globe.","confirm":"Mark 3 mm posterior to the globe-sheath junction, measure across the sheath at 90 degrees to the nerve, using consistent internal or external edges.","trap":"Measuring parallel to the nerve gives a length, not a diameter; the lamina cribrosa and 1 cm points are not standardised."},"B37_4":{"clue":"Severe periorbital oedema prevents opening the eye; ultrasound through the lid in transverse plane shows the anechoic pupil.","mech":"The pupillary light reflex is bilateral, so light in the contralateral eye causes consensual constriction of the scanned pupil, which ultrasound can measure through the closed lid.","confirm":"Set depth 1 to 1.5 cm, image the pupil, shine bright light through the contralateral eyelid and record diameter change with M-mode or a B-mode clip.","trap":"The probe covers the ipsilateral eye, so direct stimulation is impractical; colour Doppler and ONSD do not test the reflex."},"B37_5":{"clue":"A taut bright linear echo tethered to the optic disc on ocular POCUS.","mech":"The retina is firmly anchored at the optic disc and ora serrata, so a detached retina stays tethered at the disc while vitreous membranes are free.","confirm":"Use kinetic scanning: ask the patient to move the eye; a detached retina moves but stays anchored at the disc, vitreous strands swirl freely.","trap":"Vitreous haemorrhage shows wavy variable strands; posterior vitreous detachment sways like seaweed and is not tethered to the disc."},"B37_6":{"clue":"Posterior pole bulge at the optic nerve entry in an infant with suspected abusive head trauma and raised ICP.","mech":"Raised ICP transmitted along the optic nerve sheath causes disc swelling and sheath invagination into the globe at the posterior pole.","confirm":"Obtain dilated fundus examination by ophthalmology within 24 to 72 hours; ultrasound does not replace it.","trap":"Calling it retinoschisis or detachment overstates abuse evidence; POCUS neither confirms nor excludes abusive retinal injury."},"B38_1":{"clue":"900-g preterm needing radial access; which method alone cannot distinguish artery from vein.","mech":"Neonatal distal arteries are small and low-pressure, so they compress as easily as veins; compressibility loses its usual discriminating value.","confirm":"Use pulsatility on 2D, colour Doppler and a pulsed-wave arterial waveform to identify the artery.","trap":"The adult rule that veins compress and arteries do not fails in tiny neonatal vessels."},"B38_2":{"clue":"UVC tip confirmation after insertion in a neonate.","mech":"The UVC travels via the umbilical vein and ductus venosus to the IVC; the ideal tip sits at the ductus venosus-IVC/RA junction, avoiding hepatic and intracardiac complications.","confirm":"Use the longitudinal IVC (subcostal sagittal) view; inject saline microbubbles to localise the tip.","trap":"A tip in the portal sinus or liver risks hepatic necrosis; a tip deep in the RA risks effusion, tamponade and arrhythmia."},"B38_3":{"clue":"High UAC tip position and ultrasound confirmation of the aorta.","mech":"A high line sits above the celiac, SMA and renal origins, immediately below the diaphragm, reducing thrombotic risk to visceral branches.","confirm":"Fan left from the longitudinal IVC view; confirm the aorta by colour Doppler of celiac trunk and SMA, and locate the tip above them.","trap":"Between iliac bifurcation and renal arteries defines a low line; arch position is too high."},"B38_4":{"clue":"LP in a 2-month-old with conus and cauda equina visible on ultrasound.","mech":"Cartilaginous, unossified spinous processes transmit sound, allowing imaging of the canal and its contents.","confirm":"Identify the conus level, mark L3-L4 or L4-L5, and measure skin-to-dura depth as the maximum safe needle depth.","trap":"Ossified processes block sound; ultrasound does not replace sterile technique or CSF sampling."},"B38_5":{"clue":"Neonatal midsagittal suprasternal view after intubation.","mech":"The ETT tip can be visualised against mediastinal landmarks such as the aorta, carina and right pulmonary artery, allowing depth assessment.","confirm":"Image the tip relative to the aortic arch or RPA; confirm with radiograph if needed.","trap":"Absent sliding or paradoxical diaphragm motion suggests esophageal placement but is not specific for mainstem; capnography confirms tracheal, not depth."},"B39_1":{"clue":"Gastric POCUS in a 25-kg child.","mech":"In children under 40 kg, the antrum lies superficially, so a high-frequency linear probe gives better resolution.","confirm":"Scan supine then right lateral decubitus with head up 30 to 45 degrees; RLD moves contents to the dependent antrum.","trap":"Curvilinear probes are for adults and children over 40 kg; supine alone underestimates content."},"B39_2":{"clue":"Antrum empty supine but fluid seen in right lateral decubitus.","mech":"RLD moves fluid to the dependent antrum, revealing small volumes missed supine.","confirm":"Grade 0: empty both; grade 1: fluid only RLD; grade 2: fluid both positions.","trap":"Scanning supine only would call this stomach empty (grade 0); grade 2 requires fluid visible in both positions."},"B39_3":{"clue":"Antral contents heterogeneous with posterior acoustic shadowing.","mech":"Solid food contains air and particulate matter that scatters and blocks the beam, causing heterogeneous echoes and shadowing.","confirm":"Assess antral contents in two positions; solid material indicates full stomach.","trap":"Hypoechoic distended antrum with posterior enhancement is clear fluid; starry night is fluid with air; target is empty."},"B39_4":{"clue":"Epigastric sagittal landmarks for the gastric antrum.","mech":"The antrum lies between the left liver lobe anteriorly and the pancreas posteriorly, with the SMA and aorta or IVC deeper.","confirm":"Place probe just right of midline, indicator cephalad, and identify the antrum relative to liver and pancreas.","trap":"Spleen and kidney landmark the gastric body or fundus, not the antrum."},"B39_5":{"clue":"Pediatric study defining at-risk stomach by ultrasound.","mech":"Solid content or fluid volume over 1.25 mL/kg is considered aspiration risk in children.","confirm":"Remember: at-risk stomach defined as solids or fluid over 1.25 mL/kg; incidence about 1% in elective patients.","trap":"Do not confuse with the 7% figure using a different definition (over 1.5 mL/kg)."},"B39_6":{"clue":"Caudal anesthesia in a 2-year-old.","mech":"The epidural space is reached through the sacral hiatus, covered by the sacrococcygeal ligament.","confirm":"Use in-plane needle guidance through the sacrococcygeal ligament with a short-bevel 20 to 22 G needle.","trap":"Ligamentum flavum at L4-L5 is the lumbar epidural route, and dural puncture at the conus is dangerous; caudal access is through the sacral hiatus."},"B39_7":{"clue":"Spinal anesthesia in an infant under 9 months.","mech":"Poor ossification allows clear ultrasound views of the conus and dural sac.","confirm":"Mark the highest lumbar interspace caudal to the conus and measure skin-to-dura distance preprocedurally.","trap":"L1-L2 is too high in infants; sacral hiatus marks caudal, not spinal, approach."},"B39_8":{"clue":"Femoral nerve block in a child.","mech":"The femoral nerve lies lateral to the artery and deep to fascia iliaca, appearing hyperechoic and crescent-shaped.","confirm":"Place the needle tip deep to fascia iliaca, posterior to the nerve's midsection.","trap":"Superficial to fascia iliaca or medial to vein is wrong location; within vascular sheath risks intravascular injection."},"B39_9":{"clue":"Postblock check after intercostal nerve block.","mech":"The needle approaches near the pleura; pneumothorax would abolish pleural sliding.","confirm":"Confirm continued pleural sliding over the block site.","trap":"ONSD, diaphragm and Doppler checks do not address the main complication."},"B39_10":{"clue":"Rectus sheath block in a child for midline laparotomy.","mech":"The target plane lies deep to rectus abdominis and superficial to the posterior rectus sheath, where terminal intercostal nerves run.","confirm":"Insert lateral to medial, inject 0.2 mL/kg in the plane; provides somatic analgesia only.","trap":"It does not provide visceral analgesia; nerves are too small to see; transversalis fascia-peritoneum plane is wrong."},"XC01_F01":{"clue":"The bile-filled gallbladder appears black on the image.","mech":"Fluid without internal reflectors transmits sound without echoes, displaying as anechoic.","confirm":"Compare with surrounding liver and any hyperechoic stones with shadowing.","trap":"Hypoechoic is not anechoic; the liver is moderately echogenic, not the gallbladder."},"XC01_F02":{"clue":"M-mode trace below the 2D image: a single line plotted against time.","mech":"Sampling a single scan line allows thousands of pulses per second, giving very high temporal resolution.","confirm":"Use M-mode for rapidly moving structures, e.g. mitral leaflets, TAPSE, IVC.","trap":"M-mode does not improve penetration or measure velocity; Doppler does velocity."},"XC01_F03":{"clue":"Blue TR jet in the RA on apical 4-chamber colour Doppler.","mech":"Colour Doppler encodes direction relative to the probe: blue means away, red means toward.","confirm":"Confirm jet direction from RV to RA, away from the apical probe.","trap":"Turbulence shows as mosaic, aliasing as colour wraparound, not uniform blue."},"XC01_F04":{"clue":"Crisp mitral inflow envelope with dark centre on PW Doppler.","mech":"Laminar flow has a narrow velocity spectrum, so the envelope is sharp and centre dark.","confirm":"Look for clear E and A waves above baseline; spectral broadening suggests turbulence.","trap":"Aliasing wraps peaks; turbulence fills the envelope; range ambiguity is a high-PRF phenomenon."},"XC01_F05":{"clue":"Carotid PW Doppler peaks cut off and wrapped below baseline (aliasing), corrected in panel B.","mech":"Aliasing occurs when velocity exceeds the Nyquist limit (PRF/2); increasing PRF or shifting baseline raises the displayable limit.","confirm":"Increase velocity scale (PRF), move baseline, reduce depth or switch to CW Doppler.","trap":"Gain does not change the Nyquist limit; increasing depth lowers PRF and worsens aliasing."},"XC01_F06":{"clue":"Peak TR velocity of 3.28 m/s on CW Doppler.","mech":"Simplified Bernoulli: pressure gradient equals 4V squared, where V is peak velocity.","confirm":"4 x 3.28 squared = 4 x 10.76 = 43 mmHg; add RAP (e.g. 10 mmHg) for RVSP about 53 mmHg.","trap":"53 mmHg is RVSP, not the RV-RA gradient; 33 mmHg miscalculates."},"XC01_F07":{"clue":"Low-velocity spectral trace at lateral mitral annulus: tissue Doppler imaging.","mech":"TDI filters high-velocity blood flow to display slow myocardial motion; e' reflects LV relaxation.","confirm":"Measure lateral e', calculate E/e' for filling pressure estimation.","trap":"TDI is not used for PASP, stroke volume or AS grading."},"XC01_F08":{"clue":"Dark vertical bands deep to bright gallstones.","mech":"Calculi reflect and absorb most of the beam, so no sound reaches deeper tissues, producing acoustic shadowing.","confirm":"Reduce gain and confirm shadow behind each stone.","trap":"Posterior enhancement is bright, not dark; edge artifact arises from curved edges, not stones."},"XC01_F09":{"clue":"Thin dark vertical lines from lateral borders of a curved vessel.","mech":"Refraction at the curved edge bends the beam away, leaving a shadow deep to the edge.","confirm":"Recognise edge artifact; do not confuse with calcification or true shadow.","trap":"Do not call this a shadow from calcified plaque: edge shadows arise from the curved border itself, while calcification is a bright focus with a shadow beneath."},"XC01_F10":{"clue":"Linear echo in the aortic lumen at the same depth as a bright adjacent structure on TEE.","mech":"Side lobes from strong off-axis reflectors return echoes misplaced into the main beam at the same depth.","confirm":"Change angle or plane; check with colour Doppler and M-mode for independent motion.","trap":"An intimal flap has its own motion and colour Doppler separation of true and false lumens."},"XC01_F11":{"clue":"Bright vertical ring-down artifact deep to a hyperechoic line in the anterior neck soft tissues.","mech":"Ring-down arises when fluid trapped between tiny gas bubbles resonates, emitting continuous echoes; in soft tissue it marks gas dissecting through fascial planes.","confirm":"Trace the artifact along fascial planes and toward the mediastinum; correlate with crepitus and chest imaging for pneumomediastinum.","trap":"Tracheal cartilage and calcified nodules do not resonate; calcification gives a shadow, not a bright ring-down tail."},"XC01_F12":{"clue":"Image shows a tripod, pen-like grip with the ulnar side of the hand braced on the patient.","mech":"Anchoring the hand converts probe control to the fine motor muscles of hand and wrist, allowing small, stable adjustments over a narrow acoustic window.","confirm":"Hold the probe low between thumb, index and middle fingers, rest the little finger and hand on the chest, and make millimetre adjustments.","trap":"Greater pressure from the shoulder is not the goal and causes fatigue; the grip is about stability and fine control, not speed."},"XC02_F02":{"clue":"Screen indicator on the right side of a PLAX image.","mech":"Cardiology convention places the probe marker on the screen right, unlike radiology and abdominal convention with the marker on the left.","confirm":"Check the machine cardiac preset; with indicator to the right shoulder, PLAX shows the aorta on the screen right.","trap":"Calling it a reversed setting reflects abdominal habits; the indicator position must match the preset, not one universal rule."},"XC02_F04":{"clue":"Transducer movements diagram: which changes the imaging plane while the footprint stays on the same spot.","mech":"Tilting (fanning) pivots the beam perpendicular to the imaging plane, sweeping through parallel slices; rocking angles the beam within the same plane.","confirm":"Tilt to sweep through structures, e.g. from PLAX to RV inflow or outflow; rock to centre a structure in the same plane.","trap":"Rocking and tilting are often confused; rocking keeps the plane, sliding moves the footprint."},"XC02_F05":{"clue":"Left parasternal probe with indicator toward the right shoulder.","mech":"This aligns the beam with the LV long axis from base to apex, displaying LV, LVOT, aortic root, mitral valve and LA.","confirm":"Confirm PLAX with aortic and mitral valves in view and the descending aorta behind the LA; rotate 90 degrees clockwise for PSAX.","trap":"Indicator to the left shoulder gives PSAX; apical and subcostal views need different windows."},"XC02_F06":{"clue":"Image shows RV anteriorly, LV, mitral valve, LVOT and aortic root, LA and descending aorta in cross-section.","mech":"PLAX cuts the heart along the LV long axis from the parasternal window, so the RV lies closest to the probe and the descending aorta behind the LA.","confirm":"Identify the descending aorta behind the LA and the apex off the screen left.","trap":"The apical long-axis shows similar structures but with the apex at the top of the screen."},"XC02_F08":{"clue":"M-mode cursor through the midventricle in PLAX.","mech":"Sampling one line across septum, cavity and inferolateral wall over time allows precise measurement of wall thickness and diameters in systole and diastole.","confirm":"Place the cursor perpendicular to the LV long axis at the mitral leaflet tips; measure IVSd, LVIDd, LVIDs and posterior wall thickness for fractional shortening.","trap":"Mitral velocities need Doppler; TAPSE is measured at the lateral tricuspid annulus in A4C."},"XC02_F09":{"clue":"Attempted PLAX shows too much of the LV apex.","mech":"Probe placement too lateral or inferior aims the beam obliquely, cutting toward the apex rather than along the true long axis.","confirm":"Slide the probe medially and up toward the sternal border, usually third to fourth intercostal space, until the apex is off-screen left.","trap":"Rotating the indicator gives a different plane; depth does not cause apex inclusion."},"XC02_F10":{"clue":"Attempted PLAX shows RV, tricuspid valve and RA: the RV inflow view.","mech":"Tilting the tail up aims the beam inferomedially toward the right hip, displaying the RV inflow.","confirm":"Tilt the tail down slightly to return to the standard PLAX.","trap":"Rotating toward the left shoulder gives PSAX, and sliding up a rib space changes the window; neither corrects a tilt error."},"XC02_F11":{"clue":"Attempted PLAX shows RV outflow tract, pulmonic valve and pulmonary artery.","mech":"Tilting the tail down aims the beam toward the left shoulder, displaying the RVOT.","confirm":"Tilt the tail up slightly to bring the plane back to the standard PLAX with LVOT and aortic root.","trap":"Tilting the tail further down deepens the RVOT error; rotation or lateral sliding changes the plane or window rather than correcting tilt."},"XC02_F12":{"clue":"PSAX showing both papillary muscles.","mech":"The papillary muscles arise at the midventricular level, defining the mid-papillary short-axis.","confirm":"Identify anterolateral (3 o'clock) and posteromedial (7 o'clock) papillary muscles.","trap":"Mitral leaflets define the basal level; aortic cusps define the aortic valve level."},"XC02_F13":{"clue":"PSAX with small circular LV cavity and no papillary muscles or mitral valve.","mech":"Beyond the papillary insertions, the LV tapers to the apex with no intracavitary structures.","confirm":"Tilt toward the apex from the mid-papillary level to obtain this view.","trap":"Absence of structures is the key: papillary muscles mean mid level, a fish-mouth mitral valve means basal, aortic cusps mean aortic valve level."},"XC02_F14":{"clue":"PSAX with mitral leaflets in the LV: fish-mouth appearance.","mech":"At the basal level, the anterior and posterior mitral leaflets open and close in short axis, creating a fish-mouth shape.","confirm":"Tilt from the aortic valve level toward the apex until the mitral leaflets appear.","trap":"Papillary muscles define the mid level and aortic cusps the aortic valve level; the fish-mouth mitral orifice is unique to the basal level."},"XC02_F15":{"clue":"PSAX at the aortic valve level with RA, TV, RV and PV wrapping around the aorta.","mech":"The right heart wraps anteriorly around the aortic root, so the tricuspid inflow and pulmonic outflow appear in one image.","confirm":"Identify the aortic valve centrally with the tricuspid and pulmonic valves on either side.","trap":"The RV outflow view is a parasternal long-axis variant; A5C is apical."},"XC02_F16":{"clue":"Two PSAX images with apparent septal flattening; the right is from a normal heart.","mech":"Off-axis cuts through the LV distort its circular shape, mimicking septal flattening.","confirm":"Confirm a true short-axis plane with round LV and horizontal papillary muscles; check septum in multiple views.","trap":"Calling every flattening RV overload ignores the possibility of off-axis artifact."},"XC02_F17":{"clue":"Hybrid image with elongated LV while attempting A4C.","mech":"Placing the probe too close to the base (too medial or superior) cuts the LV obliquely between short and long axis.","confirm":"Slide laterally and inferiorly toward the true apex until the LV becomes elongated with tapered apex.","trap":"If attempting PSAX, the same hybrid indicates the probe is too close to the apex."},"XC02_F18":{"clue":"Probe at the point of maximal impulse, indicator toward the patient's left.","mech":"From the apex, the beam cuts through all four chambers along the long axis, giving the A4C.","confirm":"Confirm apex at the top, septum vertical, both AV valves in view.","trap":"Indicator to the right shoulder at the parasternal window gives PLAX; the subcostal view needs the probe below the xiphoid."},"XC02_F19":{"clue":"Standard A4C: which LV walls are displayed.","mech":"The A4C plane cuts through the inferoseptum medially and the anterolateral wall laterally, crossing the crux of the heart.","confirm":"Identify the septum on the left of the screen and the lateral wall on the right.","trap":"Anterior and inferior walls are in A2C; anteroseptal and inferolateral in apical long-axis."},"XC02_F20":{"clue":"A4C with heart leaning and RV near the top of the screen.","mech":"Medial probe placement aims the beam obliquely, so the RV appears at the apex of the image rather than the LV.","confirm":"Slide laterally toward the true LV apex until the septum is vertical.","trap":"Tilting the tail or rotating the indicator does not fix a footprint that sits too medially; the probe must slide laterally to the true apex."},"XC02_F21":{"clue":"A4C attempt aiming at inferior heart with foreshortened LA and coronary sinus visible.","mech":"Aiming posteriorly cuts through the coronary sinus and the inferior LA wall.","confirm":"Tilt the tail down to aim the beam anteriorly back to the standard A4C plane.","trap":"Tilting the tail up worsens the error; sliding medially changes the window."},"XC02_F22":{"clue":"Apical view with the LVOT and aortic root in the centre of an A4C-type image.","mech":"Tilting anteriorly from A4C brings the LVOT and aortic root into view as the fifth chamber.","confirm":"Use A5C to align Doppler with LVOT for VTI or aortic valve gradients.","trap":"Apical long-axis also shows the aorta but not the RA and RV."},"XC02_F23":{"clue":"A2C: which LV wall is on the right side of the screen.","mech":"Rotating counterclockwise from A4C removes the right heart and shows anterior and inferior walls.","confirm":"Identify the anterior wall on the right and the inferior wall on the left.","trap":"The inferoseptum appears in A4C and the inferolateral wall in the apical long-axis view; neither appears in A2C."},"XC02_F24":{"clue":"Apical view with LV, LA, aortic outflow and a sliver of RV.","mech":"Rotating further counterclockwise from A2C shows the LVOT and aorta in a long-axis plane from the apex.","confirm":"Identify the apex at the top with LVOT and aorta on the right of the screen.","trap":"The PLAX shows similar structures but from the parasternal window with apex off-screen."},"XC02_F26":{"clue":"Probe below the xiphoid, held flat with overhand grip, indicator to the patient's left.","mech":"Using the liver as an acoustic window and aiming toward the left shoulder, the beam cuts all four chambers in a coronal-like plane.","confirm":"Confirm liver in near field, RV closest to the probe, then RA, LV and LA; flatten the probe further if the heart is hidden.","trap":"The IVC long-axis view needs the indicator to the head; apical and parasternal views use chest wall windows."},"XC02_F27":{"clue":"Liver in the near field with the RV closest to the transducer.","mech":"The subcostal window images through the liver, so it lies above the heart on screen and the RV is the first chamber encountered.","confirm":"Identify liver, then RV free wall, RA, LV and LA; this window is often the best during ventilation or CPR.","trap":"Parasternal and apical windows do not show liver in the near field."},"XC02_F28":{"clue":"M-mode cursor placed through the subcostal IVC.","mech":"M-mode tracks IVC diameter over time with high temporal resolution, documenting respiratory changes.","confirm":"Measure maximal and minimal diameters: collapsibility = (max - min)/max; distensibility in ventilated patients = (max - min)/min.","trap":"Hepatic vein flow needs PW Doppler; aortic pulsatility would show arterial pattern."},"XC02_F29":{"clue":"From subcostal 4-chamber, how to reach the IVC long-axis view.","mech":"Rotating the indicator to the head and tilting the tail up aims the beam into a sagittal plane through the IVC entering the RA.","confirm":"Tilt the tail up and rotate counterclockwise until the indicator points cephalad; keep the probe nearly vertical but aimed at the heart.","trap":"Sliding to the midaxillary line gives the RUQ window; rotating clockwise to the feet reverses orientation."},"XC02_F30":{"clue":"Long-axis IVC shown entering the RA.","mech":"Seeing the IVC drain into the RA, often with a hepatic vein joining, proves it is the IVC and standardises measurement near the cavoatrial junction.","confirm":"Measure 1 to 2 cm from the RA junction, perpendicular to the vessel long axis.","trap":"The aorta can be mistaken for the IVC if the RA connection is not seen."},"XC02_F31":{"clue":"Thick-walled tubular vessel that does not drain into the RA.","mech":"The aorta has thick muscular walls and pulsatile flow, and it passes behind the heart rather than entering the RA.","confirm":"Track the vessel to its RA connection; use colour or PW Doppler to show arterial pulsatile flow; the IVC lies right of midline.","trap":"Mistaking the aorta for the IVC gives a non-collapsible vessel and false conclusions about volume status."},"XC03_F01":{"clue":"TEE image of left atrial appendage thrombus in AF with embolic stroke.","mech":"The LAA lies posteriorly near the esophagus; TEE avoids chest wall and lung attenuation, giving high-resolution images of posterior structures.","confirm":"Image the LAA at multiple angles (0 to 135 degrees); measure emptying velocity and look for spontaneous echo contrast.","trap":"TEE is more invasive than TTE, and not chosen for RV free wall; bronchial air limits TEE views of the distal ascending aorta."},"XC03_F05":{"clue":"Jaw thrust shown during TEE probe insertion.","mech":"Lifting the mandible and tongue forward opens the oropharynx, helping the probe pass into the esophagus.","confirm":"Combine jaw thrust with gentle midline insertion and a bite block; do not force against resistance.","trap":"Bite blocks protect the probe; jaw thrust is not for ETT confirmation or depth measurement."},"XC03_F06":{"clue":"Deep transgastric view at 0 degrees with full anteflexion.","mech":"Looking up from the LV apex aligns the beam parallel with the LVOT and aortic valve, minimising Doppler angle error.","confirm":"Place PW in LVOT for VTI and stroke volume; CW across the aortic valve for gradients.","trap":"The LAA is best seen mid-esophageal, the descending aorta by rotating the probe posteriorly, and the IVC from a bicaval or transgastric view, not this one."},"XC03_F07":{"clue":"TEE image while withdrawing the probe showing the transverse aorta in the near field.","mech":"High in the esophagus, the probe lies posterior to the arch, showing it in long axis at about 0 degrees.","confirm":"Rotate to 90 degrees for short axis; trace the left subclavian origin.","trap":"The descending aorta view is mid-esophageal; the ascending aorta long-axis view is a different plane."},"XC04_F01":{"clue":"Bright horizontal line just deep to rib shadows in healthy anterior chest.","mech":"The pleural line arises from the high acoustic impedance mismatch at the pleural surface between tissue and aerated lung.","confirm":"Confirm the bat sign (two ribs with pleural line between) and look for lung sliding.","trap":"A lines are fainter repeated reverberations deeper than the pleural line; B lines are vertical."},"XC04_F02":{"clue":"Eight-zone chest diagram; supine patient with suspected pneumothorax.","mech":"Air rises to the least dependent anterior zones in a supine patient.","confirm":"Scan zones 1 and 5 first with a high-frequency probe for sliding and lung point.","trap":"Zones 4 and 8 are dependent and checked for effusion; starting there in a supine patient misses small anterior pneumothoraces."},"XC04_F03":{"clue":"Repetitive horizontal hyperechoic lines (A lines).","mech":"A lines are reverberations between the pleural line and probe; they need air at the pleura, which exists in both normal lung and pneumothorax.","confirm":"Assess lung sliding, B lines and lung point to differentiate.","trap":"A lines do not exclude pneumothorax; they indicate air, not interstitial syndrome."},"XC04_F05":{"clue":"Absent sliding in zone 1, with a lateral boundary where sliding is present on one side and absent on the other.","mech":"The lung point marks where the visceral pleura intermittently contacts the parietal pleura at the pneumothorax edge.","confirm":"Confirm the lung point and locate its position to estimate size.","trap":"Mainstem intubation and atelectasis abolish sliding but do not create a lung point; bullae can mimic it."},"XC04_F06":{"clue":"M-mode lung pulse: rhythmic seashore segments synchronous with heartbeat.","mech":"Lung pulse requires visceral pleura in contact with parietal pleura to transmit cardiac motion.","confirm":"Look for lung pulse when sliding is equivocal; it excludes pneumothorax at that point.","trap":"Barcode sign suggests absent sliding but not specific; lung pulse is not effusion or interstitial syndrome."},"XC04_F07":{"clue":"Vertebral bodies visible above the diaphragm in a dependent scan.","mech":"Air blocks transmission; fluid or consolidation transmits sound, revealing the spine.","confirm":"Correlate with fluid or tissue density; spine sign supports effusion or consolidation.","trap":"Normal aerated lung hides the spine; pneumothorax and emphysema do not show the spine sign."},"XC04_F08":{"clue":"Dependent pleural collection with hyperechoic septations in a febrile patient.","mech":"Fibrin and inflammatory proteins in exudates form strands and septations; transudates lack the protein needed to build them.","confirm":"Sample the fluid for pH, LDH, protein and glucose; consider chest tube or fibrinolytics.","trap":"Not all exudates are septated, and anechoic does not prove transudate; but septations argue strongly against a simple transudate."},"XC04_F09":{"clue":"Effusion panel A with no lung tissue across the probe footprint.","mech":"A large effusion displaces lung away from the chest wall, leaving a fluid-only path.","confirm":"Choose the site with no lung within the footprint; measure skin-to-fluid depth.","trap":"Small effusions with lung close by are harder to drain safely."},"XC04_F10":{"clue":"Panels show progressive B lines replacing A lines.","mech":"B lines arise from thickened interlobular septa or fluid, which create reverberation and erase A lines.","confirm":"Count B lines per intercostal space; three or more makes the region positive.","trap":"B lines are not pneumothorax or effusion signs; consolidation shows tissue pattern."},"XC04_F11":{"clue":"Tissue-like mixed echogenicity pattern deep to rib shadows without A or B lines.","mech":"Fluid- or cell-filled alveoli remove the air interface, letting sound penetrate and producing a solid, liver-like tissue texture called hepatization.","confirm":"Look for air bronchograms, shred sign and colour Doppler flow.","trap":"Effusion is anechoic with a spine sign but no tissue texture; pneumothorax and normal lung keep A lines and an air interface."},"XC04_F12":{"clue":"Hyperechoic branching foci shimmer with respiration within consolidation.","mech":"Dynamic air bronchograms reflect air moving within patent bronchi, favouring pneumonia.","confirm":"Confirm movement on real-time imaging; static bronchograms can be atelectasis.","trap":"Static air bronchograms occur in both pneumonia and atelectasis; obstructive atelectasis typically shows absent or static bronchograms, not dynamic ones."},"XC04_F13":{"clue":"Small irregular hypoechoic area against the pleura with a B line.","mech":"Subpleural consolidation is a small airless area adjacent to the pleura, often from pneumonia or infarction.","confirm":"Look for colour Doppler flow and correlate clinically.","trap":"Rib shadows and lung point are different signs; effusion is anechoic and not irregular."},"XC05_F01":{"clue":"Curvilinear probe in right midaxillary line, marker cephalad.","mech":"This coronal plane aligns liver and right kidney, displaying the hepatorenal recess, the most dependent upper abdominal space.","confirm":"Sweep from above the diaphragm to the inferior liver tip, checking the pleural space and paracolic gutter.","trap":"The splenorenal recess is on the left; the subxiphoid view is cardiac."},"XC05_F02":{"clue":"Space between liver and right kidney in a supine patient.","mech":"In supine position, Morison pouch is the most dependent upper abdominal space where free fluid collects first.","confirm":"Scan the hepatorenal interface and extend to the paracolic gutter.","trap":"Morison pouch is not used for the aorta, pneumoperitoneum or retroperitoneal vessels; its value is dependency in the supine patient."},"XC05_F03":{"clue":"RUQ FAST scan extended to the inferior liver tip.","mech":"Fluid can track down the right paracolic gutter at the liver tip and be missed if only Morison pouch is scanned.","confirm":"Fan through the hepatorenal space and slide caudally to the inferior liver tip.","trap":"Measuring liver span or seeing the gallbladder are not the purpose."},"XC05_F04":{"clue":"Anechoic fluid between spleen and kidney after left-sided blunt trauma in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"Splenic injury bleeds into the splenorenal recess, the dependent LUQ space.","confirm":"Scan above the diaphragm and to the inferior splenic tip; correlate with haemodynamics.","trap":"Hydronephrosis and renal cyst are within the kidney; pleural effusion lies above the diaphragm."},"XC05_F05":{"clue":"Uterus visible behind the bladder in transverse view.","mech":"Seeing the uterus or prostate confirms the beam is directed into the deep pelvis, where the most dependent pouch lies.","confirm":"Scan the rectouterine (Douglas) or rectovesical pouch for anechoic fluid.","trap":"An overdistended bladder or cephalad probe would not reliably show the pelvic recess."},"XC05_F07":{"clue":"Curvilinear probe in RLQ, marker cephalad, fanning, in a cirrhotic patient.","mech":"Fanning finds the largest fluid pocket free of bowel and vessels, which is the safest paracentesis site.","confirm":"Mark the site with the deepest pocket, check for epigastric vessels with colour Doppler.","trap":"This sweep is not for appendix, kidney or iliac vessels; it maps the deepest pocket away from bowel and vessels before puncture."},"XC05_F09":{"clue":"Colour Doppler shows engorged abdominal wall vessels at the planned paracentesis site.","mech":"Portal hypertension creates collateral vessels; puncturing them risks major bleeding in coagulopathic patients.","confirm":"Choose another track that avoids vessels; recheck with colour Doppler.","trap":"Correcting coagulopathy does not make puncturing a visible varix or epigastric vessel safe; abandoning paracentesis is unnecessary when another track exists."},"XC05_F10":{"clue":"Anechoic stripe in Morison pouch on RUQ FAST.","mech":"Blood, ascites, urine and bile look similar acoustically, so ultrasound cannot determine composition.","confirm":"Correlate clinically; diagnostic peritoneal aspiration or CT may determine fluid type.","trap":"Detection threshold is about 100 to 250 mL, not 500 mL; anechoic does not mean blood."},"XC05_F14":{"clue":"Incidental round anechoic lesion in the left renal cortex during LUQ FAST.","mech":"Simple cysts are anechoic with posterior enhancement, but cystic renal carcinoma can mimic them.","confirm":"Confirm with consultative ultrasound or CT; use colour Doppler to exclude a vessel.","trap":"An anechoic cortical lesion usually is a simple cyst, but POCUS cannot exclude cystic carcinoma; aspiration or nephrectomy are premature."},"XC05_F15":{"clue":"Panel C shows bear-claw calyceal dilatation: moderate hydronephrosis.","mech":"Obstruction distends major and most minor calyces before sinus and cortex thin.","confirm":"Grade: mild prominent collecting ducts, moderate bear-claw, severe thin cortex.","trap":"Mild shows only prominent collecting ducts; severe shows marked sinus dilatation with a thin cortex. Bear-claw calyces without cortical thinning are moderate."},"XC05_F17":{"clue":"PW Doppler of a renal transplant hilar artery.","mech":"Rising parenchymal or downstream resistance cuts diastolic flow more than systolic flow, increasing the resistive index.","confirm":"RI = (PSV - EDV)/PSV; above 0.80 suggests allograft dysfunction.","trap":"PI, VTI and acceleration time are different indices; RI is the standard."},"XC05_F20":{"clue":"Anechoic collection behind the bladder in a male trauma patient.","mech":"The rectovesical pouch is the most dependent pelvic space in males.","confirm":"Image in transverse and sagittal planes; fluid here indicates free pelvic fluid.","trap":"Fluid within the bladder is urine; anterior wall or retroperitoneal fluid are different spaces."},"XC05_F21":{"clue":"Sagittal pelvic view in FAST.","mech":"Fluid above the bladder dome within peritoneum may only appear in the sagittal plane.","confirm":"Scan both transverse and sagittal planes for complete assessment.","trap":"Transverse view mainly looks into the pelvis; sagittal is needed for dome fluid."},"XC05_F22":{"clue":"Distended bladder with Foley balloon inside and falling urine output.","mech":"A blocked or kinked catheter prevents drainage, causing retention, not true oliguria.","confirm":"Troubleshoot or replace the Foley; recheck bladder volume.","trap":"Furosemide or fluids treat presumed renal oliguria; a full bladder around the balloon means mechanical obstruction, so diuretics or fluids only worsen retention."},"XC05_F23":{"clue":"Bladder measured in transverse and sagittal planes.","mech":"The bladder is approximated as an ellipsoid, so the product of three orthogonal diameters is multiplied by a shape correction coefficient.","confirm":"Volume = L x W x H x 0.77 (source value); most POCUS protocols use about 0.7 to 0.72.","trap":"0.52 assumes a sphere and underestimates; two-dimension formulas ignore height. Coefficients differ between sources (0.7 to 0.77)."},"XC05_F25":{"clue":"Celiac trunk branching into hepatic and splenic arteries on transverse epigastric view.","mech":"The celiac trunk with its branches resembles a seagull, marking the proximal abdominal aorta.","confirm":"Identify the aorta anterior to the vertebral body and IVC to its right (screen left).","trap":"Mickey Mouse is the portal triad; nutcracker refers to left renal vein compression."},"XC05_F29":{"clue":"Bowel gas causing dirty shadowing hides the aorta.","mech":"Air-fluid interfaces create impedance mismatch and scattering; gentle pressure displaces gas.","confirm":"Jiggle the probe with gentle pressure; reposition or change angle.","trap":"Linear probes lack penetration; reducing depth or gain does not remove gas."},"XC05_F30":{"clue":"Transverse aorta measured 1.66 cm outer wall to outer wall.","mech":"Outer wall measurement includes wall and thrombus, giving true diameter; AAA is over 3 cm.","confirm":"Measure transverse outer-to-outer wall; 1.66 cm is normal.","trap":"Inner-to-inner wall measurement underestimates, not overestimates, true diameter; longitudinal-only measurement risks off-axis cuts that miss the maximum diameter."},"XC05_F31":{"clue":"Dilated aorta measured including mural thrombus around lumen.","mech":"Mural thrombus lies within the aneurysm wall, so excluding it underestimates diameter.","confirm":"Measure outer wall to outer wall in transverse plane.","trap":"Measuring the lumen only, because thrombus seems not to be wall, underestimates aneurysm size and rupture risk."},"XC05_F33":{"clue":"Saccular aneurysm B with eccentric outpouching.","mech":"Saccular aneurysms arise from one wall and may lie outside a transverse plane.","confirm":"Scan in both transverse and longitudinal planes.","trap":"Fusiform aneurysms are most common; saccular are not best seen transversely."},"XC05_F34":{"clue":"Linear flap in a dilated abdominal aorta below the SMA in a hypotensive patient.","mech":"An intimal flap indicates dissection, which may extend from the thoracic aorta.","confirm":"Scan thoracic aorta via PLAX and suprasternal views; TEE if unstable, CT if stable.","trap":"Calling it isolated abdominal dissection and treating medically risks missing type A dissection, which needs emergency surgery."},"XC05_F36":{"clue":"Gallbladder wall measured on the anterior wall.","mech":"Posterior acoustic enhancement deep to bile makes the posterior wall appear falsely thick.","confirm":"Measure anterior wall from outer to inner surface; over 3 mm suggests thickening.","trap":"The posterior wall is visible but enhancement artifact inflates its apparent thickness; stones sit dependently, not on the anterior wall."}};
